window.BOOK_SEARCH = [{"title": "Quantum Programming Languages — Exercise 1", "chapter": "Quantum Programming Languages", "url": "01-quantum-programming-languages.html#exercise-1", "text": "How many instructions does this circuit contain, what is its depth, and what state does it prepare? When does the Python loop run? The loop runs at construction time, in Python, and appends three separate H instructions: count_ops reports {'h': 3} and the depth is \\(3\\). Since \\(H^2=I\\), the state is \\(H\\ket0=\\ket+\\). The quantum device never sees a loop; it receives three gates, which a compiler may simplify to one."}, {"title": "Quantum Programming Languages — Exercise 2", "chapter": "Quantum Programming Languages", "url": "01-quantum-programming-languages.html#exercise-2", "text": "Classify Q#, OpenQASM, and Qiskit as a standalone quantum programming language, a circuit-level description language, or a framework embedded in a host language. Which of them is a Python library? Q# is a standalone quantum programming language with its own syntax and compiler. OpenQASM is a circuit-level language for describing operations, measurements, and classical control, often used as an exchange format between tools. Qiskit is a Python SDK: a library that uses Python to construct circuit objects, which it can then compile, simulate, export, or submit. Its programs are Python programs that build quantum instructions."}, {"title": "Quantum Programming Languages — Exercise 3", "chapter": "Quantum Programming Languages", "url": "01-quantum-programming-languages.html#exercise-3", "text": "What happens when you ask for the inverse of a circuit that ends in a measurement, and why? Qiskit raises a CircuitError, “inverse() not implemented for measure.” A measurement maps different input states to the same output record and has no unitary inverse, so no automatic adjoint exists. Only purely unitary routines can be inverted gate by gate; a routine containing measurement or reset needs a different treatment, such as removing the measurement before inverting."}, {"title": "Quantum Programming Languages — Exercise 4", "chapter": "Quantum Programming Languages", "url": "01-quantum-programming-languages.html#exercise-4", "text": "Predict the statevector produced by each circuit before running it: (a) H on qubit 1 followed by cx(1, 0); (b) H on qubit 1 followed by cx(0, 1). Qiskit lists amplitudes in the order \\(00,01,10,11\\) of the labels \\(q_1q_0\\). Which circuit makes a Bell state? (a) H puts qubit 1 in \\(\\ket+\\); cx(1, 0) flips qubit 0 when qubit 1 is \\(1\\), giving \\((\\ket{00}+\\ket{11})/\\sqrt2\\), the same Bell state as the chapter’s circuit, because that state is symmetric under exchanging the qubits. (b) The control, qubit 0, is \\(\\ket0\\), so the CNOT does nothing: the state is \\(\\ket+\\) on qubit 1 and \\(\\ket0\\) on qubit 0, with amplitudes \\((0.707,0,0.707,0)\\), a product state. The roles of control and target, not just which gates appear, determine the result."}, {"title": "Quantum Programming Languages — Exercise 5", "chapter": "Quantum Programming Languages", "url": "01-quantum-programming-languages.html#exercise-5", "text": "Build \\(U\\), which applies H and then S to one qubit. (a) Predict the instructions of U.inverse(). (b) Check that composing U with its inverse gives the identity operator. (c) Why must the inverse reverse the order as well as invert each gate? (a) The inverse lists sdg, then h. (b) The composition is the identity operator, which Operator equivalence confirms. (c) \\(U=SH\\) as a matrix (H acts first), so \\(U^{-1}=H^{-1}S^{-1}=HS^\\dagger\\): the last gate applied must be undone first, exactly as when unwinding nested function calls. Inverting each gate without reversing the order would give \\(S^\\dagger H\\), which is not the inverse."}, {"title": "Quantum Programming Languages — Exercise 6", "chapter": "Quantum Programming Languages", "url": "01-quantum-programming-languages.html#exercise-6", "text": "A student wants to apply X to qubit 1 only if a measurement of qubit 0 gives 1, and writes a Python if statement that inspects a variable holding the measurement result. Explain why this cannot work at construction time, and write the version that records the condition as an instruction executed during the run. A Python if runs while the circuit is being built, before any shot executes, so no measured value exists to inspect. The condition must become part of the circuit: an instruction that refers to the classical bit and is evaluated by the controller during each run. In Qiskit, the if_test context adds an if_else instruction whose body applies X when the bit is 1."}, {"title": "Quantum Programming Languages — Exercise 7", "chapter": "Quantum Programming Languages", "url": "01-quantum-programming-languages.html#exercise-7", "text": "Export the Bell circuit to OpenQASM 3 with qiskit.qasm3.dumps. Identify which output line declares the qubits and which lines are the gates. What information would an exporter need in order to represent the circuit on a specific device, beyond this text? The output contains a header (OPENQASM 3.0 and an include of the standard gates), a declaration qubit[2] q, and the gates h q[0] and cx q[0], q[1]. This describes the logical circuit. A device implementation also needs the native gate decomposition, the mapping of logical to physical qubits, any routing, and timing or pulse details. Exporting preserves meaning, not a device-specific realization."}, {"title": "Quantum Programming Languages — Exercise 8", "chapter": "Quantum Programming Languages", "url": "01-quantum-programming-languages.html#exercise-8", "text": "A student reasons: “If r = q refers to the same qubit, then cx(q, r) uses one qubit as control and its copy as target.” What does Qiskit do with this code, and what physical principle does the check reflect? Qiskit raises a CircuitError, “duplicate bit arguments.” Assigning r = q creates a second name for the same qubit, not a copy of its state; no-cloning forbids making an independent copy of an unknown state. A CNOT needs two different qubits, and using one wire as both control and target is meaningless. Qiskit catches this when the gate is appended; a language with linear types could reject it before the program runs."}, {"title": "Quantum Programming Languages — Exercise 9", "chapter": "Quantum Programming Languages", "url": "01-quantum-programming-languages.html#exercise-9", "text": "A linear type system requires every qubit handle to be used exactly once and forbids duplicating it. (a) Which of these would it reject at compile time: a function that returns the same qubit twice; cx(q, q); a function that silently drops a qubit that is entangled with others? (b) What does Qiskit check instead, and when? (c) Give one benefit and one cost of static enforcement. (a) A linear type system rejects all three: returning a qubit twice and cx(q, q) both duplicate a handle, and dropping an entangled qubit discards a resource without an explicit operation (an affine system would allow discarding but not duplication). (b) Qiskit is a Python library, so it checks at run time of the construction code: duplicate arguments raise an error when the gate is appended, while silently ignoring a qubit is allowed. (c) Static checks catch mistakes before any execution and document resource use; the cost is a stricter language in which some valid programs, such as those that measure and discard deliberately, need extra annotations."}, {"title": "Quantum Programming Languages — Exercise 10", "chapter": "Quantum Programming Languages", "url": "01-quantum-programming-languages.html#exercise-10", "text": "A one-qubit circuit applies X with global_phase \\(\\pi/2\\), so it implements \\(iX\\). (a) Uncontrolled, how does it differ from X? (b) Form its controlled version with control(1) and inspect the matrix. What happened to the phase? (c) Why must a programming framework track global phase for this reason? (a) Uncontrolled, \\(iX\\) and \\(X\\) differ by a global phase and are physically indistinguishable. (b) The controlled matrix maps the control-1 states by \\(iX\\) and leaves the control-0 states alone: the factor \\(i\\) now distinguishes the two branches, so the controlled version is CNOT followed by S on the control. (c) A routine that is correct “up to global phase” can become wrong when controlled. Qiskit stores global_phase on the circuit and includes it in control(), so routines used inside controlled operations must have their phase specified, not discarded."}, {"title": "Language Families", "chapter": "Quantum Programming Languages", "url": "01-quantum-programming-languages.html#section-1-1", "text": "1.1 Language Families Suppose we want a computer to prepare the Bell state \\((\\ket{00}+\\ket{11})/\\sqrt2\\). We know the circuit. How many genuinely different ways are there to write it down for a machine? We first compare two ways of organizing the instructions, and then ask who reads the resulting text. 1.1.1 Imperative and functional approaches The most direct attempt copies the circuit drawing from left to right. We name the qubits and list the actions in time order: qubits q0, q1, both starting in |0⟩\napply H to q0\napply CNOT with control q0 and target q1 This is an imperative description: a sequence of commands that change named things, one after another. We could instead describe the same computation as one transformation built from smaller ones. The two-qubit operation is \\(\\mathrm{CNOT}\\,(H\\otimes I)\\), and the program is that product applied to \\(\\ket{00}\\). This is a functional description: it says what the result is, as a composition of transformations, rather than listing changes in order. Does the choice of style change the state we get? It cannot, because both describe the same matrix product. Let us check it. \\(H\\) on the first qubit gives \\((\\ket{00}+\\ket{10})/\\sqrt2\\), where the first label is q0. CNOT then flips q1 exactly when q0 is 1, giving \\((\\ket{00}+\\ket{11})/\\sqrt2\\). So the styles differ only in what they make easy to see. The imperative list looks like the drawing. The functional product makes composition visible. For example, the inverse of a product of unitaries is the product of their adjoints in reverse order, \\((H\\otimes I)\\,\\mathrm{CNOT}\\), and a language that stores the product can build that inverse for us. Bell-pair preparation. A Hadamard followed by CNOT makes (|00⟩+|11⟩)/√2. The first wire is the control. 1.1.2 Standalone languages and embedded frameworks Whichever style we choose, some program must read the text. Who? One answer is to invent a new language with its own grammar and its own compiler, the program that translates the text into instructions for a machine. That is a standalone language . Here is the Bell preparation, followed by measurement, in OpenQASM 3, a standalone language for circuits: OPENQASM 3.0;\ninclude \"stdgates.inc\";\nqubit[2] q;\nbit[2] c;\nh q[0];\ncx q[0], q[1];\nc = measure q; Each line declares two qubits, declares two classical bits, applies a gate, or measures. Q# is another standalone language, built around operations that can be composed and inverted. A second answer reuses an existing language, such as Python, and adds a library of quantum objects to it. The existing language is then called the host , and the result is an embedded framework . Qiskit is such a framework: it is a Python software development kit (SDK), not a separate language. We gain everything the host offers, such as loops, functions and files, without writing a new compiler. What do we pay for that? The host runs on our ordinary computer while it builds the circuit. Suppose we write, in Python, “repeat three times: append H to qubit 0.” The loop runs once, on our laptop, and leaves behind a circuit with three separate H instructions. The quantum device never sees a loop. So in an embedded framework we must keep two moments apart: construction time , when the host builds the circuit, and execution time , when the circuit runs. Problem 1.1. Why distinguish construction time from execution time? Worked solution The host may expand a loop into a fixed gate sequence before any quantum run. A measurement-dependent loop requires controller support and cannot be resolved during ordinary circuit construction."}, {"title": "Design Principles", "chapter": "Quantum Programming Languages", "url": "01-quantum-programming-languages.html#section-1-2", "text": "1.2 Design Principles So far the styles differ only on the surface. But a classical language also lets us copy a variable, reuse it freely, undo any step we like, and branch on any value. Which of these freedoms survive when the values are qubits? We test copying first, then undoing and branching, and each failure becomes a rule a quantum language should enforce. 1.2.1 Types, ownership, and no-cloning In a classical program, writing y = x gives us a copy of the value in x . What should q2 = q1 mean when q1 is a qubit? Suppose it meant “copy the state.” Then some unitary \\(U\\) would send \\(\\ket\\psi\\ket0\\) to \\(\\ket\\psi\\ket\\psi\\) for every \\(\\psi\\). On the basis states this forces \\(U\\ket0\\ket0=\\ket{00}\\) and \\(U\\ket1\\ket0=\\ket{11}\\). Since \\(U\\) is linear, \\(U\\ket+\\ket0\\) must then be \\((\\ket{00}+\\ket{11})/\\sqrt2\\). A copy of \\(\\ket+\\) would be \\(\\ket+\\ket+=(\\ket{00}+\\ket{01}+\\ket{10}+\\ket{11})/2\\), which is a different state. So no such \\(U\\) exists. This is the no-cloning theorem. So q2 can only be a second name for the same qubit. A name that refers to a qubit without containing its state is called a handle . Assigning a handle copies the reference, never the quantum state. Handles bring a new hazard. Suppose we write cx(q, q) , a CNOT whose control and target are the same qubit. A CNOT acts on two distinct systems, so this instruction has no meaning. A language can reject it with a type rule , a rule about how values may be used that is checked before the program runs. A linear rule requires each qubit value to be used exactly once. An affine rule requires it to be used at most once, so a qubit may also be discarded. The quantum lambda calculus of Selinger and Valiron is built on a linear rule. Qiskit enforces neither rule. It refuses a gate whose two arguments name the same qubit, but only when we try to append it. No-cloning still holds physically whether or not the language checks for it. 1.2.2 Reversibility, measurement, and classical control Next, undoing. A routine built from unitaries \\(U_1,\\dots,U_k\\) has the inverse \\(U_1^\\dagger\\cdots U_k^\\dagger\\), so a language can generate it automatically, as in our functional description. Does this work for every routine? Take a reset , which sets a qubit to \\(\\ket0\\). It sends \\(\\ket0\\) to \\(\\ket0\\) and also \\(\\ket1\\) to \\(\\ket0\\). After it, nothing tells us which input we had, so no operation can undo it. A measurement whose result we throw away loses information in the same way. So only the unitary parts of a program have automatic inverses. Now branching. A measurement produces a classical bit, and we often want a later gate to depend on it. Could we write an ordinary host-language if : “if qubit 0 is 1, apply X to qubit 1”? At construction time no measurement has happened yet. The host is only building a description, so there is no bit to test. The condition must therefore be an instruction inside the circuit that names a recorded bit. The controller evaluates it during execution, when the bit exists. This is called classical control . Problem 1.2. Can every quantum function have an automatic inverse? Worked solution Only operations with a specified reversible action can be inverted this way. Measurement with a discarded outcome and reset lose information in the circuit-level description."}, {"title": "Abstraction and Execution", "chapter": "Quantum Programming Languages", "url": "01-quantum-programming-languages.html#section-1-3", "text": "1.3 Abstraction and Execution We now have a description that a language can check. Two questions remain before we can run it. Real devices do not all offer the same gates, so how can one description serve many machines? And what does the description look like in Qiskit? Answering the first separates meaning from realization, and the second gives us our first program. 1.3.1 Circuit languages and intermediate representations Our Bell description uses H and CNOT. Suppose a device can apply only a few kinds of rotation and one particular two-qubit gate. The operations a device implements directly are its native gates . Suppose also that each qubit can interact only with certain neighbours. Who turns our description into something this device can run? A compiler does, and to rewrite a circuit safely it must know which operations act on which qubits and which must happen before which. A form of the program that records exactly this is called an intermediate representation . The compiler can replace each H by native rotations that agree with it up to a global phase. If two qubits that need a CNOT are not neighbours, it can insert swaps that move their states next to each other. That step is called routing . After such rewriting the gate list looks different, yet the program means the same computation. So a language should keep two things apart: the circuit’s mathematical meaning, and its device-specific realization. Standalone circuit languages such as OpenQASM also serve as an exchange format between tools. Writing a circuit out in that format does not make every destination able to run it, since a device may lack, for example, classical control during execution. 1.3.2 Qiskit as a Python SDK Qiskit has no grammar of its own. So what does our Bell preparation look like when Python is the host? We read the program below a few lines at a time. It uses only a handful of Python features, and each one is there because the program needs it. Python starts with a small core. The from … import … lines borrow two definitions from the installed Qiskit package: QuantumCircuit , which describes circuits, and Statevector , which computes amplitudes. Lines beginning with # are comments for human readers. We want a recipe we can reuse, so we package it as a function . The line def bell(): starts a function named bell . The indented lines below it form its body. Inside, circuit = QuantumCircuit(2) creates an empty circuit on qubits 0 and 1, and the equals sign gives it the name circuit . The dot in circuit.h(0) calls a method , an operation that belongs to that object. Here it appends H on qubit 0, and circuit.cx(0, 1) appends a CNOT with control 0 and target 1. Finally return circuit hands the finished circuit back. The line circuit = bell() runs the recipe. Notice what it produces: a description, not a quantum run. What should that description contain? Before running anything we predict the amplitudes of \\(\\ket{00},\\ket{01},\\ket{10},\\ket{11}\\): \\((1/\\sqrt2,0,0,1/\\sqrt2)\\). In Python, 2**-0.5 is \\(2^{-1/2}=1/\\sqrt2\\). Statevector.from_instruction(circuit) computes the amplitudes of the circuit’s output by an ordinary classical calculation. The method equiv compares that state with our prediction, ignoring any global phase. The word assert turns the comparison into a check. If the claim is true, nothing happens; if it is false, Python stops with an error. So a silent run means the prediction held. The last line prints a text drawing of the circuit. \"\"\"Runnable companion for Quantum Programming, Chapter 1.\"\"\"\nfrom qiskit import QuantumCircuit\nfrom qiskit.quantum_info import Statevector\n\n# A Python function builds a circuit; it does not execute a quantum device.\ndef bell():\n    circuit = QuantumCircuit(2)\n    circuit.h(0)\n    circuit.cx(0, 1)\n    return circuit\n\ncircuit = bell()\nassert Statevector.from_instruction(circuit).equiv(\n    Statevector([2**-0.5, 0, 0, 2**-0.5]))\nprint(circuit.draw(output=\"text\")) Companion script: chapter_01.py . The program shows the two roles we separated. The function builds a description, and Statevector evaluates it on a classical computer. Neither step touches a quantum device. Problem 1.3. Does assigning other = circuit create an independent circuit? Worked solution No. Both Python names refer to the same mutable object. Use circuit.copy() when subsequent edits must be independent. Neither action copies a physical quantum state. Our program passed its check on this computer. Would it give the same result on someone else’s, with whatever versions of Python and Qiskit they happen to have installed? Sources and further study: OpenQASM language specification ; Selinger and Valiron, quantum lambda calculus ."}, {"title": "The Qiskit Environment — Exercise 1", "chapter": "The Qiskit Environment", "url": "02-the-qiskit-environment.html#exercise-1", "text": "For \\(1024\\) fair-coin shots, find the expected count of 1 and the standard deviations of the count and of the frequency. Would \\(540\\) ones be surprising? Expected count \\(512\\); count standard deviation \\(\\sqrt{1024/4}=16\\); frequency standard deviation \\(1/(2\\sqrt{1024})=1/64\\approx0.016\\). A count of \\(540\\) is \\(28/16\\approx1.75\\) standard deviations high: unusual but not alarming. Tests should use tolerances of several standard deviations, chosen in advance."}, {"title": "The Qiskit Environment — Exercise 2", "chapter": "The Qiskit Environment", "url": "02-the-qiskit-environment.html#exercise-2", "text": "A circuit is built with its own classical register: QuantumCircuit(1, 1), H, then measure(0, 0). The sampler result has no field named meas. Under which name are the counts stored, and why? The counts are under data.c. QuantumCircuit(1, 1) creates a default classical register named c, and the sampler’s result fields are named after the circuit’s classical registers. measure_all adds a new register named meas, which is why the chapter’s example used data.meas. Code that hard-codes the name breaks when the circuit is built differently."}, {"title": "The Qiskit Environment — Exercise 3", "chapter": "The Qiskit Environment", "url": "02-the-qiskit-environment.html#exercise-3", "text": "Which package is needed for each task: (a) sampling an ideal circuit locally with StatevectorSampler; (b) simulating with a noise model; (c) submitting a job to an IBM device? Does importing the remote client submit anything? (a) The Qiskit SDK. (b) Qiskit Aer, the separate simulation package with noise models. (c) IBM Runtime. Importing the Runtime client only loads code; submitting requires credentials, a chosen backend, and an explicit run call, which is why the course keeps such calls behind explicit options."}, {"title": "The Qiskit Environment — Exercise 4", "chapter": "The Qiskit Environment", "url": "02-the-qiskit-environment.html#exercise-4", "text": "Prepare a qubit with ry(\\(\\theta\\)) so that it reads 1 with probability \\(0.75\\). (a) Find \\(\\theta\\) using \\(P(1)=\\sin^2(\\theta/2)\\). (b) For \\(4096\\) shots, find the expected count and a five-standard-deviation tolerance on the frequency. (c) Write a test that checks the sampled frequency against this tolerance. (a) \\(\\theta/2=\\pi/3\\), so \\(\\theta=2\\pi/3\\). (b) Expected count \\(3072\\); the frequency standard deviation is \\(\\sqrt{0.1875/4096}\\approx0.0068\\), so a five-sigma tolerance is about \\(0.034\\). (c) The test below asserts that the observed frequency lies within that tolerance of \\(0.75\\); with a fixed seed it is also a reproducible regression test."}, {"title": "The Qiskit Environment — Exercise 5", "chapter": "The Qiskit Environment", "url": "02-the-qiskit-environment.html#exercise-5", "text": "The chapter’s test asserts \\(|f-0.5|<0.05\\) for \\(4096\\) fair-coin shots. (a) How many standard deviations is \\(0.05\\)? Estimate how often a correct circuit fails the test. (b) A buggy circuit has \\(P(1)=0.45\\). How often does it pass? (c) What does this say about choosing shots and tolerance together? (a) \\(0.05/0.0078\\approx6.4\\) standard deviations; a correct circuit fails with probability around \\(10^{-10}\\). (b) For \\(p=0.45\\), the observed frequency is centred on \\(0.45\\), exactly at the boundary, so the bug passes about half the time. (c) A wide tolerance avoids false alarms but misses moderate bugs. Choosing the smallest error that must be detected, and then the shots needed to resolve it at the desired confidence, gives a test that is both reliable and sensitive; checking exact probabilities with Statevector avoids sampling altogether when possible."}, {"title": "The Qiskit Environment — Exercise 6", "chapter": "The Qiskit Environment", "url": "02-the-qiskit-environment.html#exercise-6", "text": "Run a single sampler call on two circuits: H then measure, and X then measure, with \\(1000\\) shots and a fixed seed. How do you access each circuit’s counts, and what should each contain? The result is indexed in submission order: result()[0] for the H circuit and result()[1] for the X circuit, each with its own data.meas. The H counts are near \\(500/500\\); the X counts are exactly {'1': 1000}. Batching circuits in one call is efficient, but the index ties each result to its circuit, so the order must be preserved."}, {"title": "The Qiskit Environment — Exercise 7", "chapter": "The Qiskit Environment", "url": "02-the-qiskit-environment.html#exercise-7", "text": "Show what a seed does. (a) Run the fair-coin circuit twice with seed 7 and once with seed 8. Which counts agree? (b) What does a fixed seed guarantee, and what does it not guarantee? (a) The two seed-7 runs give identical counts; the seed-8 run gives different counts, still near \\(2048\\). (b) A seed makes a particular sampling run repeatable with the same software and settings, which is valuable for regression tests. It does not make the result “correct,” it does not guarantee identical counts across library versions or simulators, and it does not remove statistical uncertainty: a seeded sample is still one sample."}, {"title": "The Qiskit Environment — Exercise 8", "chapter": "The Qiskit Environment", "url": "02-the-qiskit-environment.html#exercise-8", "text": "A student increases the shots from \\(4096\\) to \\(40960\\) with the same seed and expects every count to be exactly ten times larger. Why is this wrong, and how do the frequency fluctuations change? Counts are random samples: with more shots the sampler draws a different and longer sequence, so the new counts are not a scaled copy of the old ones. What changes predictably is the precision: the frequency standard deviation shrinks by \\(\\sqrt{10}\\approx3.2\\), from about \\(0.0078\\) to \\(0.0025\\). Expect the frequency to move closer to \\(0.5\\) on average, not the counts to scale exactly."}, {"title": "The Qiskit Environment — Exercise 9", "chapter": "The Qiskit Environment", "url": "02-the-qiskit-environment.html#exercise-9", "text": "Write a reusable test helper that decides whether counts from a fair-coin circuit are consistent with probability \\(\\frac12\\), using a tolerance of \\(z\\) standard deviations of the frequency. Apply it to a seeded run. How would you choose \\(z\\) for a suite of \\(100\\) such tests? The helper below computes the tolerance \\(z/(2\\sqrt R)\\) and compares the observed frequency. With \\(z=5\\) a correct test fails with probability under \\(10^{-6}\\), so even \\(100\\) tests rarely raise a false alarm (about \\(10^{-4}\\) in total); with \\(z=2\\), about \\(5\\%\\) of correct tests would fail somewhere in every run of the suite. The number of tests, the acceptable false-alarm rate, and the smallest bug to catch together determine \\(z\\) and the shots."}, {"title": "The Qiskit Environment — Exercise 10", "chapter": "The Qiskit Environment", "url": "02-the-qiskit-environment.html#exercise-10", "text": "For deterministic properties, sampling is unnecessary. Use Statevector to obtain the exact outcome probabilities of the ry(\\(2\\pi/3\\)) circuit (after removing its final measurements), and test them against \\(0.25\\) and \\(0.75\\). When is sampling still the right test? Statevector computes the exact probabilities \\(0.25\\) and \\(0.75\\), so the test can use a tight numerical tolerance and has no statistical failures. Sampling remains the right test when the behavior being checked is statistical or involves classical processing of samples: estimator code, mid-circuit measurements with feedback, noise simulations, or the sampler interface itself. Use exact methods for the mathematics and sampling for the pipeline."}, {"title": "Python and Environments", "chapter": "The Qiskit Environment", "url": "02-the-qiskit-environment.html#section-2-1", "text": "2.1 Python and Environments Our experiment starts from \\(\\ket0\\), applies H, and measures, many times. Since \\(H\\ket0=(\\ket0+\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\), each outcome has probability one half, so we expect a fair coin. Writing this program needs a few Python features beyond the Bell function, and running it elsewhere needs the same software. We take the language first and the software second. 2.1.1 Python through circuit examples How do we tell the computer to build \\(H\\ket0\\), measure it many times, and count the outcomes? The building part uses only what we already know. We name a one-qubit circuit and append gates with method calls: circuit = QuantumCircuit(1)\ncircuit.h(0)\ncircuit.measure_all() The method measure_all() measures every qubit. It also adds a classical register, a row of classical bits that holds the results, and names it meas . Running the circuit is the job of a sampler , an object that runs circuits and returns their measured outcomes. A sampler is built to run many circuits in one request, so it expects a list of circuits, even when we have only one. Square brackets make a list: [circuit] is a list with one item, and [a, b] has two. Inputs can also be given by name, as in shots=4096 , which asks for 4096 repetitions. What comes back is a tally: how many times each bit string appeared. Python stores such a tally as a dictionary , a table that maps each key to a value. A run might return {'1': 2030, '0': 2066} , where the keys are the bit strings and the values are their counts. We look up a value with counts.get(\"1\", 0) . The second argument is what we get if the key is missing, which happens when a string never occurred. The expression sum(counts.values()) adds all the counts. Finally, we turn our prediction into checks with assert , as in the Bell program. The test == asks whether two values are equal, < asks whether one is smaller, and abs gives an absolute value. 2.1.2 Packages, versions, and isolated environments Suppose the coin program runs on our computer but fails on a friend’s, with an error saying some name does not exist. How can the same text behave differently? The program is not the only code involved. Its import lines pull in packages , bundles of code written by others and installed separately, such as qiskit . Each package exists in many versions, and between versions names change and behaviour can change. A package also relies on other packages, its dependencies , which have versions of their own. So the friend’s machine runs our text with different code underneath it. The remedy has two parts. First we record the exact versions we tested. The file requirements.txt lists them as lines such as qiskit==2.6.0 , which pins that package to one version. A second file, requirements-lock.txt , records every package that was installed, including the dependencies of dependencies. Second, we install those versions in a virtual environment , a folder holding its own Python and its own packages, separate from the rest of the computer. Other projects then cannot change our versions, and we cannot change theirs. From the repository root, the first command below creates the environment, and the second switches the terminal to it. The third uses pip , Python’s package installer, to install the pinned versions. The fourth runs the companion program. The accompanying TESTING.md records the Python version and platform on which the companions were tested. python3 -m venv .venv-quantum\nsource .venv-quantum/bin/activate\npython -m pip install -r education/programming/requirements.txt\npython education/programming/examples/chapter_02.py"}, {"title": "The Qiskit Ecosystem", "chapter": "The Qiskit Environment", "url": "02-the-qiskit-environment.html#section-2-2", "text": "2.2 The Qiskit Ecosystem The requirements file installs three Qiskit packages, not one. Why three, and which do we need to flip a simulated coin? Once the software is installed, we must also decide where the program lives: in a script or in a notebook. The packages come first, because they decide what can run locally. 2.2.1 SDK, Aer, and Runtime Which pieces do we need to run the coin on our own computer, and which only to reach real hardware? Each package answers a different need. The package qiskit is the SDK itself. It builds circuits and computes quantum-information objects such as Statevector . It contains the compiler that rewrites circuits for a particular device. It also supplies reference primitives : standard interfaces for two basic requests, “sample these circuits” and “estimate these expectation values,” carried out by exact classical simulation. Our coin needs nothing more, because its sampler is one of these primitives. Exact simulation of an ideal circuit is not always enough. When we later want faster simulators, or simulators that imitate a noisy device, we use the separate package qiskit-aer , called Aer. To send circuits to IBM’s remote devices we use qiskit-ibm-runtime , which needs an account. Importing that package contacts no one: only an explicit submission does. So none of the local examples needs an account or a token. 2.2.2 Notebooks and executable scripts Suppose we develop the coin in a notebook, where code sits in cells that we run one at a time. We set shots = 100 in one cell and run it. Later we change that cell to shots = 4096 , run a different cell, and delete the first one. Can a reader reproduce our result from the notebook they receive? Not reliably. A notebook keeps every variable from the cells run so far, in whatever order we ran them. The result can depend on a cell that no longer exists. A script is a plain file that Python runs from top to bottom in a fresh process, so nothing hidden survives from before. If we use a notebook, we restart it and run all cells in order before trusting a result. The tested version of each experiment in this course is a script. What else must we write down? The versions, the parameters, the number of shots, and the seed. A simulator does not use true randomness. It uses a pseudorandom number generator , a fixed algorithm whose outputs look random and are determined by a starting number called the seed . The same seed with the same implementation gives the same sequence of outcomes. A new version may consume the generator differently, so a seed reproduces a run only within one implementation. Problem 2.1. What does changing the seed change? Worked solution It changes the pseudorandom shot sequence, not the ideal quantum probability. A changed probability indicates a different circuit, model, or execution setting."}, {"title": "A First Program", "chapter": "The Qiskit Environment", "url": "02-the-qiskit-environment.html#section-2-3", "text": "2.3 A First Program We now have the language, a pinned environment and the packages. We can run the coin. Two questions remain: what exactly does the program get back, and which outputs should we accept as correct? The first is about reading results, and the second about the randomness that every quantum program must allow for. 2.3.1 Build, run, and inspect What happens between handing the sampler our circuit and holding a dictionary of counts? The program below writes each step on its own line, so that we can name it. The first two imports let the program report the Python and Qiskit versions it ran with, the record we decided to keep. The next two bring in the circuit class and StatevectorSampler , a reference sampler. After building the circuit, sampler = StatevectorSampler(seed=7) creates a sampler with a fixed seed. Its method run does not hand back counts directly. It returns a job , a handle to work that may still be in progress. This matters for remote devices, where a job can wait in a queue. Calling job.result() waits until the work is done and fetches the result. The result holds one entry for each circuit in our list. Square brackets after it select an entry by position, starting from zero, so result[0] is the entry for our only circuit. Inside it, .data.meas is the record of the register meas , and get_counts() turns that record into the dictionary. \"\"\"Runnable companion for Quantum Programming, Chapter 2.\"\"\"\nimport sys\nfrom importlib.metadata import version\nfrom qiskit import QuantumCircuit\nfrom qiskit.primitives import StatevectorSampler\n\ncircuit = QuantumCircuit(1)\ncircuit.h(0)\ncircuit.measure_all()\n\nsampler = StatevectorSampler(seed=7)\njob = sampler.run([circuit], shots=4096)\nresult = job.result()\ncounts = result[0].data.meas.get_counts()\n\nassert sum(counts.values()) == 4096\nassert abs(counts.get(\"1\", 0) / 4096 - 0.5) < 0.05\nprint(\"Python\", sys.version.split()[0], \"Qiskit\", version(\"qiskit\"))\nprint(counts) Companion script: chapter_02.py . The sampler computed the amplitudes \\((1/\\sqrt2,1/\\sqrt2)\\) internally to generate its samples. What we received, though, is only a classical record of outcomes, the same kind of record a physical device would give. Problem 2.2. Where are amplitudes measured in this example? Worked solution They are not physically measured. The reference simulator computes them classically to generate samples. Hardware can provide measurement outcomes, not its complete internal state vector. 2.3.2 Predictions and reproducible results Which counts should our test accept? The obvious attempt demands exactly 2048 ones out of \\(R=4096\\) shots. Our run gave 2030, and the circuit is correct. So how far from 2048 may a correct run land? Let \\(b_k\\) be 1 if shot \\(k\\) gives one and 0 otherwise, with probability \\(p=\\tfrac12\\) of a one. A single \\(b_k\\) has mean \\(p\\). Its variance is \\(\\mathbb E[b_k^2]-p^2=p-p^2=p(1-p)\\), because \\(b_k^2=b_k\\). The frequency is \\(f=(b_1+\\dots+b_R)/R\\). For independent shots the variances add, and dividing by \\(R\\) divides the variance by \\(R^2\\), so \\[\\operatorname{Var} f=\\frac{p(1-p)}{R},\\qquad \\text{standard deviation}=\\sqrt{\\frac{p(1-p)}{R}}=\\frac1{2\\sqrt R}=\\frac1{128}.\\] The test accepts any frequency within 0.05 of one half. That is \\(0.05\\times128=6.4\\) standard deviations. For so many shots the frequency is very nearly normally distributed, and a correct circuit lands outside that window with probability about \\(2\\times10^{-10}\\). Our run lies \\(18/4096\\approx0.0044\\) from one half, well inside. A second check confirms that all 4096 shots are present. What does a pass establish? Only that the outcome distribution looks fair. The circuit that prepares \\((\\ket0-\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\) would pass too, and so would a classical coin, so a pass is not evidence that we applied H rather than something else. The fixed seed adds a different kind of value. With the same seed and versions, the program prints the same counts every time, so a change in the output tells us that something in the code or the environment changed. Re-running a fixed program to detect such changes is called regression testing . Problem 2.3. Why should a fair coin not be tested by demanding exactly 2048 ones? Worked solution Even an ideal fair source fluctuates. Exact equality would reject many correct executions. Test a distribution with a justified tolerance and test the unitary separately. Our coin had a single qubit, so each outcome was one character. With two qubits a sampler returns strings such as 01 . Which character belongs to qubit 0? Sources and further study: IBM, SDK primitive simulation ."}, {"title": "Circuit Construction — Exercise 1", "chapter": "Circuit Construction", "url": "03-circuit-construction.html#exercise-1", "text": "A three-qubit circuit applies X only to qubit 2. Predict its bit-string label and its statevector index before running it. The label is 100 and the index is \\(4\\): qubit 2 is the most significant of three bits. probabilities_dict returns {'100': 1.0}. Reading labels left to right as qubits 0, 1, 2 would wrongly predict 001."}, {"title": "Circuit Construction — Exercise 2", "chapter": "Circuit Construction", "url": "03-circuit-construction.html#exercise-2", "text": "Qubit 0 is flipped to 1, and qubit 1 is left at 0. The circuit then measures qubit 0 into classical bit 1 and qubit 1 into classical bit 0. What bit string appears in the counts? Classical bit 1 receives \\(1\\) and classical bit 0 receives \\(0\\), and count strings list classical bits from highest to lowest, so every shot gives 10. The physical state is the same \\(\\ket{q_1q_0}=\\ket{01}\\) as without the swapped mapping; only the storage changed. Decoding counts requires knowing the measurement map."}, {"title": "Circuit Construction — Exercise 3", "chapter": "Circuit Construction", "url": "03-circuit-construction.html#exercise-3", "text": "For the state prepared by X on qubit 0 of two qubits, predict the expectation values of the Pauli labels ZI and IZ. ZI acts with \\(Z\\) on qubit 1, which is \\(\\ket0\\): expectation \\(+1\\). IZ acts on qubit 0, which is \\(\\ket1\\): expectation \\(-1\\). Swapping the two predictions is the typical symptom of reading Pauli labels in the wrong order."}, {"title": "Circuit Construction — Exercise 4", "chapter": "Circuit Construction", "url": "03-circuit-construction.html#exercise-4", "text": "A Bell-pair subcircuit (H on its qubit 0, CX from 0 to 1) should be placed on qubits 2 and 0 of a three-qubit circuit. A student writes the code below and finds that big has no instructions. (a) Why? (b) Fix it. (c) Predict the resulting state’s labels. (a) compose returns a new circuit and leaves big unchanged unless inplace=True is given; the returned circuit was discarded. (b) Assign the result (or pass inplace=True). (c) The mapping puts H on qubit 2 and a CX from qubit 2 to qubit 0, entangling those two while qubit 1 stays \\(\\ket0\\): the labels are 000 and 101, each with probability \\(\\frac12\\)."}, {"title": "Circuit Construction — Exercise 5", "chapter": "Circuit Construction", "url": "03-circuit-construction.html#exercise-5", "text": "A Toffoli gate is drawn as one box. Decompose it once and count the resulting instructions. What does the count suggest about judging circuit cost from a drawing? One level of decomposition gives \\(6\\) CNOTs, \\(4\\) \\(T\\) gates, \\(3\\) \\(T^\\dagger\\) gates, and \\(2\\) Hadamards: fifteen elementary instructions for one drawn box. On fault-tolerant hardware the seven \\(T\\)-type gates dominate the cost. A compact drawing says nothing about resources; counts must be taken after decomposition, ideally after transpiling to the target gate set."}, {"title": "Circuit Construction — Exercise 6", "chapter": "Circuit Construction", "url": "03-circuit-construction.html#exercise-6", "text": "Counts of only 00 and 11 do not distinguish a Bell state from a classical mixture of \\(\\ket{00}\\) and \\(\\ket{11}\\). Write a check that does: apply H to both qubits before measuring, and compute the exact outcome probabilities for both preparations, using DensityMatrix for the mixture. After \\(H\\otimes H\\), the Bell state still gives only 00 and 11, each with probability \\(\\frac12\\), while the mixture gives all four outcomes with probability \\(\\frac14\\). Exact probabilities make the test deterministic; in an experiment, the same comparison is made with sampled counts and a tolerance. Checking a second basis is what certifies coherence."}, {"title": "Circuit Construction — Exercise 7", "chapter": "Circuit Construction", "url": "03-circuit-construction.html#exercise-7", "text": "Prepare a five-qubit GHZ state \\((\\ket{00000}+\\ket{11111})/\\sqrt2\\) in two ways: a chain of CNOTs \\(0\\to1\\to2\\to3\\to4\\), and a tree in which qubit 0 controls 1 and 2, then qubit 1 controls 3 while qubit 0 controls 4. Compare their depths and verify that the states agree. The chain has depth \\(5\\) (H plus four sequential CNOTs); the tree has depth \\(4\\), because the CNOTs \\(0\\to2\\) and \\(1\\to3\\) run in the same layer. Both prepare the same state. For \\(n\\) qubits a tree needs depth about \\(1+\\lceil\\log_2n\\rceil\\), against \\(n\\) for a chain, so the same mathematical state can be much shallower, an important difference when qubits decohere while waiting."}, {"title": "Circuit Construction — Exercise 8", "chapter": "Circuit Construction", "url": "03-circuit-construction.html#exercise-8", "text": "A student reads count strings from left to right in the same order as the wires in the text drawing, top to bottom, and concludes that 01 means “qubit 0 is 0 and qubit 1 is 1.” Show with a one-gate example that this is backwards. Apply X to qubit 0 only: the label is 01. The rightmost character is qubit 0, which is \\(1\\); the leftmost is qubit 1, which is \\(0\\). The drawing lists qubit 0 at the top, but the bit string lists it last, following the integer convention in which qubit 0 is the least significant bit. Every analysis that maps strings back to qubits must use this rule, or reverse the order explicitly."}, {"title": "Circuit Construction — Exercise 9", "chapter": "Circuit Construction", "url": "03-circuit-construction.html#exercise-9", "text": "Some textbooks write the first qubit leftmost. Use Statevector.reverse_qargs() to convert the state of X on qubit 0 into that convention, and explain when such a conversion is appropriate and when it hides a bug. In Qiskit’s convention the state is labelled 01; after reverse_qargs() it is labelled 10, matching a convention in which qubit 0 is written first. Converting is appropriate at a single, documented boundary, for example when comparing with a formula from a paper. Scattering conversions through a program, or reversing to make a failing test pass without understanding why, can mask a genuine wiring or measurement-map error."}, {"title": "Circuit Construction — Exercise 10", "chapter": "Circuit Construction", "url": "03-circuit-construction.html#exercise-10", "text": "A circuit has two classical registers: a (one bit) measuring qubit 0, and b (two bits) measuring qubits 1 and 2. Qubit 1 is flipped. Predict the counts stored for each register and the combined string returned by join_data(). Why is it safer to read registers by name? Register a reports 0 (qubit 0 is \\(\\ket0\\)). Register b reports 01: its bit 0 holds qubit 1 (\\(1\\)) and its bit 1 holds qubit 2 (\\(0\\)). join_data() concatenates the registers, here as 010, with b on the left because it was added after a. Reading registers by name avoids depending on concatenation order, which changes if the circuit is built differently."}, {"title": "Registers and Gates", "chapter": "Circuit Construction", "url": "03-circuit-construction.html#section-3-1", "text": "3.1 Registers and Gates The Bell pair comes from H on one qubit followed by a CNOT. To write it as a program we must name the qubits, name the bits that receive their measurements, and put the gates in order. Only equal bits should appear when we measure, but a symmetric result can hide an ordering mistake, so we will need an asymmetric test before trusting the program. We begin with the names. 3.1.1 Quantum and classical registers What does QuantumCircuit(2) actually create? It creates two quantum wires, numbered 0 and 1. A group of qubits addressed together is called a quantum register . Qubits carry quantum states, but measurement results are ordinary bits, and they need somewhere to go. A classical register is a row of classical bits that receives measurement records. Gates refer to qubits by number. In bell.h(0) followed by bell.cx(0, 1) , the first argument of cx is the control and the second is the target. So qubit 0 gets the H and controls the CNOT, and qubit 1 is flipped when qubit 0 is 1. That fixes what each instruction does. It does not yet fix how a two-qubit state or a measured string is written down. When we computed the Bell state by hand, we wrote qubit 0 as the first label. Whether Qiskit does the same is a question we will settle by experiment. 3.1.2 Gate order and circuit composition Each method call appends its gate after the ones already there, so the program lists gates in the order they happen. How does that order appear in the matrix of the whole circuit? Suppose we apply \\(U\\) and then \\(V\\). The state becomes \\(V(U\\ket\\psi)=(VU)\\ket\\psi\\). The gate applied last stands on the left. So a program that appends \\(U_1,U_2,\\dots,U_k\\) has the matrix \\(U_k\\cdots U_2U_1\\), the reverse of the reading order. Programs also need to join circuits. The method compose places one circuit after another and returns the combined circuit. Its qubits argument says which wires of the larger circuit receive the smaller circuit’s wires. Writing qubits=[1, 0] , for instance, sends the small circuit’s qubit 0 to wire 1. Without such a mapping, a component built for one wire order would be silently attached in another. A composed circuit may also appear as one labelled box. A box can stand for many elementary gates, and its size on the page says nothing about that number. The method decompose() replaces each box by its contents, one level at a time, so we can see what it costs."}, {"title": "Measurement", "chapter": "Circuit Construction", "url": "03-circuit-construction.html#section-3-2", "text": "3.2 Measurement The gates are now in order, but the result of a program is a string of classical bits. Two choices stand between a qubit and a character of that string: which bit stores the qubit’s measurement, and where that bit appears in the printed string. We make the first choice explicit, then use an asymmetric experiment to discover the second. 3.2.1 Mapping qubits to classical bits Who decides which classical bit receives which qubit’s outcome? We do, or a default does. The coin program used measure_all() , which adds a register named meas and measures qubit \\(j\\) into bit \\(j\\). The method measure(qubit, clbit) instead names one qubit and one classical bit, so we can choose any mapping. Suppose we measure qubit 0 into bit 1 and qubit 1 into bit 0. The quantum state is unchanged, yet every result string now describes the qubits in the swapped order. A count string describes classical storage, not wires. So decoding a result requires knowing the measurement mapping. 3.2.2 Bit strings and tensor-product ordering With the default mapping, which character of a printed string belongs to qubit 0? The Bell pair cannot tell us, because 00 and 11 read the same in both directions. So we need a state that is not symmetric. Suppose we apply X to qubit 0 alone. Then qubit 0 is 1 and qubit 1 is 0. If Qiskit wrote qubit 0 first, as we did by hand, we would see 10 . The companion asks for the probabilities of this state, and the answer is {\"01\": 1.0} . So qubit 0 is the rightmost character. Qiskit reads a bit string as a binary number, with qubit 0 as the least significant digit, just as the ones digit of an ordinary number sits on the right. A two-qubit basis state is written \\(\\ket{q_1q_0}\\), and its position in the state vector is \\(k=q_0+2q_1\\). Our state is \\(\\ket{01}\\), at index 1. The tensor product follows the same convention. The state vector is \\(\\ket{q_1}\\otimes\\ket{q_0}\\), so a gate \\(A\\) on qubit 0 alone has the matrix \\(I\\otimes A\\), with \\(A\\) on the right. The Bell state \\((\\ket{00}+\\ket{11})/\\sqrt2\\) is the same in both conventions, which is why the Bell test could not catch a mistake. A drawing with qubit 0 at the top does not change any of this. It is a picture, not a reordering of the mathematics. Problem 3.1. What state results from X on qubit one alone? Worked solution It is |10〉, with state-vector index two. This is the opposite single-bit position from X on qubit zero."}, {"title": "Circuit Inspection", "chapter": "Circuit Construction", "url": "03-circuit-construction.html#section-3-3", "text": "3.3 Circuit Inspection We now know what every index means. Can we confirm that the program we wrote is the circuit we meant? Two kinds of evidence are available: what the circuit looks like, and what it computes. We take the picture first, then compare the computed results with our prediction. 3.3.1 Drawing and decomposing circuits The quickest check is to look. draw(output=\"text\") prints a drawing made of characters, so it needs no plotting software. We read its wires from left to right in time, and qubit 0 is the top wire. The returned bit strings still follow the classical register order, with qubit 0 on the right. So the top-to-bottom order of the drawing is the right-to-left order of a result string. A drawing can mislead in one way. A familiar-looking box may hide arithmetic or gates with many controls. When a box appears, decompose() shows what it contains, and count_ops() counts the gates of each kind. A compact picture is not a small circuit. 3.3.2 Comparing code with predicted evolution What should the program’s numbers be? The Bell state is \\((\\ket{00}+\\ket{11})/\\sqrt2\\), so ideal measurements give 00 and 11 , each with probability one half, and never 01 or 10 . The exact split between 00 and 11 fluctuates from run to run, so the test cannot demand it. What it can demand is that no other string ever appears. The companion repeats the steps of the coin program on one line. Each dot applies to the result of everything to its left. The expression set(counts) makes a set , a collection without order or repeats, holding the keys of the dictionary. The test <= between sets asks whether the first is contained in the second. So the first check says that every observed string is 00 or 11 . The second check is the asymmetric experiment, computed exactly with probabilities_dict() . \"\"\"Runnable companion for Quantum Programming, Chapter 3.\"\"\"\nfrom qiskit import QuantumCircuit\nfrom qiskit.primitives import StatevectorSampler\nfrom qiskit.quantum_info import Statevector\n\nbell = QuantumCircuit(2)\nbell.h(0)\nbell.cx(0, 1)\nbell.measure_all()\ncounts = StatevectorSampler(seed=8).run([bell], shots=1024).result()[0].data.meas.get_counts()\nassert set(counts) <= {\"00\", \"11\"}\n# An asymmetric check is essential: a Bell state hides reversed bit order.\nasymmetric = QuantumCircuit(2)\nasymmetric.x(0)\nassert Statevector.from_instruction(asymmetric).probabilities_dict() == {\"01\": 1.0}\nprint(bell.draw(output=\"text\"))\nprint(counts) Companion script: chapter_03.py . With seed 8, the run printed 533 counts of 00 and 491 of 11 , and no other strings. Is that enough to show that the two qubits are entangled? Not yet. Consider a device that prepares \\(\\ket{00}\\) half the time and \\(\\ket{11}\\) otherwise. Such a random choice between states is a classical mixture . It gives exactly the same measured counts. To tell the two apart, we apply H to both qubits before measuring. On the Bell state, H on both qubits gives \\((\\ket{{+}{+}}+\\ket{{-}{-}})/\\sqrt2\\). Expanding, \\(\\ket{{+}{+}}=(\\ket{00}+\\ket{01}+\\ket{10}+\\ket{11})/2\\) and \\(\\ket{{-}{-}}=(\\ket{00}-\\ket{01}-\\ket{10}+\\ket{11})/2\\), so the 01 and 10 terms cancel and the Bell state returns. We still see only 00 and 11 . But H on both sends \\(\\ket{00}\\) to \\(\\ket{{+}{+}}\\) and \\(\\ket{11}\\) to \\(\\ket{{-}{-}}\\), and each of these gives all four strings with probability one quarter. Testing in this second basis exposes the difference. Problem 3.2. Would counts containing only 00 and 11 prove entanglement? Worked solution No. A classical mixture of \\(\\ket{00}\\) and \\(\\ket{11}\\) has the same computational-basis distribution. Applying H to both qubits before measurement distinguishes them: the Bell state still gives only 00 and 11, while the mixture gives all four strings equally often. Problem 3.3. Why include a text drawing in a runnable example? Worked solution It makes the appended gate order and wire targets inspectable. The numerical test still supplies the stronger check of the circuit’s action. Bell-pair preparation. A Hadamard followed by CNOT makes (|00⟩+|11⟩)/√2. The first wire is the control. Sampled counts gave us only indirect evidence about the state. Since a simulator computes the full state anyway, could we compare a circuit with its vector and its matrix directly? Sources and further study: IBM, bit ordering ."}, {"title": "States and Operators — Exercise 1", "chapter": "States and Operators", "url": "04-states-and-operators.html#exercise-1", "text": "Two qubits are prepared with H on qubit 0 only. Predict partial_trace(state, [1]) and partial_trace(state, [0]). partial_trace(state, [1]) discards qubit 1 and keeps qubit 0, giving \\(\\ket+\\bra+\\), the matrix with all entries \\(\\frac12\\). partial_trace(state, [0]) keeps qubit 1, giving \\(\\ket0\\bra0\\). For a product state each reduced state is pure; for the Bell state both would be \\(I/2\\)."}, {"title": "States and Operators — Exercise 2", "chapter": "States and Operators", "url": "04-states-and-operators.html#exercise-2", "text": "Predict the Bell-state expectation values of ZZ, XX, YY, and XZ before computing them. \\(\\langle ZZ\\rangle=1\\), \\(\\langle XX\\rangle=1\\), \\(\\langle YY\\rangle=-1\\), and \\(\\langle XZ\\rangle=0\\). The value of YY follows from \\(Y\\otimes Y=-(X\\otimes X)(Z\\otimes Z)\\), and both factors are \\(+1\\) on the Bell state. Expectation values of several Paulis are a compact fingerprint of a state that can be checked exactly in simulation and estimated in experiment."}, {"title": "States and Operators — Exercise 3", "chapter": "States and Operators", "url": "04-states-and-operators.html#exercise-3", "text": "A dense operator on \\(n\\) qubits has \\(4^n\\) complex entries of \\(16\\) bytes each. Estimate its memory for \\(n=10\\) and \\(n=20\\). What does this imply for verifying circuits with Operator? \\(n=10\\): about \\(1.05\\times10^6\\) entries, \\(17\\) MB. \\(n=20\\): about \\(1.1\\times10^{12}\\) entries, \\(18\\) TB. Operator is an excellent check for small subcircuits, a dozen qubits or so, but not a general simulation strategy; larger tests need statevectors, structured simulators, or properties checked on samples."}, {"title": "States and Operators — Exercise 4", "chapter": "States and Operators", "url": "04-states-and-operators.html#exercise-4", "text": "Replace H by ry(\\(\\pi/3\\)) in the chapter’s companion, followed by the same CNOT. Predict the reduced state of qubit 0 and the expectations of ZZ and XX, then verify them. The state is \\(\\cos\\frac\\pi6\\ket{00}+\\sin\\frac\\pi6\\ket{11}\\). Qubit 0’s reduced state is \\(\\mathrm{diag}(0.75,0.25)\\) with no off-diagonal terms. \\(\\langle ZZ\\rangle=1\\), since both terms have equal bits, and \\(\\langle XX\\rangle=\\sin\\frac\\pi3\\approx0.866\\). The reduced state alone looks like a classical mixture; the \\(XX\\) correlation reveals the coherent superposition."}, {"title": "States and Operators — Exercise 5", "chapter": "States and Operators", "url": "04-states-and-operators.html#exercise-5", "text": "Compute the purity \\(\\mathrm{tr}\\rho^2\\) of the Bell state and of its one-qubit reduced state using DensityMatrix.purity(). Interpret the two numbers. The two-qubit Bell state has purity \\(1\\): it is pure. Its reduced state \\(I/2\\) has purity \\(\\frac12\\), the minimum for a qubit. Purity is a convenient numerical check: a globally pure state whose parts are mixed must be entangled, and the purity of the parts measures how strongly."}, {"title": "States and Operators — Exercise 6", "chapter": "States and Operators", "url": "04-states-and-operators.html#exercise-6", "text": "Define \\(H=0.5\\,ZZ-0.3\\,XI\\) as a SparsePauliOp. Predict its expectation value in the Bell state and in \\(\\ket{++}\\), and verify. Bell state: \\(\\langle ZZ\\rangle=1\\) and \\(\\langle XI\\rangle=0\\), so \\(\\langle H\\rangle=0.5\\). For \\(\\ket{++}\\): \\(\\langle ZZ\\rangle=0\\) and \\(\\langle XI\\rangle=1\\), so \\(\\langle H\\rangle=-0.3\\). Linear combinations of Pauli strings are evaluated term by term, which is also how an experiment estimates them, one measurement setting per group of compatible terms."}, {"title": "States and Operators — Exercise 7", "chapter": "States and Operators", "url": "04-states-and-operators.html#exercise-7", "text": "Verify the identity “Hadamards on the target turn CX into CZ” with Operator equivalence. Then explain why Operator.equiv, rather than exact matrix equality, is the right comparison. The circuit H on qubit 1, CX from 0 to 1, H on qubit 1 has the same operator as CZ, and Operator.equiv confirms it. equiv tests equality up to a global phase within a numerical tolerance, which is what “the same gate” means physically. Exact equality would fail on harmless phase conventions or rounding, while a relative phase error would still be caught."}, {"title": "States and Operators — Exercise 8", "chapter": "States and Operators", "url": "04-states-and-operators.html#exercise-8", "text": "A student calls Operator on a circuit that contains a measurement, expecting “the matrix of the circuit.” What happens, and what should they do to inspect the unitary part? Qiskit raises a QiskitError, “Cannot apply operation with classical bits: measure.” A measurement has no unitary matrix. To inspect the unitary part, remove the final measurements (remove_final_measurements(inplace=False)) and build the Operator of what remains; mid-circuit measurements need a channel or instrument description, not a unitary."}, {"title": "States and Operators — Exercise 9", "chapter": "States and Operators", "url": "04-states-and-operators.html#exercise-9", "text": "Estimate \\(\\langle ZZ\\rangle\\) and \\(\\langle XX\\rangle\\) for the ry(\\(\\pi/3\\)) state from sampled counts, as an experiment would: measure directly for ZZ, and apply H to both qubits before measuring for XX. Compare with the exact values \\(1\\) and \\(0.866\\), and explain the size of the discrepancy. With \\(8192\\) shots, the ZZ estimate is exactly \\(1\\) (only 00 and 11 occur) and the XX estimate is near \\(0.866\\), within about \\(\\sqrt{(1-0.75)/8192}\\approx0.006\\). The XX measurement requires a different circuit: one setting per non-commuting observable. Exact expectations check the program; sampled estimates model what an experiment reports, with an uncertainty that must be carried along."}, {"title": "States and Operators — Exercise 10", "chapter": "States and Operators", "url": "04-states-and-operators.html#exercise-10", "text": "A statevector of \\(n\\) qubits needs \\(2^n\\) amplitudes of \\(16\\) bytes. (a) Estimate the memory for \\(n=30\\) and \\(n=100\\). (b) Run a \\(100\\)-qubit GHZ circuit with Aer’s stabilizer method and check the outcomes. (c) Why is this possible, and what is its limit? (a) \\(n=30\\): about \\(17\\) GB; \\(n=100\\): about \\(2\\times10^{31}\\) bytes, impossible. (b) The stabilizer simulator runs the \\(100\\)-qubit GHZ circuit in a fraction of a second, and every outcome is all zeros or all ones. (c) Clifford circuits keep the state in a form described by polynomially many bits (a stabilizer tableau), so they can be simulated efficiently. Adding non-Clifford gates such as \\(T\\) or arbitrary rotations removes this shortcut; a simulator’s capacity depends on the circuit’s structure, not only its width."}, {"title": "State Representations", "chapter": "States and Operators", "url": "04-states-and-operators.html#section-4-1", "text": "4.1 State Representations A simulator can show the state hidden inside a circuit, which makes it a debugging instrument: we can compare the program with its predicted action before sampling or noise enter. What form should that state take? A list of amplitudes is the natural first answer. We will find two situations it cannot describe, a random preparation and one part of an entangled pair, and one representation that handles both. 4.1.1 State vectors and density matrices What exactly does Statevector.from_instruction(qc) compute? It starts every qubit in \\(\\ket0\\), applies the circuit’s gates, and returns the \\(2^n\\) amplitudes in index order, with index \\(k=q_0+2q_1+\\cdots\\). That works only for a circuit with no measurement or reset, since those are not unitary steps. Now recall the device that prepares \\(\\ket{00}\\) or \\(\\ket{11}\\) with probability one half each. Can some single vector \\(\\psi\\) describe it? To decide, we need a form in which predictions are easy to average. The average of an observable \\(A\\) in a pure state is \\(\\psi^\\dagger A\\psi\\), and we can rewrite this using the trace, the sum of a matrix’s diagonal entries: \\[\\psi^\\dagger A\\psi=\\sum_{j,k}\\psi_j^*A_{jk}\\psi_k=\\operatorname{tr}\\!\\left(A\\,\\psi\\psi^\\dagger\\right).\\] A random preparation averages its predictions, so it gives \\(\\operatorname{tr}(A\\rho)\\) with \\[\\rho=\\tfrac12\\ketbra{00}{00}+\\tfrac12\\ketbra{11}{11}.\\] The matrix \\(\\rho\\) is called a density matrix . For a mixture of states \\(\\psi_i\\) with probabilities \\(p_i\\), it is \\(\\rho=\\sum_ip_i\\,\\psi_i\\psi_i^\\dagger\\), and every prediction is \\(\\operatorname{tr}(A\\rho)\\). Is our \\(\\rho\\) secretly of the form \\(\\psi\\psi^\\dagger\\)? Any unit vector satisfies \\((\\psi\\psi^\\dagger)^2=\\psi(\\psi^\\dagger\\psi)\\psi^\\dagger=\\psi\\psi^\\dagger\\). Our \\(\\rho\\) instead gives \\(\\rho^2=\\tfrac14\\ketbra{00}{00}+\\tfrac14\\ketbra{11}{11}=\\rho/2\\). So no vector describes the mixture, and we need the more general object. Qiskit’s DensityMatrix class holds it, and DensityMatrix(state) turns a vector into \\(\\psi\\psi^\\dagger\\). 4.1.2 Composite systems and subsystem order Density matrices settle a question vectors could not. In a Bell pair, what can we say about qubit 0 alone? Any test on qubit 0 alone uses an observable of the form \\(I\\otimes A\\), with \\(A\\) on the right because qubit 0 is the right factor. For the Bell state \\(\\Phi=(\\ket{00}+\\ket{11})/\\sqrt2\\), \\[\\Phi^\\dagger(I\\otimes A)\\Phi=\\tfrac12\\left(\\bra0A\\ket0+\\bra1A\\ket1\\right)=\\operatorname{tr}\\!\\left(A\\,\\tfrac I2\\right).\\] So every test on qubit 0 behaves as if that qubit had the density matrix \\(I/2\\). Since \\((I/2)^2=I/4\\), no vector describes it either. The operation that produces a subsystem’s density matrix is the partial trace . To discard qubit 1, we sum over its label: \\[(\\rho_0)_{ab}=\\sum_{c}\\bra{c\\,a}\\rho\\ket{c\\,b},\\] where the left label \\(c\\) belongs to qubit 1. For the Bell pair only \\(c=a=b=0\\) and \\(c=a=b=1\\) contribute, each giving \\(\\tfrac12\\), so \\(\\rho_0=I/2\\). In Qiskit, partial_trace(state, [1]) takes the list of subsystems to discard , not those to keep. Is \\(I/2\\) just another way of writing \\(\\ket+\\)? Both give zero and one with probability one half. But \\[\\ketbra++=\\begin{pmatrix}\\tfrac12&\\tfrac12\\\\\\tfrac12&\\tfrac12\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad \\frac I2=\\begin{pmatrix}\\tfrac12&0\\\\0&\\tfrac12\\end{pmatrix}.\\] The average of X is \\(\\operatorname{tr}(X\\rho)=\\rho_{01}+\\rho_{10}\\), which is 1 for \\(\\ket+\\) and 0 for \\(I/2\\). So an X-basis test tells them apart. Problem 4.1. What does partial_trace(state,[1]) keep for two qubits? Worked solution It discards subsystem one and keeps subsystem zero. Confusing the two can pass unnoticed on symmetric states."}, {"title": "Operators and Observables", "chapter": "States and Operators", "url": "04-states-and-operators.html#section-4-2", "text": "4.2 Operators and Observables A state tells us what the circuit produced from one input, all zeros. A wrong circuit could still produce the right state from that input. And the averages we compared were computed from states one test at a time. We therefore need operators of two kinds: the circuit’s whole matrix, which covers every input, and the observables whose averages we predict. 4.2.1 Unitary operators How can we check a circuit on every input at once? Its matrix does that, because column \\(k\\) of the matrix is the output for basis input \\(k\\). Operator(qc) builds this matrix for a circuit with no measurement or reset. For it to be a valid quantum evolution, it must be unitary, \\(U^\\dagger U=I\\), and the companion checks this. The check uses NumPy, imported as np . NumPy stores matrices as arrays and supplies matrix arithmetic. Operator(qc).data is the matrix as such an array. The method .conj() takes complex conjugates and .T transposes, so .conj().T is the dagger. The operator @ multiplies matrices, and np.eye(4) is the \\(4\\times4\\) identity. Why not test equality with == ? Computers store \\(1/\\sqrt2\\) only approximately. In Python, (2**-0.5)**2 gives 0.5000000000000001 , not 0.5, so an exact comparison can fail for a correct circuit. np.allclose instead accepts entries that agree within a small tolerance. Its default tolerance is far tighter than any mistake we are looking for, and far looser than these rounding errors. How far does this check scale? An \\(n\\)-qubit unitary is a \\(2^n\\times2^n\\) matrix, so it has \\(2^n\\cdot2^n=4^n\\) complex entries. At 20 qubits that is about \\(1.1\\times10^{12}\\) entries, roughly 18 terabytes at 16 bytes each. A full matrix is a check for small circuits only. 4.2.2 Pauli observables and expectation values When we tested the Bell pair with H on both qubits, we inferred the result from counts. Can we predict such averages directly? The observables we need are Pauli strings : tensor products of \\(I,X,Y,Z\\), written as letters. Following the bit-order convention, the rightmost letter acts on qubit 0, so ZI means \\(Z\\) on qubit 1 and nothing on qubit 0. Each Pauli string has eigenvalues \\(\\pm1\\), so a single measurement of it gives \\(+1\\) or \\(-1\\), and its average lies between \\(-1\\) and \\(1\\). For the Bell state, the two Z outcomes always agree, so their product is always \\(+1\\) and \\(\\langle ZZ\\rangle=1\\). Since \\(XX\\) swaps \\(\\ket{00}\\) with \\(\\ket{11}\\), it leaves the Bell state unchanged, so \\(\\langle XX\\rangle=1\\) as well. Qubit 1 alone gives each Z outcome half the time, so \\(\\langle ZI\\rangle=0\\). Measuring \\(XX\\) is the H-on-both test, since \\(HXH=Z\\): we apply H to each qubit and read the product of the Z outcomes. Qiskit’s SparsePauliOp stores an observable as a list of Pauli strings with coefficients, such as \\(-ZZ-XX\\), without building the \\(4^n\\)-entry matrix. The method state.expectation_value(op) computes \\(\\langle\\psi|\\text{op}|\\psi\\rangle\\) exactly. An experiment cannot do that. It estimates the same number by repeating measurements and averaging. Problem 4.2. Does a zero expectation of Z imply a maximally mixed state? Worked solution No. Plus, minus, and many other states have zero Z expectation. Additional observables are needed to determine the state."}, {"title": "Simulation", "chapter": "States and Operators", "url": "04-states-and-operators.html#section-4-3", "text": "4.3 Simulation We now hold three kinds of numbers for one circuit: exact amplitudes and density matrices, exact expectation values, and sampled counts. They come from different calculations and mean different things. When a result disagrees with a prediction, which of them should we doubt, and which could a laboratory even produce? We compare exact and sampled numbers first, then ask what physical measurement can recover. 4.3.1 Exact evolution and sampled results Suppose a test reports \\(\\langle ZI\\rangle=0.03\\) instead of 0. Is the program wrong? That depends on where the number came from. An exact expectation from expectation_value checks the ideal mathematical action, so a 0.03 there would signal a bug. A mean of \\(R\\) sampled \\(\\pm1\\) values fluctuates, with a standard deviation near \\(1/\\sqrt R\\) when the true mean is 0, so at \\(R=1024\\) a value of 0.03 is ordinary. Later we will add a third layer, a simulation of a noisy device, whose results differ from both. If we keep exact, sampled and noisy results separate, a discrepancy points to one cause: the code, the sampling, or the noise model. 4.3.2 Simulator access versus physical measurement The companion prints the density matrix of qubit 0. Which of its numbers could an experiment recover, and how? A single run of a device gives one bit string, never an amplitude. Repeated runs estimate probabilities, and these are the diagonal entries of a density matrix. The off-diagonal entries take more work. Writing a one-qubit density matrix as \\(\\rho=(I+xX+yY+zZ)/2\\), with \\(x=\\langle X\\rangle\\) and so on, gives \\(\\rho_{01}=(x-iy)/2\\). So averages in the X and Y bases recover them. This reconstruction from several measurement bases is called tomography , and for \\(n\\) qubits it needs \\(4^n-1\\) averages, so it is practical only for small systems. A global phase is never recoverable at all. A simulator, by contrast, hands us every number for free. That is its value as a debugging tool, and also why it can mislead us about what an experiment will show. The companion checks the Bell correlations, the reduced state and unitarity, all with tolerances for floating-point rounding. \"\"\"Runnable companion for Quantum Programming, Chapter 4.\"\"\"\nimport numpy as np\nfrom qiskit import QuantumCircuit\nfrom qiskit.quantum_info import Statevector, DensityMatrix, Operator, SparsePauliOp, partial_trace\n\nqc = QuantumCircuit(2)\nqc.h(0)\nqc.cx(0, 1)\nstate = Statevector.from_instruction(qc)\nlocal = partial_trace(DensityMatrix(state), [1])\nassert np.allclose(local.data, np.eye(2) / 2)\nassert np.isclose(state.expectation_value(SparsePauliOp(\"XX\")), 1)\nassert np.isclose(state.expectation_value(SparsePauliOp(\"ZI\")), 0)\nassert np.allclose(Operator(qc).data.conj().T @ Operator(qc).data, np.eye(4))\nprint(\"Local state:\", local.data) Companion script: chapter_04.py . To extend the check, replace H by a rotation \\(R_y(\\theta)\\). The circuit then prepares \\(\\cos(\\theta/2)\\ket{00}+\\sin(\\theta/2)\\ket{11}\\). Predict qubit 0’s density matrix before running: \\(\\operatorname{diag}(\\cos^2(\\theta/2),\\sin^2(\\theta/2))\\). Its off-diagonal entries vanish after the CNOT, even though the two-qubit state is pure. Problem 4.3. Why avoid dense matrices when a program must handle many qubits? Worked solution Their classical construction may dominate the experiment exponentially. Use them as small-instance correctness checks, and report scalable resource counts separately. Bell-pair preparation. A Hadamard followed by CNOT makes (|00⟩+|11⟩)/√2. The first wire is the control. Every circuit so far was written out gate by gate. When a construction is used many times, at different angles or on different wires, how do we write it once and trust each use? Sources and further study: IBM, quantum information API ."}, {"title": "Reusable Quantum Programs — Exercise 1", "chapter": "Reusable Quantum Programs", "url": "05-reusable-quantum-programs.html#exercise-1", "text": "The factory below appends to a module-level circuit and returns it. What does each returned object contain after two calls, and why is this a bug? Both names refer to the same circuit, which now holds two H gates, so the first result changed after it was returned. Any experiment that used it silently ran the wrong circuit. A factory should construct and return a fresh circuit on every call, as the chapter’s rotation factory does."}, {"title": "Reusable Quantum Programs — Exercise 2", "chapter": "Reusable Quantum Programs", "url": "05-reusable-quantum-programs.html#exercise-2", "text": "After bound = component.assign_parameters({theta: 0.7}), which circuit still has a free parameter: component or bound? What does that imply for reusing component? component still lists theta in its parameters; bound has none. assign_parameters returns a new, bound circuit and leaves the template symbolic, so the same component can be bound to many values in a sweep without being rebuilt."}, {"title": "Reusable Quantum Programs — Exercise 3", "chapter": "Reusable Quantum Programs", "url": "05-reusable-quantum-programs.html#exercise-3", "text": "Package ry(0.7) as a gate, add one control, and transpile the two-qubit circuit to the basis cx, rz, sx, x. How many CNOTs does the controlled rotation need? The transpiled circuit uses \\(2\\) CNOTs plus single-qubit gates. Adding a control turned one rotation into an entangling subcircuit; for a controlled multi-gate routine the cost grows with every gate controlled. control() is a mathematical construction with a real price, not a free flag."}, {"title": "Reusable Quantum Programs — Exercise 4", "chapter": "Reusable Quantum Programs", "url": "05-reusable-quantum-programs.html#exercise-4", "text": "The chapter checks that a bound rotation composed with its inverse is the identity. (a) Show that this test also passes for a wrong rotation, ry(\\(-0.7\\)). (b) Write a test that checks the specification itself: the state prepared from \\(\\ket0\\) should be \\(\\cos(0.35)\\ket0+\\sin(0.35)\\ket1\\). (c) What does the \\(UU^\\dagger\\) test actually establish? (a) For any unitary \\(U\\), \\(UU^\\dagger=I\\), so ry(\\(-0.7\\)) passes just as ry(\\(0.7\\)) does. (b) Compare the prepared statevector with the expected amplitudes; the wrong rotation gives \\(\\cos(0.35)\\ket0-\\sin(0.35)\\ket1\\) and fails. (c) The inverse test checks only that the circuit is unitary and that inverse() is consistent with it. It says nothing about whether the circuit implements the intended operation; tests must compare against an independent specification."}, {"title": "Reusable Quantum Programs — Exercise 5", "chapter": "Reusable Quantum Programs", "url": "05-reusable-quantum-programs.html#exercise-5", "text": "A predicate factory computes \\(x_0\\land x_1\\) into a scratch qubit (2) with a Toffoli, copies it into a target (3) with a CNOT, and forgets to uncompute. (a) For the inputs in uniform superposition, find the probability that the scratch qubit is left in \\(\\ket1\\). (b) Fix the factory and verify that the scratch qubit returns to \\(\\ket0\\) on this coherent input. (c) Why test with a superposition rather than with each basis input separately? (a) The scratch qubit holds \\(x_0x_1\\), so it is \\(\\ket1\\) with probability \\(\\frac14\\), and it is entangled with the inputs. (b) Applying the Toffoli again after the copy returns the scratch to \\(\\ket0\\) for every input, and its reduced state is exactly \\(\\ket0\\bra0\\). (c) Each basis input alone would show a definite scratch value and might pass a check that only looks at the target. The coherent input reveals whether the scratch factors out, which is what later interference requires."}, {"title": "Reusable Quantum Programs — Exercise 6", "chapter": "Reusable Quantum Programs", "url": "05-reusable-quantum-programs.html#exercise-6", "text": "A circuit applies ry(theta) and then rz(alpha) to one qubit. A student passes the parameter values [0.0, \\(\\pi/2\\)] to an Estimator, intending theta \\(=0\\) and alpha \\(=\\pi/2\\), and measures \\(\\langle X\\rangle\\). (a) What value do they expect? (b) Qiskit orders a circuit’s parameters by name. What value is returned, and why? (c) How should the values be supplied to avoid this? (a) With theta \\(=0\\) the qubit stays \\(\\ket0\\), so \\(\\langle X\\rangle=0\\). (b) The circuit’s parameters are ordered alpha, theta, so the values assign alpha \\(=0\\) and theta \\(=\\pi/2\\): ry(\\(\\pi/2\\)) prepares \\(\\ket+\\) and the result is \\(1\\). (c) Inspect circuit.parameters before building value arrays, or bind with an explicit dictionary such as {theta: 0.0, alpha: \\(\\pi/2\\)}. Positional parameter arrays are a frequent source of silent errors."}, {"title": "Reusable Quantum Programs — Exercise 7", "chapter": "Reusable Quantum Programs", "url": "05-reusable-quantum-programs.html#exercise-7", "text": "rz(\\(\\lambda\\)) and p(\\(\\lambda\\)) differ only by a global phase. Compare their controlled versions crz(0.9) and cp(0.9) with Operator equivalence. Then find the one-qubit gate on the control that makes them equal. The controlled versions are not equivalent: the phase \\(e^{-i\\lambda/2}\\) that was global for the single-qubit gate now multiplies only the control-1 branch. Adding p(\\(0.45\\)) on the control to crz(0.9) restores the factor and makes it equal to cp(0.9). A routine correct “up to global phase” must have that phase fixed before it is controlled."}, {"title": "Reusable Quantum Programs — Exercise 8", "chapter": "Reusable Quantum Programs", "url": "05-reusable-quantum-programs.html#exercise-8", "text": "A student believes inverse() cannot be applied until all parameters are bound, “because the inverse angle is not known yet.” Build ry(theta).inverse() with theta unbound, bind theta afterwards, and check the result. inverse() works symbolically: it produces ry(\\(-\\)theta), whose angle is a parameter expression. Binding theta \\(=0.7\\) afterwards gives the same operator as inverting the bound circuit. Symbolic circuits can be composed, inverted, and controlled before binding, which is what lets one template serve a whole parameter sweep."}, {"title": "Reusable Quantum Programs — Exercise 9", "chapter": "Reusable Quantum Programs", "url": "05-reusable-quantum-programs.html#exercise-9", "text": "Try to package a circuit that contains a measurement with to_gate(), and then with to_instruction(). Explain the difference between a gate and an instruction, and what it means for inverting or controlling the packaged object. to_gate() raises a QiskitError, because a gate represents a unitary and a measurement is not one. to_instruction() succeeds: an instruction may include classical bits and non-unitary steps. Consequently a gate can be inverted and controlled, while such an instruction generally cannot. Packaging should preserve this distinction so that later code cannot ask for an inverse that does not exist."}, {"title": "Reusable Quantum Programs — Exercise 10", "chapter": "Reusable Quantum Programs", "url": "05-reusable-quantum-programs.html#exercise-10", "text": "A borrowed (dirty) qubit \\(b\\) may start in any unknown state and must be returned unchanged. Show that the sequence cx(x, b), cx(b, t), cx(x, b), cx(b, t) adds \\(x\\) to the target \\(t\\) for every value of \\(b\\), and leaves \\(b\\) unchanged. Verify it on all eight basis inputs. Why is this useful? Tracking values: \\(b\\to b\\oplus x\\); \\(t\\to t\\oplus b\\oplus x\\); \\(b\\to b\\); \\(t\\to t\\oplus x\\). The unknown \\(b\\) appears twice in the target and cancels, so the sequence implements \\(t\\mathrel{\\oplus}=x\\) and restores \\(b\\), on all eight inputs and, by linearity, on superpositions. Borrowed qubits let a circuit use idle wires as temporary workspace without requiring them to be clean, saving qubits in large constructions."}, {"title": "Circuit Interfaces", "chapter": "Reusable Quantum Programs", "url": "05-reusable-quantum-programs.html#section-5-1", "text": "5.1 Circuit Interfaces Suppose a computation needs the same \\(R_y\\) rotation in several places, at different angles. We could copy its lines each time, but every copy is a chance for a mistake. A component that can be reused needs two things: a way to produce it on demand, and a stated agreement about how it connects to the rest of a circuit. We take them in that order. 5.1.1 Python circuit factories Why not build the rotation circuit once and reuse that one object everywhere? Recall that two Python names can refer to one circuit. Suppose two experiments share the object and the first appends a measurement to it. The second experiment now contains that measurement too, although nobody added it there. Its result depends on what ran before. The cure is a function that builds a new circuit every time it is called. Such a function is called a circuit factory . In the companion, rotation(theta) creates a one-qubit circuit, applies \\(R_y(\\theta)\\) and returns it. The input name=\"rotation\" labels the circuit, so the label appears when it is drawn as a box. Because the caller receives the circuit rather than a result, the caller also decides where it is placed and when it is run. Problem 5.1. Why return a fresh circuit instead of a shared global one? Worked solution Appending gates mutates circuit objects. A shared object can accumulate instructions across calls and make experiments depend on call history. 5.1.2 Register contracts and composition A factory returns a correct circuit. What can still go wrong when we plug it into a larger one? Consider a component that computes the AND of two bits into a third qubit, using a Toffoli gate. A function from input bits to one output bit is called a Boolean predicate . The component is correct only if the larger circuit attaches its wires as intended: inputs on the first two wires, the result qubit, called the target , on the third. So the component needs a stated agreement, a contract , written beside the factory. For the AND component it reads: “wires 0 and 1 are inputs and are left unchanged; wire 2 is the target and is flipped exactly when both inputs are 1.” When we place the component with compose , its qubits argument must follow the contract. A component that is mathematically right but attached to the wrong wires is still a bug, and the bit-order convention makes such mistakes easy."}, {"title": "Reusable Operations", "chapter": "Reusable Quantum Programs", "url": "05-reusable-quantum-programs.html#section-5-2", "text": "5.2 Reusable Operations A factory gives us circuits. Algorithms ask more of a component: to act as one gate, to act only when a control qubit is 1, to run backwards, and to be evaluated at many angles. Each request has a trap. We take control first, because it reveals that a phase we called unobservable can matter, and then turn to inverses and symbolic angles. 5.2.1 Custom gates and controlled gates Many algorithms need “apply \\(U\\) only if the control qubit is 1.” Could we write a host-language if around the append? No: at construction time the control qubit has no value, and in superposition it never has one. We need a single unitary that acts as \\(I\\) on the target when the control is \\(\\ket0\\) and as \\(U\\) when it is \\(\\ket1\\), the controlled \\(U\\). Qiskit builds it in two steps. to_gate() packages a circuit as one gate object, and control(1) adds one control qubit. In qc.append(controlled, [0, 1]) the control comes first in the list, so qubit 0 controls and qubit 1 is the target. Control is not free. A controlled \\(R_y\\), for example, decomposes into two CNOTs and two one-qubit gates. There is a subtler trap. Suppose a component’s circuit implements \\(e^{i\\varphi}I\\), the identity up to a global phase. Alone, that phase changes no prediction. Is it still harmless when controlled? With the control on qubit 0, the controlled operation is \\[\\operatorname{diag}(1,\\,e^{i\\varphi},\\,1,\\,e^{i\\varphi})=I\\otimes P(\\varphi),\\] where \\(P(\\varphi)=\\operatorname{diag}(1,e^{i\\varphi})\\) is a phase gate on the control. If the control is in \\(\\ket+\\), it becomes \\((\\ket0+e^{i\\varphi}\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\), a different state. So a global phase of the base operation becomes a relative phase on the control. A component that may be controlled must keep its global phase, and Qiskit records it in the circuit’s global_phase attribute. 5.2.2 Inverses and parameter binding Suppose we need the rotation at 100 different angles. Must we build 100 circuits? Instead, we can build one circuit in which the angle is a symbol. Parameter(\"theta\") creates such a symbol, and passing it to the factory gives a circuit with \\(R_y(\\theta)\\) and \\(\\theta\\) left open. To fix a value we call assign_parameters({angle: 0.7}) . Its input is a dictionary from symbol to number, and it returns a copy of the circuit with the number in place, leaving the symbolic circuit unchanged. Running backwards is the other request. inverse() reverses the order of the gates and replaces each by its adjoint, as for any product of unitaries. A circuit that contains measurement or reset has no such inverse, as we saw for reset. The companion uses both. It binds the angle to 0.7, composes the bound circuit with its inverse, and checks that the product is the identity matrix. It then controls the bound rotation and draws the result. \"\"\"Runnable companion for Quantum Programming, Chapter 5.\"\"\"\nimport numpy as np\nfrom qiskit import QuantumCircuit\nfrom qiskit.circuit import Parameter\nfrom qiskit.quantum_info import Operator\n\nangle = Parameter(\"theta\")\ndef rotation(theta):\n    qc = QuantumCircuit(1, name=\"rotation\")\n    qc.ry(theta, 0)\n    return qc\n\ncomponent = rotation(angle)\nbound = component.assign_parameters({angle: 0.7})\nidentity = bound.compose(bound.inverse())\nassert np.allclose(Operator(identity).data, np.eye(2))\ncontrolled = bound.to_gate().control(1)\nqc = QuantumCircuit(2)\nqc.append(controlled, [0, 1])\nprint(qc.draw(output=\"text\")) Companion script: chapter_05.py . Problem 5.2. Does U followed by U† test the intended specification? Worked solution It checks reversibility and the implementation of the inverse, but a wrong U can also pass. Compare with independently predicted outputs or matrices. Problem 5.3. Can a symbolic circuit be sampled before all necessary parameters are bound? Worked solution Only if parameter values are supplied through a supported execution interface. Otherwise its numerical evolution is unspecified and execution should reject it."}, {"title": "Workspace Contracts", "chapter": "Reusable Quantum Programs", "url": "05-reusable-quantum-programs.html#section-5-3", "text": "5.3 Workspace Contracts Some components cannot be built from their inputs and target alone. Computing the AND of three bits with Toffoli gates, for instance, needs a qubit to hold an intermediate result. What must such an extra qubit contain when the component starts, and what must the component leave in it? We first state the starting requirement, then the cleanup requirement and how to test it. 5.3.1 Ancilla allocation Suppose we want \\(t\\oplus(x_0\\wedge x_1\\wedge x_2)\\) on a target \\(t\\). A Toffoli has only two controls, so we first compute \\(x_0\\wedge x_1\\) into an extra qubit \\(a\\), then apply a Toffoli controlled by \\(a\\) and \\(x_2\\) onto \\(t\\). An extra qubit used as workspace is called an ancilla . What must \\(a\\) start in? A Toffoli adds \\(x_0x_1\\) to \\(a\\), so \\(a\\) holds \\(x_0\\wedge x_1\\) only if it started at 0. If it started at 1, the target would receive the wrong value. So the contract must say “one clean ancilla in \\(\\ket0\\).” A qubit whose state is unknown, which must be handed back unchanged, is called a borrowed qubit. It is a different resource and needs a different construction. An ancilla is a qubit, not a temporary Python variable. When a Python name stops referring to it, nothing physical happens. Whatever correlation it has with the data remains. 5.3.2 Uncomputation and cleanup checks After the second Toffoli, \\(a\\) still holds \\(x_0\\wedge x_1\\). Why not leave it there? On a basis input nothing seems wrong. On a superposition \\(\\sum_x\\alpha_x\\ket x\\), however, the state becomes \\(\\sum_x\\alpha_x\\ket x\\ket{t\\oplus f(x)}\\ket{x_0x_1}\\). The ancilla now carries information about the inputs, so it is entangled with them. As with one half of a Bell pair, the inputs alone lose the off-diagonal entries that later interference needs. So we apply the first Toffoli again. It adds \\(x_0x_1\\) a second time and returns \\(a\\) to 0 for every input. Undoing a computation to clear workspace is called uncomputation . The full state then factors as \\(\\left(\\sum_x\\alpha_x\\ket x\\ket{t\\oplus f(x)}\\right)\\otimes\\ket0_a\\). How do we test that? Checking basis inputs one at a time shows that \\(a\\) returns to 0 for each, which is necessary. It does not show that the useful registers keep the right relative phases. A test on a superposition of inputs does, by comparing the whole state with the factored form above. Measuring the ancilla and always seeing 0 is useful evidence, but it says nothing about the target’s action. A phase-sensitive test of this kind is exactly what an oracle needs, and we build one when we implement oracles. Our components now have interfaces we can trust. Running them still gives us only counts or amplitudes. How does a program get from a circuit to the particular number, a probability or an average, that it actually needs? Sources and further study: IBM, QuantumCircuit API ."}, {"title": "Sampling and Estimation — Exercise 1", "chapter": "Sampling and Estimation", "url": "06-sampling-and-estimation.html#exercise-1", "text": "For ry(\\(2\\pi/3\\))\\(\\ket0\\), predict \\(p(1)\\) and \\(\\langle Z\\rangle\\), and check that they satisfy \\(\\langle Z\\rangle=1-2p(1)\\). \\(p(1)=\\sin^2(\\pi/3)=0.75\\) and \\(\\langle Z\\rangle=\\cos(2\\pi/3)=-0.5=1-2(0.75)\\). The Sampler would report counts near \\(1024/3072\\) for \\(4096\\) shots; the Estimator reports the mean \\(-0.5\\). They are two views of the same distribution."}, {"title": "Sampling and Estimation — Exercise 2", "chapter": "Sampling and Estimation", "url": "06-sampling-and-estimation.html#exercise-2", "text": "A sampled estimate gives \\(\\langle Z\\rangle\\approx0.2\\) from \\(1000\\) shots. Find its standard error, and the shots needed to reach a standard error of \\(0.01\\). Standard error \\(\\sqrt{0.96/1000}\\approx0.031\\). Reaching \\(0.01\\) needs \\(R=0.96/10^{-4}=9600\\) shots: about ten times more for three times the precision, the familiar \\(1/\\sqrt R\\) scaling."}, {"title": "Sampling and Estimation — Exercise 3", "chapter": "Sampling and Estimation", "url": "06-sampling-and-estimation.html#exercise-3", "text": "A user passes a circuit that ends in measure_all to StatevectorEstimator together with the observable Z. What happens, and what should they pass instead? The Estimator raises a QiskitError (“Cannot apply instruction with classical bits: measure”). Expectation values are defined for the state before measurement; the Estimator handles any basis changes and sampling internally. Pass the circuit without measurements, and keep a separate measured copy for the Sampler."}, {"title": "Sampling and Estimation — Exercise 4", "chapter": "Sampling and Estimation", "url": "06-sampling-and-estimation.html#exercise-4", "text": "One Estimator PUB combines the parameterized ry circuit with observables arranged as [[Z], [X]] and parameter values [[0], [\\(\\pi/2\\)], [\\(\\pi\\)]]. (a) Predict the shape of data.evs. (b) Predict its entries. (c) Why is checking the shape part of a correct program? (a) Broadcasting the observables array of shape \\((2,1)\\) against three parameter sets gives shape \\((2,3)\\). (b) The Z row is \\(\\cos\\theta=(1,0,-1)\\) and the X row is \\(\\sin\\theta=(0,1,0)\\). (c) A different arrangement, say observables of shape \\((2,)\\), would fail to broadcast against three parameter sets, or would pair them differently. Asserting the expected shape catches workload mistakes that numerical checks on one entry would miss."}, {"title": "Sampling and Estimation — Exercise 5", "chapter": "Sampling and Estimation", "url": "06-sampling-and-estimation.html#exercise-5", "text": "A two-qubit state has qubit 0 in ry(\\(\\pi/3\\))\\(\\ket0\\) and qubit 1 in \\(\\ket0\\). The measurement maps qubit 0 to classical bit 1 and qubit 1 to classical bit 0. (a) Estimate \\(\\langle IZ\\rangle\\) (Z on qubit 0) from the counts, decoding the map correctly. (b) What would a student get by treating the rightmost count bit as qubit 0? (c) Does the mapping matter for \\(\\langle ZZ\\rangle\\)? (a) Qubit 0’s value is in classical bit 1, the left character. Averaging \\(+1\\) for 0 and \\(-1\\) for 1 over that character gives about \\(0.5=\\cos(\\pi/3)\\). (b) The rightmost character holds qubit 1, which is always \\(0\\), so the student would report \\(1\\). (c) ZZ depends only on whether the two bits are equal, which is unchanged by swapping them, so it is immune to this mistake; single-qubit observables are not."}, {"title": "Sampling and Estimation — Exercise 6", "chapter": "Sampling and Estimation", "url": "06-sampling-and-estimation.html#exercise-6", "text": "An experiment must estimate \\(\\langle Z\\rangle+\\langle X\\rangle\\) for ry(\\(\\pi/3\\))\\(\\ket0\\) with \\(10\\,000\\) total shots, split between a Z-basis and an X-basis circuit. (a) Find the single-shot variances. (b) Compare the variance of the estimate for an equal split and for the allocation \\(R_j\\propto\\sigma_j\\). (c) Is the improvement worth the bookkeeping here? (a) \\(\\sigma_Z^2=1-0.25=0.75\\) and \\(\\sigma_X^2=1-0.75=0.25\\). (b) Equal split: \\(0.75/5000+0.25/5000=2.0\\times10^{-4}\\). Optimal: \\(R_Z\\approx6340\\), \\(R_X\\approx3660\\), giving \\((0.866+0.5)^2/10^4\\approx1.87\\times10^{-4}\\). (c) Only a \\(7\\%\\) reduction here. The gain becomes large when coefficients or variances differ strongly, as for Hamiltonians with many terms; the variances must themselves be estimated first."}, {"title": "Sampling and Estimation — Exercise 7", "chapter": "Sampling and Estimation", "url": "06-sampling-and-estimation.html#exercise-7", "text": "Estimate a small probability: a circuit outputs 1 with probability \\(p=0.002\\). (a) With \\(1000\\) shots, what is the expected count and the relative standard error of \\(\\hat p\\)? (b) How many shots give a \\(10\\%\\) relative error? (c) If zero ones are observed in \\(1000\\) shots, what upper bound on \\(p\\) holds at about \\(95\\%\\) confidence? (a) Expected count \\(2\\), relative error \\(\\sqrt{0.998/(0.002\\times1000)}\\approx0.71\\): the estimate is barely meaningful. (b) \\(0.1^2=0.998/(0.002R)\\) gives \\(R\\approx5\\times10^4\\). (c) About \\(3/1000=0.003\\). Small entries of a normalized count table carry large relative uncertainty, so rare outcomes need either many more shots or an estimation method designed for them."}, {"title": "Sampling and Estimation — Exercise 8", "chapter": "Sampling and Estimation", "url": "06-sampling-and-estimation.html#exercise-8", "text": "A student argues: “Doubling the shots halves the uncertainty, so going from \\(1000\\) to \\(2000\\) shots takes my standard error from \\(0.03\\) to \\(0.015\\).” Correct the claim and give the shots that actually halve it. Standard error scales as \\(1/\\sqrt R\\), so doubling the shots reduces it by \\(\\sqrt2\\), from \\(0.03\\) to about \\(0.021\\). Halving it requires four times the shots, \\(4000\\). Every extra digit of precision costs a factor of one hundred in shots, which is why measurement budgets dominate many estimation workloads."}, {"title": "Sampling and Estimation — Exercise 9", "chapter": "Sampling and Estimation", "url": "06-sampling-and-estimation.html#exercise-9", "text": "Compare two ways of running a \\(100\\)-point parameter sweep of \\(\\langle Z\\rangle\\) for ry(theta): one PUB with a \\((100,1)\\) array of values, and \\(100\\) separate PUBs with one value each. Show that the expectation values agree, and explain why the single-PUB form is preferable. The two runs return the same \\(100\\) values of \\(\\cos\\theta\\). A single PUB states the experiment’s structure explicitly (one circuit, one observable, a value array), lets the primitive reuse the circuit and compilation, and returns one array whose order matches the input array, reducing bookkeeping errors. Separate PUBs repeat overhead and scatter results across many objects; on remote services they may also be scheduled and billed separately."}, {"title": "Sampling and Estimation — Exercise 10", "chapter": "Sampling and Estimation", "url": "06-sampling-and-estimation.html#exercise-10", "text": "Write a function that converts Sampler counts for two measured qubits into an estimate of any two-qubit diagonal Pauli observable (II, IZ, ZI, or ZZ), given the label and assuming the default measure_all mapping. Test it on the Bell state, where \\(\\langle ZZ\\rangle=1\\) and \\(\\langle ZI\\rangle=\\langle IZ\\rangle=0\\). Each shot contributes the product of \\(\\pm1\\) factors from the qubits where the label has Z. Since both labels and count strings put qubit 0 on the right, character \\(j\\) of the label pairs with character \\(j\\) of the bit string. On the Bell state the estimates are \\(1\\) for ZZ and close to \\(0\\) for ZI and IZ. Encoding the ordering rule once in a tested helper is safer than reimplementing it in every analysis."}, {"title": "Execution Primitives", "chapter": "Sampling and Estimation", "url": "06-sampling-and-estimation.html#section-6-1", "text": "6.1 Execution Primitives The rotation prepares \\(R_y(\\theta)\\ket0=\\cos(\\theta/2)\\ket0+\\sin(\\theta/2)\\ket1\\). Two natural questions about it have different answers. The probability of measuring one is \\(p(1)=\\sin^2(\\theta/2)\\). The average of \\(Z\\) is \\(\\cos^2(\\theta/2)-\\sin^2(\\theta/2)=\\cos\\theta\\). Which interface should answer each question, and where in its result do we find the number? We take the interfaces first and their results second. 6.1.1 V2 Sampler and Estimator We already have a sampler, and from its counts we could compute \\(\\langle Z\\rangle\\) ourselves. Why would we want a separate interface for averages? For one qubit and one observable, we would not. But an observable such as \\(-ZZ-XX\\) needs measurements in more than one basis, and larger observables need many. Choosing those bases and combining their results is work that the software, or a remote service, can do for us, and can do with techniques of its own. So Qiskit offers two primitives , the standard interfaces we met as reference primitives. A Sampler takes measured circuits and returns their outcomes. An Estimator takes circuits without measurements, together with observables, and returns expectation values. Their current versions are called V2. Both accept many requests in one call. Each request is written as a tuple , a fixed-order group of values in parentheses, such as (qc, SparsePauliOp(\"Z\"), values) . For an Estimator the order is circuit, observable, parameter values. Qiskit calls one such request a primitive unified bloc , or PUB. A PUB is a grouping of inputs, nothing more: it is not a gate, and it does not promise that its parts run together on a device. The companion uses the local StatevectorSampler and StatevectorEstimator , so no account is involved. 6.1.2 Local implementations and result structures Once a run finishes, where is our number? The result holds one entry per PUB, in the order we sent them, so result[0] belongs to the first PUB. For an Estimator, result[0].data.evs is an array of expectation values, one for each set of parameter values in that PUB. For a Sampler, result[0].data is organized by classical register name. We have always used measure_all() , which creates a register named meas , so we read data.meas . A circuit with its own register named c would be read as data.c . So an access expression copied from another program can fail, or read the wrong register, unless we check the register names first. Problem 6.1. At θ=π/3, predict p(1) and the Z mean. Worked solution They are 1/4 and 1/2. A result near zero would suggest a wrong angle, observable, or parameter mapping."}, {"title": "Statistical Readout", "chapter": "Sampling and Estimation", "url": "06-sampling-and-estimation.html#section-6-2", "text": "6.2 Statistical Readout The local Estimator computes \\(\\cos\\theta\\) exactly. A device cannot: it repeats the circuit a finite number of times and averages. How far from the true value should we expect such an estimate to land? And when we only have bit strings, how do we turn them into an average ourselves? We derive the uncertainty first, then the decoding. 6.2.1 Shots, precision, and uncertainty Suppose we run the circuit \\(R\\) times at \\(\\theta=\\pi/2\\), where \\(\\langle Z\\rangle=0\\). How close to 0 will our estimate be? Each shot gives a bit, and we record \\(s=+1\\) for outcome 0 and \\(s=-1\\) for outcome 1, the two eigenvalues of \\(Z\\). The mean of \\(s\\) is \\(p(0)-p(1)\\), and since \\(p(0)=1-p(1)\\), \\[\\langle Z\\rangle=1-2p(1).\\] Because \\(s^2=1\\), the variance of one shot is \\(\\mathbb E[s^2]-\\langle Z\\rangle^2=1-\\langle Z\\rangle^2\\). As with the coin, averaging \\(R\\) independent shots divides the variance by \\(R\\): \\[\\operatorname{Var}\\bar s=\\frac{1-\\langle Z\\rangle^2}{R},\\qquad \\operatorname{Var}\\hat p(1)=\\frac{p(1)\\bigl(1-p(1)\\bigr)}{R}.\\] The two agree: \\(\\bar s=1-2\\hat p(1)\\), so its variance is four times that of \\(\\hat p(1)\\), and \\(4p(1-p)=1-(1-2p)^2\\). At \\(\\theta=\\pi/2\\) with \\(R=4096\\), the standard deviation of \\(\\bar s\\) is \\(1/64\\approx0.016\\). This standard deviation of an estimate is called its standard error . Doubling the shots divides it by \\(\\sqrt2\\), so each extra digit of accuracy costs a hundred times more shots. An Estimator lets us ask for a target standard error, its precision . StatevectorEstimator uses precision 0 by default and then returns the exact value. With a positive precision it adds random noise of about that size, to imitate a finite-shot estimate. Either way it is not a model of a noisy device. 6.2.2 Bit strings and classical postprocessing Now suppose we have two-qubit counts and want \\(\\langle ZZ\\rangle\\). How do we compute it? The Z outcome of qubit \\(j\\) is \\(+1\\) when its bit is 0 and \\(-1\\) when it is 1. \\(ZZ\\) is the product of the two, so it is \\(+1\\) when the bits are equal and \\(-1\\) when they differ. Hence \\[\\widehat{\\langle ZZ\\rangle}=\\frac{N_{\\text{equal}}-N_{\\text{unequal}}}{R}.\\] For \\(ZI\\), by contrast, only the left character, qubit 1, matters. So we must decode each string with the measurement mapping before computing. A mapping that swaps the bits leaves \\(ZZ\\) unchanged but exchanges \\(ZI\\) and \\(IZ\\). What about small entries of a count table? Dividing counts by \\(R\\) gives an empirical distribution . An outcome with probability \\(p\\) has standard error \\(\\sqrt{p(1-p)/R}\\), which relative to \\(p\\) is about \\(1/\\sqrt{pR}\\). At \\(p=0.001\\) and \\(R=4096\\) we expect about 4 counts, and the relative uncertainty is about 50%. Small entries are the least reliable. Problem 6.2. How does doubling shots change a standard error? Worked solution It reduces the standard error by √2, assuming independent identically distributed shots. It does not remove systematic bias."}, {"title": "Repeated Workloads", "chapter": "Sampling and Estimation", "url": "06-sampling-and-estimation.html#section-6-3", "text": "6.3 Repeated Workloads We usually want \\(\\langle Z\\rangle\\) not at one angle but at many. Must we build and send one circuit per angle? A symbolic parameter lets one circuit serve every angle. What remains is to describe the list of angles so that the primitive runs the experiments we mean, and to keep their results in order. 6.3.1 Parameter sweeps How do we ask for \\(\\theta=0,\\pi/2,\\pi\\) in one PUB? The companion writes values = [[0], [np.pi / 2], [np.pi]] , a list of lists. Each inner list is one row , holding one value for each parameter of the circuit. Our circuit has one parameter, so each row has one entry, and three rows ask for three experiments. The array has shape \\(3\\times1\\), and the predicted results are \\(\\cos\\theta=1,0,-1\\). Shapes decide which experiments run, so a mistake can change the workload without any warning. Suppose we also want \\(\\langle X\\rangle\\) at the same three angles. Passing the two observables as a flat list, of shape 2, beside three rows, of shape 3, raises an error, because the two shapes cannot be matched. Passing them as [[Z], [X]] , of shape \\(2\\times1\\), gives a \\(2\\times3\\) result: every observable at every angle. The Estimator combines the shapes by NumPy’s broadcasting rule, which stretches a dimension of length 1 to match the other array. So we check the shape of evs before trusting the order of its entries. 6.3.2 Batching inputs and interpreting results When we have several PUBs, why send them in one call rather than one at a time? Each call has overhead, and on a remote service each job may wait in a queue. Sending several PUBs in one call, called batching , saves that overhead. It does not reduce the work: every circuit, parameter row, observable and shot still costs what it costs. Since results come back in PUB order, we keep the parameter order and the result order together in the saved record. The companion keeps its two sources of output apart. First the Estimator computes the exact three-point sweep and checks it with np.allclose . The middle value prints as about \\(2\\times10^{-16}\\) rather than 0, a rounding error of the kind tolerances exist for. Then the program binds \\(\\theta=\\pi/2\\), adds measurements and samples 4096 shots, so the counts should be close to an even split. \"\"\"Runnable companion for Quantum Programming, Chapter 6.\"\"\"\nimport numpy as np\nfrom qiskit import QuantumCircuit\nfrom qiskit.circuit import Parameter\nfrom qiskit.primitives import StatevectorSampler, StatevectorEstimator\nfrom qiskit.quantum_info import SparsePauliOp\n\ntheta = Parameter(\"theta\")\nqc = QuantumCircuit(1)\nqc.ry(theta, 0)\nvalues = [[0], [np.pi / 2], [np.pi]]\nresult = StatevectorEstimator().run([(qc, SparsePauliOp(\"Z\"), values)]).result()\nassert np.allclose(result[0].data.evs, [1, 0, -1])\nmeasured = qc.assign_parameters({theta: np.pi / 2})\nmeasured.measure_all()\ncounts = StatevectorSampler(seed=9).run([measured], shots=4096).result()[0].data.meas.get_counts()\nprint(\"Exact means:\", result[0].data.evs, \"sampled counts:\", counts) Companion script: chapter_06.py . With seed 9 the sampled counts were 2051 zeros and 2045 ones, which gives \\(\\bar s=6/4096\\approx0.0015\\), well within one standard error of the exact value 0. Problem 6.3. Why keep a circuit without measurements for the Estimator? Worked solution It represents the prepared state whose observable is requested. The estimation implementation chooses its measurement procedure; terminal measurements in the input can conflict with that contract. Every circuit so far acted on inputs we wrote in by hand. Many algorithms instead assume an oracle, a circuit that evaluates a function on whatever input it receives. How do we build one, rather than assume it exists? Sources and further study: IBM, StatevectorEstimator ; IBM, StatevectorSampler ."}, {"title": "Oracles and Arithmetic — Exercise 1", "chapter": "Oracles and Arithmetic", "url": "07-oracles-and-arithmetic.html#exercise-1", "text": "For the oracle \\(f(a,b,c)=ab\\oplus c\\) (qubits 0, 1, 2 hold \\(a,b,c\\); qubit 3 is the target), which basis state does the input \\(a=b=c=1\\) with target \\(0\\) map to? Give its index. \\(f(1,1,1)=1\\oplus1=0\\), so the target stays \\(0\\) and the state is unchanged: index \\(7\\) maps to index \\(7\\). The Toffoli and the CNOT each flip the target, and the two flips cancel. Exhaustive checks over all sixteen columns catch mistakes in exactly these cancelling cases."}, {"title": "Oracles and Arithmetic — Exercise 2", "chapter": "Oracles and Arithmetic", "url": "07-oracles-and-arithmetic.html#exercise-2", "text": "The two-bit incrementer applies cx(0, 1) and then x(0). Tabulate its action on the four inputs \\(0,1,2,3\\). What happens to \\(3\\), and why does that keep the circuit reversible? \\(0\\to1\\), \\(1\\to2\\), \\(2\\to3\\), and \\(3\\to0\\). The overflow wraps around: this is addition modulo \\(4\\), a permutation of the four labels, and therefore reversible. Producing \\(4\\) would need a third bit; the width is part of the function’s definition."}, {"title": "Oracles and Arithmetic — Exercise 3", "chapter": "Oracles and Arithmetic", "url": "07-oracles-and-arithmetic.html#exercise-3", "text": "The oracle uses one Toffoli and one CNOT. Transpile it to the basis cx, rz, sx, x with all-to-all connectivity, and then on a line of four qubits. Report the CNOT counts. With all-to-all connectivity the compiled oracle has \\(7\\) CNOTs (six for the Toffoli, one for the CNOT). On a line it needs more, about \\(10\\) in this run, because qubits 0, 1, and 3 are not all adjacent and swaps are inserted. Report both the logical count and the compiled count for a stated target."}, {"title": "Oracles and Arithmetic — Exercise 4", "chapter": "Oracles and Arithmetic", "url": "07-oracles-and-arithmetic.html#exercise-4", "text": "A buggy oracle appends cz(3, 0) after the correct gates. (a) Show that its output probabilities match the correct oracle on every basis input. (b) Show that the full operator comparison fails. (c) What kind of error does this represent, and which test in the chapter catches it? (a) CZ changes only signs, so every basis input produces the same output label with the same probability as the correct oracle. (b) The operators differ: the bug multiplies outputs with \\(a=1\\) and target \\(1\\) by \\(-1\\). (c) It is a phase error. When the oracle is used coherently, as in a phase-kickback algorithm, it corrupts interference. The chapter’s coherent sign test on a uniform superposition with target \\(\\ket-\\), and the full column comparison including phases, both catch it; histograms do not."}, {"title": "Oracles and Arithmetic — Exercise 5", "chapter": "Oracles and Arithmetic", "url": "07-oracles-and-arithmetic.html#exercise-5", "text": "Build a two-bit equality test: qubits 0, 1 hold \\(a\\), qubits 2, 3 hold \\(b\\), and qubit 4 receives \\([a=b]\\), with \\(b\\) restored. Use CNOTs to compute \\(a\\oplus b\\) into \\(b\\), X gates and a Toffoli to set the flag when both XOR bits are \\(0\\), and then uncompute. Verify all \\(16\\) inputs. Compute \\(a\\oplus b\\) into qubits 2 and 3 with two CNOTs, flip both with X, and apply a Toffoli from qubits 2, 3 to the flag, which fires exactly when both XOR bits were \\(0\\). Then undo the X gates and the CNOTs in reverse order, restoring \\(b\\). The check below confirms that every input maps to itself with the correct flag. The comparison reuses \\(b\\) as workspace instead of a separate scratch register, at the cost of a careful uncompute."}, {"title": "Oracles and Arithmetic — Exercise 6", "chapter": "Oracles and Arithmetic", "url": "07-oracles-and-arithmetic.html#exercise-6", "text": "For \\(f(a,b,c)=ab\\oplus c\\), the phase \\((-1)^{f}\\) factors as \\((-1)^{ab}(-1)^{c}\\). (a) Implement the phase oracle with cz(0, 1) and z(2), using no target qubit. (b) Verify that, on the uniform input with the target in \\(\\ket-\\), it produces the same state as the bit oracle. (c) What does this save? (a) cz(0, 1) multiplies by \\((-1)^{ab}\\) and z(2) by \\((-1)^c\\), together \\((-1)^{f}\\). (b) Applying them to the uniform input, with the unused target prepared in \\(\\ket-\\), gives the same state as the bit oracle with target \\(\\ket-\\). (c) It removes the Toffoli entirely (six CNOTs after compilation) and the target qubit. When a function is an XOR of simple terms, writing the phase directly is far cheaper than computing the bit and kicking it back."}, {"title": "Oracles and Arithmetic — Exercise 7", "chapter": "Oracles and Arithmetic", "url": "07-oracles-and-arithmetic.html#exercise-7", "text": "An oracle must apply the phase \\(e^{2\\pi i\\,0.3}\\) using a fixed-point register. (a) With \\(3\\) fractional bits, which representable value is closest to \\(0.3\\), and what is the phase error? (b) Repeat with \\(5\\) bits. (c) How many bits keep the phase error below \\(10^{-3}\\) rad? (a) \\(0.3\\times8=2.4\\), so the nearest value is \\(2/8=0.25\\), an error of \\(0.05\\) and a phase error of \\(0.31\\) rad. (b) \\(0.3\\times32=9.6\\), nearest \\(10/32=0.3125\\): error \\(0.0125\\), phase error \\(0.079\\) rad. (c) Rounding error is at most \\(2^{-m-1}\\), so the phase error is at most \\(\\pi2^{-m}\\); requiring \\(\\le10^{-3}\\) gives \\(m\\ge12\\). Each bit halves the error but adds a wire and its arithmetic."}, {"title": "Oracles and Arithmetic — Exercise 8", "chapter": "Oracles and Arithmetic", "url": "07-oracles-and-arithmetic.html#exercise-8", "text": "A student uses the two-bit incrementer to compute \\(3+1\\) and reports a bug when the output is \\(0\\). Is it a bug? What should the program have specified, and how would you obtain \\(4\\)? It is not a bug in the circuit: the two-bit incrementer computes \\(x+1\\bmod4\\), and \\(3+1\\equiv0\\). The bug is a mismatch between the specification and the intended use. To obtain \\(4\\), use a three-bit register (and a three-bit incrementer) or an explicit carry output; every arithmetic component must state its width and its overflow behavior."}, {"title": "Oracles and Arithmetic — Exercise 9", "chapter": "Oracles and Arithmetic", "url": "07-oracles-and-arithmetic.html#exercise-9", "text": "Build a three-bit incrementer using a multi-controlled X: first flip bit 2 if bits 0 and 1 are both \\(1\\), then flip bit 1 if bit 0 is \\(1\\), then flip bit 0. Verify it on all eight inputs, and explain why the order must go from the high bit down. Each bit should flip exactly when all lower bits are \\(1\\) in the original input. Processing from the high bit down ensures every controlled flip reads the lower bits before they are modified; the reverse order would read already-incremented bits. The check confirms \\(v\\to v+1\\bmod8\\) for all inputs. Larger widths need multi-controlled X gates with many controls, whose decomposition cost grows with \\(n\\)."}, {"title": "Oracles and Arithmetic — Exercise 10", "chapter": "Oracles and Arithmetic", "url": "07-oracles-and-arithmetic.html#exercise-10", "text": "An oracle is specified only by its truth table on \\(n\\) inputs. (a) How many basis columns must an exhaustive operator check compare, including both target values? (b) For which \\(n\\) does a dense Operator check become impractical? (c) What alternative checks scale better, and what do they give up? (a) \\(2^{n+1}\\) columns (all inputs, both target values), each of length \\(2^{n+1}\\). (b) Around \\(n=12\\) to \\(14\\), the dense matrix has \\(4^{n+1}\\) entries, hundreds of millions to billions, too large for routine testing. (c) Check each basis input with a statevector or a classical reversible simulation (\\(2^{n+1}\\) runs, each cheap), plus a few coherent superposition tests for phases; or verify the construction compositionally, component by component. These scale further but no longer examine every matrix entry at once, so phase checks must be designed deliberately."}, {"title": "Reversible Predicates", "chapter": "Oracles and Arithmetic", "url": "07-oracles-and-arithmetic.html#section-7-1", "text": "7.1 Reversible Predicates Our predicate takes three bits and returns one. To evaluate it with unitary gates, we cannot simply replace the inputs with the output, because that would discard information. Instead we keep the inputs and add the answer to a fourth qubit. We first choose the gates, then state and test the contract for every value of that fourth qubit. 7.1.1 Boolean evaluation Which gates add \\(ab\\oplus c\\) to a target qubit? We place \\(a,b,c\\) on qubits 0, 1 and 2 and the target on qubit 3. The function is an XOR of two terms, and flipping a bit twice is the same as flipping it by the XOR of the two reasons. So we can handle each term with its own gate. The term \\(ab\\) is an AND, and a Toffoli controlled by qubits 0 and 1 flips the target exactly when \\(ab=1\\). The term \\(c\\) needs a CNOT from qubit 2. Together they send the target \\(t\\) to \\(t\\oplus ab\\oplus c\\) and leave the inputs untouched. This predicate needs no ancilla. A larger predicate would. For \\((a\\wedge b)\\wedge(c\\vee d)\\), say, the intermediate values need ancillas, and we use the pattern we met for the three-input AND. We compute the intermediate values into clean ancillas, copy the final value into the target with a CNOT, and then reverse the computing gates so the ancillas return to \\(\\ket0\\). This pattern is called compute–copy–uncompute . 7.1.2 Answer registers and retained inputs Suppose we test the circuit on the eight inputs with target 0, and the target always ends as \\(f\\). Is the oracle correct? Not necessarily. A circuit that first resets the target and then computes \\(f\\) passes every one of those tests. On target 1, though, it returns \\(f\\) instead of \\(1\\oplus f\\), and since it contains a reset it is not even unitary. The answer operation of an oracle is XOR, not assignment, so the contract must hold for target 0 and target 1. So we check all \\(2^4=16\\) basis inputs. Column \\(k\\) of the circuit’s matrix is its output for basis input \\(k\\), and the expected output is the same basis state with the target bit flipped when \\(f=1\\). How does a program compute that? An integer from 0 to 15 holds the four qubit values as its binary digits, with qubit 0 as the least significant digit, exactly as in our bit-order convention. Python’s bitwise operators act on those digits. To read qubit \\(k\\), we shift the digits right by \\(k\\) places with index >> k , which drops the lowest \\(k\\) digits. Then & 1 keeps only the lowest remaining digit, so (index >> k) & 1 is the value of qubit \\(k\\). To write the target, we need the opposite shift: f << 3 moves \\(f\\) to digit 3, giving 8 when \\(f=1\\) and 0 otherwise. Finally ^ is XOR applied digit by digit. So index ^ (f << 3) flips the target’s digit exactly when \\(f=1\\). In the companion, for index in range(16): repeats its indented body for each index from 0 to 15. The line a, b, c = … assigns three names at once from three values. matrix[:, index] selects column index , where the colon means “all rows,” and np.eye(16)[:, expected] is the basis vector we expect. Why compare whole columns rather than probabilities? A linear operator is fixed by what it does to the basis states, including the phases of the outputs. An extra Z on one qubit changes no basis probability, yet it changes the column’s sign, and the column test catches it. Problem 7.1. What happens to an answer target initially one when f=1? Worked solution It becomes zero. The answer operation is XOR, not assignment."}, {"title": "Arithmetic Circuits", "chapter": "Oracles and Arithmetic", "url": "07-oracles-and-arithmetic.html#section-7-2", "text": "7.2 Arithmetic Circuits Our predicate used only AND and XOR. Algorithms often need oracles that do arithmetic, adding or comparing integers stored in qubits. An integer on qubits has a fixed number of digits, and arithmetic usually needs intermediate values. So we must decide what happens when a result does not fit, and where the intermediate values live. We start with addition and comparison, then turn to width and workspace. 7.2.1 Addition and comparison How do we add 1 to a two-bit number \\(x=x_0+2x_1\\) with reversible gates? In ordinary binary addition the low digit always flips, and the high digit flips exactly when the low digit was 1, because that produces a carry. The order of gates matters: the carry depends on the low digit’s old value. So we first apply a CNOT from qubit 0 to qubit 1, and only then an X on qubit 0. Checking the four inputs: \\(0\\to1\\), \\(1\\to2\\), \\(2\\to3\\), and \\(3\\to0\\). The last case shows that the circuit computes addition modulo 4. It has to: it is a permutation of four basis states, so it cannot produce a fifth. If we want to keep the overflow, we must add a qubit for it. Either choice is legitimate, but the program must make it explicitly. The bit width is part of the function. Comparison follows the compute–copy–uncompute pattern. To decide whether \\(a<b\\), we compute \\(a-b\\) and look at whether the subtraction needs a borrow from beyond the top digit. We copy that borrow flag into the target, then reverse the subtraction to restore the workspace. 7.2.2 Precision and workspace requirements What if the numbers are not integers? A common choice is fixed point : store an integer \\(m\\) and read it as \\(m/2^k\\). With 4 bits and \\(k=2\\), the representable values are \\(0,0.25,0.5,\\dots,3.75\\). Adding a bit can extend the range or refine the spacing, so changing the width changes both range and precision. Arithmetic also needs space. Carries, comparison flags and partial products each need wires, and each must be uncomputed. If we report only the final gate that writes the answer, we hide most of the oracle’s cost. The same holds when an oracle looks up values in a finite table. Loading the table, or reading from it, needs a circuit of its own, with its own cost. Problem 7.2. Is a four-bit adder an implementation of arbitrary-precision integer addition? Worked solution No. Its range and overflow behavior are fixed. A scalable implementation specifies a family parameterized by width and gives its resource growth."}, {"title": "Phase Oracles", "chapter": "Oracles and Arithmetic", "url": "07-oracles-and-arithmetic.html#section-7-3", "text": "7.3 Phase Oracles Search and many other algorithms do not want \\(f(x)\\) written in a target. They want each input’s amplitude multiplied by \\((-1)^{f(x)}\\). Can our bit oracle provide that sign without a new circuit? If it can, we must test the signs, and a histogram cannot see a sign. We derive the phase form first, then test it and count its cost. 7.3.1 Compute, phase, and uncompute Suppose the target starts in \\(\\ket-=(\\ket0-\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\) instead of a basis state. The bit oracle sends \\(\\ket x\\ket0\\) to \\(\\ket x\\ket{f(x)}\\) and \\(\\ket x\\ket1\\) to \\(\\ket x\\ket{1\\oplus f(x)}\\), so \\[U_f\\ket x\\ket-=\\ket x\\,\\frac{\\ket{f(x)}-\\ket{1\\oplus f(x)}}{\\sqrt2}=(-1)^{f(x)}\\ket x\\ket-.\\] When \\(f(x)=0\\) nothing changes, and when \\(f(x)=1\\) the two terms swap, which flips the sign. The target is unchanged, and the sign has moved onto the input. This is called phase kickback , and \\(U_f\\) used this way is a phase oracle . In a larger predicate, the ancillas must be uncomputed before this works. A retained ancilla would carry \\(f\\)-dependent values, and the input state would not factor away from the workspace. To test it, the companion prepares all eight inputs with equal amplitude, using H on qubits 0, 1 and 2. It prepares \\(\\ket-\\) on the target with X and then H. Each input amplitude is \\(1/\\sqrt8\\) and each target amplitude is \\(\\pm1/\\sqrt2\\), so after the oracle the amplitude of basis index \\(k\\) should be \\[\\frac{(-1)^{f(a,b,c)\\oplus t}}{4},\\] where \\(t\\) is the target bit of \\(k\\). The code builds this list of 16 numbers. The expression [(index >> k) & 1 for k in range(4)] is a list comprehension : it makes a list from the expression before for , evaluated at each \\(k\\). Here it gives the four bits of index . Starting from the empty list [] , the method append adds one expected amplitude per index. 7.3.2 Superposition tests and resource counts Why insist on comparing amplitudes? In this state every one of the 16 amplitudes has magnitude \\(1/4\\). So every basis probability is \\(1/16\\), whatever \\(f\\) is. A histogram of measured counts would look the same for the correct oracle, for a wrong one, and for no oracle at all. Only the signs carry \\(f\\), and the companion compares all 16 complex amplitudes at once. That one test checks every sign and also checks that the target ends in \\(\\ket-\\), unentangled from the inputs. What does the oracle cost? Logically, one Toffoli and one CNOT, plus the preparation and any readout. A Toffoli is not native on most devices. Compiled to CNOTs and one-qubit gates, it takes 6 CNOTs. So a resource report should give both the logical count and the compiled count. Because its contract is simple, inputs kept and target XORed, the oracle can serve as a component in search, in phase encoding, or in a larger arithmetic circuit. \"\"\"Runnable companion for Quantum Programming, Chapter 7.\"\"\"\nimport numpy as np\nfrom qiskit import QuantumCircuit\nfrom qiskit.quantum_info import Operator, Statevector\n\n# f(a,b,c) = ab XOR c; q0=a, q1=b, q2=c, q3=target.\nqc = QuantumCircuit(4)\nqc.ccx(0, 1, 3)\nqc.cx(2, 3)\nmatrix = Operator(qc).data\nfor index in range(16):\n    a, b, c = index & 1, (index >> 1) & 1, (index >> 2) & 1\n    expected = index ^ (((a & b) ^ c) << 3)\n    assert np.allclose(matrix[:, index], np.eye(16)[:, expected])\n# Target minus makes the same oracle a phase oracle.\nprep = QuantumCircuit(4)\nprep.h([0, 1, 2])\nprep.x(3)\nprep.h(3)\nstate = Statevector.from_instruction(prep.compose(qc))\nexpected = []\nfor index in range(16):\n    a, b, c, target = [(index >> k) & 1 for k in range(4)]\n    expected.append((-1)**((a & b) ^ c ^ target) / 4)\nassert np.allclose(state.data, expected)\nprint(\"All basis columns and coherent signs verified\") Companion script: chapter_07.py . Problem 7.3. Why test a target minus state? Worked solution It turns function values into relative phases and exposes phase or cleanup errors that may be invisible on measured basis inputs. Phase-oracle readout. U_f is the reversible bit oracle on the input register and the minus target, not an elementary gate. The final input measurement reads the encoded pattern. With an oracle we can build and verify, the algorithms we studied abstractly become possible to program. What does each of them add to the code beyond its oracle? Sources and further study: IBM, circuit API ."}, {"title": "Algorithm Implementation — Exercise 1", "chapter": "Algorithm Implementation", "url": "08-algorithm-implementation.html#exercise-1", "text": "Verify that the inverse of the chapter’s positive-sign QFT matches \\(e^{-2\\pi ixy/4}/2\\). Why is the sign convention worth an explicit test? qft.inverse() matches the matrix with \\(e^{-2\\pi ixy/4}/2\\). Libraries and papers differ on which sign is “the” QFT, and phase estimation needs the inverse of whichever transform produced its phase ramp. A wrong sign returns \\(-\\phi\\) (mod \\(1\\)) instead of \\(\\phi\\), an error that a test on \\(\\ket0\\) alone would miss, because both transforms send \\(\\ket0\\) to the uniform state."}, {"title": "Algorithm Implementation — Exercise 2", "chapter": "Algorithm Implementation", "url": "08-algorithm-implementation.html#exercise-2", "text": "Modify the chapter’s four-item search to mark \\(01\\) (qubit 0 equal to \\(1\\), qubit 1 equal to \\(0\\)) instead of \\(11\\). Which gates change, and what probability does the marked index reach after one iteration? Only the oracle changes: apply X to qubit 1, then CZ, then X to qubit 1, so the sign falls on \\(\\ket{01}\\). The diffusion step is unchanged. After one iteration, index \\(1\\) has probability \\(1\\). Separating the oracle from the diffusion makes this change local and testable."}, {"title": "Algorithm Implementation — Exercise 3", "chapter": "Algorithm Implementation", "url": "08-algorithm-implementation.html#exercise-3", "text": "Remove the final swap from the two-qubit QFT. Show that the result equals the full QFT followed by an extra swap. Is the swap-free circuit wrong? The swap-free circuit followed by a swap equals the full QFT, so the swap-free version computes the same transform with its output qubits in reversed order. It is not wrong if the reversal is accounted for, for example by relabeling outputs or by absorbing it into later wiring; many implementations omit the swaps to save gates. It is wrong only if downstream code assumes the standard order."}, {"title": "Algorithm Implementation — Exercise 4", "chapter": "Algorithm Implementation", "url": "08-algorithm-implementation.html#exercise-4", "text": "Implement two-bit phase estimation for \\(U=P(\\pi/2)\\), whose eigenstate \\(\\ket1\\) has phase \\(\\phi=1/4\\). Controls are qubits 0 (power \\(1\\)) and 1 (power \\(2\\)); the target is qubit 2 in \\(\\ket1\\). Apply the controlled powers as controlled phases and then the inverse of the chapter’s QFT on the controls. Predict and verify the control readout. Controlled-\\(U\\) is cp(\\(\\pi/2\\)) from qubit 0 and controlled-\\(U^2\\) is cp(\\(\\pi\\)) from qubit 1. They write \\(e^{2\\pi ik/4}\\) onto control basis state \\(\\ket k\\), and the inverse QFT returns \\(\\ket{j}\\) with \\(j=1\\). The full label is 101 (target 1, controls 01), with probability \\(1\\), so \\(\\phi=j/4=1/4\\). Testing with an exactly representable phase first isolates convention errors from rounding effects."}, {"title": "Algorithm Implementation — Exercise 5", "chapter": "Algorithm Implementation", "url": "08-algorithm-implementation.html#exercise-5", "text": "Repeat phase estimation with three control qubits for a phase that is not exactly representable, \\(\\phi=1/3\\). (a) Which outcome is most likely, and with what probability? (b) What estimate of \\(\\phi\\) does it give? (c) How would you test this case, given that the outcome is random? (a) The control readout \\(j=3\\) has probability about \\(0.69\\), with \\(j=2\\) next at about \\(0.17\\). (b) \\(j=3\\) gives \\(\\phi\\approx3/8=0.375\\), the nearest grid value to \\(1/3\\). (c) Compare the exact output distribution from Statevector with the analytic formula for every \\(j\\), rather than asserting a single outcome; in sampled runs, test that the frequency of \\(j=3\\) lies within a tolerance of \\(0.69\\)."}, {"title": "Algorithm Implementation — Exercise 6", "chapter": "Algorithm Implementation", "url": "08-algorithm-implementation.html#exercise-6", "text": "Implement a coined walk on a four-cycle: qubit 0 is the coin, qubits 1 and 2 hold the position (low bit on qubit 1). Each step applies H to the coin, increments the position if the coin is \\(1\\), and decrements it if the coin is \\(0\\). Starting from coin \\(0\\) at position \\(0\\), predict the position distribution after three steps and verify it. After three steps the walker is at position \\(3\\) with certainty: interference cancels every route to position \\(1\\). The controlled increment is ccx(0, 1, 2), cx(0, 1); the decrement controlled on coin \\(0\\) is X on the coin, then cx(0, 1), ccx(0, 1, 2), then X on the coin. A classical random walk would be at position \\(1\\) or \\(3\\) with probability \\(\\frac12\\) each; the coin register must be kept, not measured, for the interference to occur."}, {"title": "Algorithm Implementation — Exercise 7", "chapter": "Algorithm Implementation", "url": "08-algorithm-implementation.html#exercise-7", "text": "Implement Grover search for one marked item, \\(111\\), among eight. Use H, Toffoli, H on qubit 2 as the controlled-controlled-Z oracle, and the standard diffusion. Compute the success probability after \\(0,1,2,3\\) iterations and compare with \\(\\sin^2((2k+1)\\theta)\\), \\(\\sin\\theta=1/\\sqrt8\\). The success probabilities are \\(0.125\\), \\(0.781\\), \\(0.945\\), and \\(0.330\\) for \\(k=0,1,2,3\\), exactly \\(\\sin^2((2k+1)\\theta)\\). The third iteration overshoots. The test compares each value with the formula, which checks both the oracle and the diffusion, and documents why the iteration count is a parameter derived from \\(N\\), not a loop that runs until some budget is exhausted."}, {"title": "Algorithm Implementation — Exercise 8", "chapter": "Algorithm Implementation", "url": "08-algorithm-implementation.html#exercise-8", "text": "A student tests a QFT implementation only on the input \\(\\ket{00}\\), checks that the output is the uniform superposition, and declares it correct. Construct a wrong circuit that passes this test, and state a test that would catch it. \\(H\\otimes H\\) alone sends \\(\\ket{00}\\) to the uniform superposition, exactly like the QFT, and so does the inverse QFT. All three differ on other inputs; for example the QFT and \\(H\\otimes H\\) give different phases on \\(\\ket{01}\\). A correct test compares the full operator with the defining matrix (as the chapter does), or checks every basis input including relative phases. A single input exercises only one column of the matrix."}, {"title": "Algorithm Implementation — Exercise 9", "chapter": "Algorithm Implementation", "url": "08-algorithm-implementation.html#exercise-9", "text": "Build a four-qubit positive-sign QFT and an approximate version that omits the smallest controlled phase, cp(\\(\\pi/8\\)). (a) Compute the operator-norm distance between them. (b) Compare it with the bound \\(|e^{i\\pi/8}-1|\\). (c) How does this scale to large registers? (a) The distance is about \\(0.390\\). (b) That equals \\(|e^{i\\pi/8}-1|\\approx0.390\\): removing one controlled phase changes the circuit by exactly that gate’s deviation from the identity, because unitaries before and after it preserve norms. (c) Dropping every rotation smaller than \\(2\\pi/2^b\\) gives total error below about \\(2\\pi n/2^b\\) with only \\(O(n\\log n)\\) gates, the approximate QFT of Quantum Algorithms; omitting larger angles, as here, gives a large error."}, {"title": "Algorithm Implementation — Exercise 10", "chapter": "Algorithm Implementation", "url": "08-algorithm-implementation.html#exercise-10", "text": "In phase estimation with \\(m\\) control qubits, controlled-\\(U^{2^j}\\) built by repetition needs \\(2^j\\) uses of controlled-\\(U\\). (a) Count the total uses for \\(m=3\\) and \\(m=10\\). (b) For \\(U=P(\\lambda)\\), what shortcut implements \\(U^{2^j}\\) at the cost of one gate? (c) Why does a benchmark of phase estimation need to state which of these was used? (a) \\(m=3\\): \\(7\\) uses; \\(m=10\\): \\(1023\\). (b) \\(P(\\lambda)^{2^j}=P(2^j\\lambda)\\), a single controlled phase, which is why the worked problems used cp(\\(2^j\\lambda\\)) directly. (c) With repetition, the circuit grows exponentially in the number of bits of precision; with a shortcut, linearly. Modular multiplication in Shor’s algorithm has such a shortcut, while general Hamiltonian evolution does not. A runtime or gate count is meaningless without saying which construction was used."}, {"title": "Fourier Methods", "chapter": "Algorithm Implementation", "url": "08-algorithm-implementation.html#section-8-1", "text": "8.1 Fourier Methods The quantum Fourier transform appears in phase estimation, period finding and many other algorithms. Its formula is simple. Which gates realize it, and in what order? We derive the two-qubit circuit from the formula, then use it to read out the phase in phase estimation. 8.1.1 Constructing the Fourier circuit We take the transform with the positive exponent, \\[F\\ket y=\\frac12\\sum_{x=0}^{3}e^{2\\pi ixy/4}\\ket x,\\] with integer labels \\(x=x_0+2x_1\\) and \\(y=y_0+2y_1\\), and qubit 0 as the low digit. Can we build \\(F\\) from one- and two-qubit gates? The key is that the output is a product state. Splitting the exponent, \\(e^{2\\pi ixy/4}=e^{2\\pi ix_0y/4}\\,e^{2\\pi ix_1y/2}\\), so \\[F\\ket y=\\frac{\\ket0+e^{2\\pi iy/2}\\ket1}{\\sqrt2}\\otimes\\frac{\\ket0+e^{2\\pi iy/4}\\ket1}{\\sqrt2},\\] where the left factor is output qubit \\(x_1\\) and the right factor is \\(x_0\\). Now we read off what each factor needs. Since \\(e^{2\\pi iy/2}=(-1)^{y_0}\\), the \\(x_1\\) factor is \\((\\ket0+(-1)^{y_0}\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\). That is H applied to the input bit \\(y_0\\). The \\(x_0\\) factor has phase \\(e^{2\\pi iy/4}=(-1)^{y_1}\\,i^{y_0}\\). That is H applied to \\(y_1\\), followed by a phase \\(i=e^{i\\pi/2}\\) when \\(y_0=1\\), a controlled phase \\(P(\\pi/2)\\). The circuit follows. H on qubit 1 makes \\((\\ket0+(-1)^{y_1}\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\). The controlled phase from qubit 0 must come next, while qubit 0 still holds the bit \\(y_0\\), and it completes the \\(x_0\\) factor, on qubit 1. H on qubit 0 then makes the \\(x_1\\) factor, on qubit 0. Each factor has ended up on the other wire, so a final swap puts them in place: qft = QuantumCircuit(2)\nqft.h(1)\nqft.cp(np.pi / 2, 0, 1)\nqft.h(0)\nqft.swap(0, 1) The companion compares all 16 entries of this circuit’s matrix with \\(e^{2\\pi ixy/4}/2\\). The expected matrix is built by a list comprehension with two for clauses, one per index, and 2j is Python’s notation for the complex number \\(2i\\). Omitting the swap would reverse the output bit order. Taking the inverse would flip the sign of the exponent. Positive-sign two-qubit Fourier circuit. The final SWAP exchanges the two wires; its connected endpoints are a two-wire operation. The controlled phase applies only when both bits are one. Problem 8.1. Why compare QFT matrices instead of only transforming zero? Worked solution Both forward and inverse Fourier transforms map zero to the same uniform state. A zero-input test misses the sign convention. 8.1.2 Controlled powers and phase estimation Phase estimation asks: given a unitary \\(U\\) and an eigenstate with \\(U\\ket u=e^{2\\pi i\\varphi}\\ket u\\), what is \\(\\varphi\\)? We need a test case whose answer we know exactly. We choose \\(U=P(2\\pi\\varphi)\\) with \\(\\varphi=1/4\\) and eigenstate \\(\\ket1\\), prepared on qubit 2 with an X gate. Qubits 0 and 1 form the counting register. We put each counting qubit in \\(\\ket+\\) and let counting qubit \\(j\\) control \\(U^{2^j}\\). By phase kickback, qubit \\(j\\) picks up the phase \\(e^{2\\pi i\\varphi2^j}\\) on its \\(\\ket1\\). Together the two qubits hold \\[\\frac12\\sum_{x=0}^{3}e^{2\\pi i\\varphi x}\\ket x.\\] With \\(\\varphi=y/4\\), this is exactly \\(F\\ket y\\). So the inverse Fourier circuit returns \\(\\ket y\\), here \\(y=4\\varphi=1\\). In the program, qft.inverse() supplies the inverse, and compose(…, qubits=[0, 1], inplace=True) attaches it to the counting wires, changing qpe itself rather than returning a copy. Here \\(U^{2^j}\\) is again a phase gate, so each power costs one controlled phase. For a general \\(U\\), implementing \\(U^{2^j}\\) by repetition costs \\(2^j\\) uses of \\(U\\). phi = 1 / 4\nqpe = QuantumCircuit(3)\nqpe.x(2)\nqpe.h([0, 1])\nfor j in range(2):\n    qpe.cp(2 * np.pi * phi * 2**j, j, 2)  # controlled U^(2^j)\nqpe.compose(qft.inverse(), qubits=[0, 1], inplace=True) The check asks for the probabilities of the counting qubits alone, probabilities([0, 1]) , and requires that integer 1 has probability 1. We decode the counting register through this list of qubits, not through the order of wires in a drawing. A phase that is exactly representable in two bits makes the first test clean. Approximate phases and superpositions of eigenstates come after it passes."}, {"title": "Amplitude Manipulation", "chapter": "Algorithm Implementation", "url": "08-algorithm-implementation.html#section-8-2", "text": "8.2 Amplitude Manipulation Phase estimation reads out a phase. Search uses phases differently: a phase oracle marks the good items with a sign, and reflections turn that sign into probability. The algorithm fixes the reflections only up to conventions. Which conventions are safe to choose freely, and how many iterations should the program run? We implement the reflections first, then the search loop. 8.2.1 Preparation and reflections Take four items, with only \\(\\ket{11}\\) marked. We begin with the uniform state \\(\\ket s=H^{\\otimes2}\\ket{00}\\). How do we mark \\(\\ket{11}\\) with a sign? A CZ gate multiplies exactly \\(\\ket{11}\\) by \\(-1\\), so it is the phase oracle here. Next comes the reflection about \\(\\ket s\\), usually written \\(D=2\\ketbra ss-I\\). The companion builds it as H on both, X on both, CZ, X on both, H on both. What does that sequence compute? Conjugating CZ by X on both qubits moves its sign from \\(\\ket{11}\\) to \\(\\ket{00}\\), giving \\(I-2\\ketbra{00}{00}\\). Conjugating by H on both then gives \\(I-2\\ketbra ss=-D\\). So the companion implements \\(-D\\), which differs from the textbook reflection by a global sign. For the search alone that sign changes no probability. If the whole iteration were later controlled, though, it would become a relative phase on the control, by the result we derived for controlled operations. So the convention must be recorded with the component. 8.2.2 Search and amplification How many iterations should we run? One marked item out of four gives initial success probability \\(\\sin^2\\theta=1/4\\), so \\(\\theta=\\pi/6\\). Each iteration rotates the state by \\(2\\theta\\) toward the marked item, so after \\(k\\) iterations the success probability is \\(\\sin^2((2k+1)\\theta)\\). One iteration gives \\(\\sin^2(\\pi/2)=1\\). The companion checks that the probability of index 3, the state \\(\\ket{11}\\), is 1. search = QuantumCircuit(2)\nsearch.h([0, 1])\nsearch.cz(0, 1)\nsearch.h([0, 1]); search.x([0, 1]); search.cz(0, 1)\nsearch.x([0, 1]); search.h([0, 1]) Python allows several statements on one line when they are separated by semicolons, which keeps the reflection on two lines. For larger instances, we choose the number of iterations from the initial success probability. When that probability is unknown, we need a schedule with a justified guarantee. More iterations can reduce success, so a loop that simply runs “long enough” is not an algorithm. Problem 8.2. What happens after a second Grover iteration in the four-item example? Worked solution The success returns to one quarter. The rotation has passed the good direction."}, {"title": "Quantum Walks", "chapter": "Algorithm Implementation", "url": "08-algorithm-implementation.html#section-8-3", "text": "8.3 Quantum Walks A quantum walk moves amplitude around a graph, one step at a time, steered by an extra register called the coin. To program it, we must make the step reversible with the gates we have, and then decide what a fair comparison with a classical walk would measure. We first implement one step on a four-site cycle, then ask what its cost and its correctness tests do and do not tell us. 8.3.1 Position and coin registers Suppose a walker sits on a cycle of four sites. A step should move it one site forward or one site back. Can we write that step as a unitary on the position alone? No. Both “site 1 moved forward” and “site 3 moved back” end at site 2, so a position-only map would merge two states into one, and a unitary cannot do that. We add a coin qubit that records the direction. The step is then reversible, because the coin tells us which way to undo it. We put the coin on qubit 0 and the position, \\(q_1+2q_2\\), on qubits 1 and 2. One step applies H to the coin and then shifts: coin 0 moves the position by \\(+1\\), coin 1 by \\(-1\\), both modulo 4. The increment is the two-bit incrementer we built for arithmetic oracles, with an extra control on the coin. The decrement is its inverse. To condition on coin 0 instead of coin 1, we surround the controlled increment with X gates on the coin: walk = QuantumCircuit(3)\nwalk.h(0)\nwalk.cx(0, 1); walk.ccx(0, 1, 2)                 # coin 1: position - 1\nwalk.x(0); walk.ccx(0, 1, 2); walk.cx(0, 1); walk.x(0)  # coin 0: position + 1 Our independent prediction is a matrix. The shift sends basis index \\(c+2p\\) to \\(c+2((p\\pm1)\\bmod4)\\), and the coin step is \\(I\\otimes H\\), with H on the right because the coin is qubit 0. The companion builds both with NumPy: % is the remainder, so -1 % 4 is 3, and np.kron forms a tensor product. It then compares the circuit’s matrix with their product. Two further rules follow from the reasoning above. We keep the coin from step to step, since discarding its direction after a step would turn the walk into a different, non-unitary process. And if the number of sites is not a power of two, some basis states of the position register stand for no site, so the circuit needs a stated rule for them to remain unitary. Problem 8.3. Can the position register alone generally implement a reversible coined shift? Worked solution The direction label is needed to distinguish the routes. Removing it without another reversible encoding can merge states and invalidate the proposed unitary. 8.3.2 Implementation and classical baselines Our walk passes its matrix check. Does that tell us anything about whether a quantum walk beats a classical one? No. A matrix check establishes correctness for one small instance. A fair comparison with a classical method must give both the same access to the graph and ask both for the same output. The quantum side should then be measured by quantities that grow with the instance: compiled gates, depth, qubits, oracle calls and readout cost. What about the time our simulation took? That measures the classical simulator, not the quantum algorithm. A simulation that slows down rapidly with size says nothing about how the circuit would perform on quantum hardware. The companion collects the checks from this chapter: the Fourier matrix, the phase-estimation readout, the search success and the walk step. They establish correctness, not a performance advantage. \"\"\"Runnable companion for Quantum Programming, Chapter 8.\"\"\"\nimport numpy as np\nfrom qiskit import QuantumCircuit\nfrom qiskit.quantum_info import Operator, Statevector\n\n# Positive-sign QFT on integer labels |q1 q0>.\nqft = QuantumCircuit(2)\nqft.h(1)\nqft.cp(np.pi / 2, 0, 1)\nqft.h(0)\nqft.swap(0, 1)\nexpected = np.array([[np.exp(2j*np.pi*x*y/4)/2 for x in range(4)] for y in range(4)])\nassert np.allclose(Operator(qft).data, expected)\n\n# Phase estimation: counting qubits 0 and 1, eigenstate |1> of P(2*pi*phi) on qubit 2.\nphi = 1 / 4\nqpe = QuantumCircuit(3)\nqpe.x(2)\nqpe.h([0, 1])\nfor j in range(2):\n    qpe.cp(2 * np.pi * phi * 2**j, j, 2)  # controlled U^(2^j)\nqpe.compose(qft.inverse(), qubits=[0, 1], inplace=True)\ncounting = Statevector.from_instruction(qpe).probabilities([0, 1])\nassert np.isclose(counting[1], 1)  # integer 1 = phi * 4\n\n# One Grover step for marked state |11> in four items.\nsearch = QuantumCircuit(2)\nsearch.h([0, 1])\nsearch.cz(0, 1)\nsearch.h([0, 1]); search.x([0, 1]); search.cz(0, 1)\nsearch.x([0, 1]); search.h([0, 1])\nassert np.isclose(Statevector.from_instruction(search).probabilities()[3], 1)\n\n# One coined-walk step on a 4-cycle: coin q0, position q1 + 2*q2.\nwalk = QuantumCircuit(3)\nwalk.h(0)\nwalk.cx(0, 1); walk.ccx(0, 1, 2)                 # coin 1: position - 1\nwalk.x(0); walk.ccx(0, 1, 2); walk.cx(0, 1); walk.x(0)  # coin 0: position + 1\nshift = np.zeros((8, 8))\nfor coin in range(2):\n    for position in range(4):\n        moved = (position + (1 if coin == 0 else -1)) % 4\n        shift[coin + 2 * moved, coin + 2 * position] = 1\ncoin_flip = np.kron(np.eye(4), np.array([[1, 1], [1, -1]]) / np.sqrt(2))\nassert np.allclose(Operator(walk).data, shift @ coin_flip)\nprint(qft.draw(output=\"text\")) Companion script: chapter_08.py . Each check in this chapter compared a program with a prediction we derived first. Some checks were stronger than others: a full matrix catches more than one probability. What, in general, counts as enough evidence that a quantum program does what it should? Sources and further study: IBM, quantum information operators ."}, {"title": "Testing and Verification — Exercise 1", "chapter": "Testing and Verification", "url": "09-testing-and-verification.html#exercise-1", "text": "A circuit should do nothing to one qubit, but a bug inserts an S gate. Which of these tests detects the bug: (a) basis-state output probabilities for \\(\\ket0\\) and \\(\\ket1\\); (b) statevector equivalence on \\(\\ket+\\); (c) Operator equivalence? (a) Misses it: S leaves the basis probabilities unchanged. (b) Catches it: \\(S\\ket+=(\\ket0+i\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\) is not equivalent to \\(\\ket+\\). (c) Catches it: diag\\((1,i)\\) is not the identity up to a global phase. Tests of coherent components must include superposition inputs or full operators."}, {"title": "Testing and Verification — Exercise 2", "chapter": "Testing and Verification", "url": "09-testing-and-verification.html#exercise-2", "text": "Why does np.isclose(1e-17, 0, rtol=1e-9, atol=0) return False, while np.isclose(1e-17, 0, rtol=0, atol=1e-12) returns True? Which tolerance should an amplitude test use? With atol=0, the allowed difference is rtol times the magnitude of the reference value, which is \\(0\\), so even \\(10^{-17}\\) fails. An absolute tolerance of \\(10^{-12}\\) accepts floating-point noise. Amplitude tests should include an absolute tolerance, because many correct amplitudes are exactly zero and their computed values are tiny nonzero numbers (such as the \\(10^{-33}\\) entries seen in earlier chapters)."}, {"title": "Testing and Verification — Exercise 3", "chapter": "Testing and Verification", "url": "09-testing-and-verification.html#exercise-3", "text": "rz(0.4) and p(0.4) differ by a global phase. Does np.allclose on their Operator matrices succeed? Does Operator.equiv? Which comparison answers “are these the same standalone gate?” np.allclose fails, because every entry differs by the factor \\(e^{-0.2i}\\). Operator.equiv succeeds, because it allows a global phase. equiv answers the standalone question; allclose answers the stricter question that matters when the gate will be controlled, where the two are genuinely different. A test must use the notion of equality that matches the gate’s intended use."}, {"title": "Testing and Verification — Exercise 4", "chapter": "Testing and Verification", "url": "09-testing-and-verification.html#exercise-4", "text": "Write a test that compares an implementation with its specification on a few random input states, using random_statevector with fixed seeds. Apply it to the S-gate bug of the quick check. Why are random states useful, and what do they not replace? For each seeded random state, evolve it through both circuits and require equivalence; the S bug fails on every generic input, since a random state has both basis components. Random inputs exercise all relative phases at once and catch errors that special inputs such as \\(\\ket0\\) hide. They do not replace an exhaustive operator check for small components, and they are not a proof: a bug confined to a tiny part of a large space may need many samples or a structured test."}, {"title": "Testing and Verification — Exercise 5", "chapter": "Testing and Verification", "url": "09-testing-and-verification.html#exercise-5", "text": "A test suite runs \\(20\\) independent sampling tests, each with a two-sided tolerance of \\(z\\) standard deviations. (a) For \\(z=3\\), each correct test fails with probability about \\(0.0027\\). What is the chance that a run of the whole suite has at least one false failure? (b) Repeat for \\(z=4\\) (each \\(6.3\\times10^{-5}\\)). (c) What else should be done instead of simply raising \\(z\\)? (a) \\(1-0.9973^{20}\\approx5.3\\%\\): one in twenty runs of a correct suite fails. (b) \\(1-(1-6.3\\times10^{-5})^{20}\\approx0.13\\%\\). (c) Larger tolerances reduce false alarms but also hide small bugs. Better: test deterministic properties exactly with Statevector or Operator, use fixed seeds for regression tests of sampling code, and size the shot counts so that the bugs that matter remain detectable at the chosen \\(z\\)."}, {"title": "Testing and Verification — Exercise 6", "chapter": "Testing and Verification", "url": "09-testing-and-verification.html#exercise-6", "text": "A product-formula simulation of \\(e^{-i(X+Z)t}\\) for \\(t=1\\) with \\(r=10\\) first-order steps has the error bound \\(t^2\\|[X,Z]\\|/(2r)=0.1\\). Write a test that compares the circuit’s operator with the exact exponential and uses this analytic bound as the tolerance. Check the actual error. The circuit repeats ten steps of \\(e^{-iX\\delta}e^{-iZ\\delta}\\), implemented as rz(\\(2\\delta\\)) then rx(\\(2\\delta\\)), since \\(R_x(\\theta)=e^{-i\\theta X/2}\\). The exact unitary comes from diagonalizing \\(X+Z\\). The operator-norm error is about \\(0.07\\), within the bound \\(0.1\\), so the test passes. A tight tolerance such as \\(10^{-12}\\) would wrongly fail this correct approximate circuit; the tolerance must come from the approximation’s error budget."}, {"title": "Testing and Verification — Exercise 7", "chapter": "Testing and Verification", "url": "09-testing-and-verification.html#exercise-7", "text": "A biased-coin bug shifts \\(P(1)\\) from \\(0.50\\) to \\(0.48\\). How many shots are needed for a test with a three-standard-deviation false-alarm threshold to detect this bug with \\(90\\%\\) probability? Use \\(N\\approx\\big((z_\\alpha+z_\\beta)\\sigma/\\delta\\big)^2\\) with \\(z_\\alpha=3\\), \\(z_\\beta=1.28\\), \\(\\sigma=0.5\\), \\(\\delta=0.02\\). \\(N\\approx107^2\\approx1.1\\times10^4\\) shots. With the \\(4096\\) shots of earlier examples, the test would usually miss a \\(2\\%\\) bias. Detection power must be planned: decide the smallest error that matters, then choose shots. When the property is deterministic, an exact Statevector check detects the bias without any shots."}, {"title": "Testing and Verification — Exercise 8", "chapter": "Testing and Verification", "url": "09-testing-and-verification.html#exercise-8", "text": "A student argues: “If a test passes with a large tolerance, the circuit is correct; larger tolerances are just safer.” Using the S-gate bug, show a tolerance at which a statevector comparison on \\(\\ket+\\) would pass, and explain why “safer” is the wrong word. The outputs \\(\\ket+\\) and \\(S\\ket+\\) have fidelity \\(\\frac12\\); a test that accepts any fidelity above \\(0.4\\), or any amplitude difference below \\(1\\), would pass the bug. A tolerance is not a safety margin: it defines which differences count as errors. It must be larger than numerical noise or approximation error, and much smaller than the smallest bug the test exists to catch. Too large is as wrong as too small."}, {"title": "Testing and Verification — Exercise 9", "chapter": "Testing and Verification", "url": "09-testing-and-verification.html#exercise-9", "text": "A subroutine is supposed to act only on qubits 0 and 1 of a three-qubit register. Write a test that verifies its Operator equals \\(I\\otimes U_{\\rm sub}\\), so that qubit 2 is untouched. What kinds of bugs does this catch that tests of the two-qubit block alone would not? Comparing Operator(big) with Operator(I).tensor(Operator(sub)) checks that the embedded circuit acts as the subroutine on qubits 0, 1 and as the identity on qubit 2. It catches wrong wire mappings, gates accidentally applied to spectator qubits, and entanglement with the spectator (for example a stray CNOT onto qubit 2), none of which a test of the subroutine in isolation would see. The tensor order follows Qiskit’s convention that the left factor acts on the higher index."}, {"title": "Testing and Verification — Exercise 10", "chapter": "Testing and Verification", "url": "09-testing-and-verification.html#exercise-10", "text": "Mutation testing measures a test suite by injecting small bugs. For the Bell circuit, consider four mutants: swap control and target; append Z on qubit 0; replace H by SX; drop the CNOT. Compare two tests: (a) “the counts contain only 00 and 11”; (b) statevector equivalence with \\((\\ket{00}+\\ket{11})/\\sqrt2\\). How many mutants does each detect? Swapping control and target, or dropping the CNOT, produces outcomes 00 and 01, so test (a) catches both. Appending Z gives \\((\\ket{00}-\\ket{11})/\\sqrt2\\), and SX gives \\((\\ket{00}-i\\ket{11})/\\sqrt2\\): both still give only 00 and 11, so test (a) misses them. Test (b) catches all four. Mutation testing turns “my tests pass” into a measurable statement about which bugs they would catch, and shows that phase errors need phase-sensitive tests."}, {"title": "Functional Tests", "chapter": "Testing and Verification", "url": "09-testing-and-verification.html#section-9-1", "text": "9.1 Functional Tests Suppose a component should do nothing to its qubit, and by mistake it applies Z. Which test catches the mistake? The obvious tests do not, and seeing why tells us what a functional test must compare. We find the failing test first, then decide which differences between two operations should count as errors. 9.1.1 Basis inputs and superpositions The first attempt runs both circuits on the basis inputs \\(\\ket0\\) and \\(\\ket1\\) and compares the output probabilities. Both tests pass. Since \\(Z\\ket0=\\ket0\\) and \\(Z\\ket1=-\\ket1\\), the outputs differ only by a sign, and a sign changes no probability. What input would expose the difference? One in which the sign becomes a relative phase between two amplitudes. On \\(\\ket+\\), the identity gives \\(\\ket+\\), while \\(Z\\) gives \\(\\ket-\\). These two states are orthogonal, the most different two states can be. A Z-basis histogram still cannot tell them apart, but a comparison of amplitudes can, and so can a measurement in the X basis. The companion makes this argument executable. Statevector.from_label(\"0\") builds a basis state from its label, and \"+\" builds \\(\\ket+\\). The method evolve applies a circuit to a state. A loop over the tuple (\"0\", \"1\") shows that the basis probabilities agree. The final checks use not to assert that the two outputs on \\(\\ket+\\), and the two operators, are not equivalent. So a coherent contract needs tests on superpositions, comparing amplitudes, not only labels. 9.1.2 Equivalence and global phase If we compare amplitudes, should every difference count as a bug? Take a circuit whose only content is a global phase of 0.3, set through phase.global_phase = 0.3 . On its own, it makes the same predictions as the identity, so a test that rejects it would be wrong. Operator.equiv accepts two operators that differ by one overall phase factor, and the companion asserts that the identity and this circuit pass. Two kinds of difference are not covered by that freedom. A separate phase on each basis column is not global: \\(Z=\\operatorname{diag}(1,-1)\\) differs from \\(I\\) by a phase on one column only, and the equivalence check rejects it. And under control, even a global phase becomes a relative phase on the control, as we derived for controlled operations. So the companion also checks that the controlled identity and the controlled phase circuit are not equivalent. The rule for a test, then, is to allow a global phase only for a component that will never be controlled, and to record that assumption. Problem 9.1. Why does testing Z on zero miss the bug? Worked solution Zero is an eigenstate of Z with eigenvalue one. Test plus to expose the relative sign between zero and one."}, {"title": "State and Workspace Checks", "chapter": "Testing and Verification", "url": "09-testing-and-verification.html#section-9-2", "text": "9.2 State and Workspace Checks Functional tests check the action on the useful registers. A component promises more than that: its wires are attached in a stated order, its workspace starts and ends clean, and it can be inverted and combined with others. Which tests check these promises, and which only seem to? We take wire order and workspace first, then inverses and composition. 9.2.1 Register ordering and ancilla cleanup Which input exposes two wires attached in the wrong order? Not a symmetric one: the Bell pair and the all-zero input look the same either way round. An asymmetric input, such as X on qubit 0 alone, makes the two orders give different outputs. For workspace, we have three checks of increasing strength. The weakest measures the ancilla and requires outcome 0 with probability 1. A stronger one computes the reduced state of the useful registers and compares it with the predicted pure state. The strongest, possible when the system is small, compares the whole joint state with the predicted useful state times \\(\\ket0\\) on the ancilla. A clean workspace must factor away from the useful registers in this way. Suppose a contract promises that the ancilla starts in \\(\\ket0\\). Then we test only inputs with the ancilla in \\(\\ket0\\). Such tests say nothing about other ancilla states. That is acceptable, because the contract never promised anything about them. 9.2.2 Inverse and composition tests Is “\\(U\\) followed by its inverse gives the identity” a good test of \\(U\\)? Recall that inverse() computes the inverse from \\(U\\)’s own gates. So the product is the identity whatever \\(U\\) does, right or wrong. The test checks the inverse construction, not the specification, and we pair it with a test against an independent prediction. Composition tests have a different value. Joining two components can reveal a wire contract that one of them breaks, or a phase convention that one of them omitted. Feeding in a randomly chosen superposition is another useful check, because it exercises all the amplitudes and phases at once. For a small component, though, the complete matrix remains the decisive test. A random input gives evidence about one input. The matrix covers all of them. Problem 9.2. Can two individually global-equivalent gates always be substituted under control? Worked solution No. Their differing phase acts only in the control-one branch and becomes observable relative to the control-zero branch."}, {"title": "Statistical Tests", "chapter": "Testing and Verification", "url": "09-testing-and-verification.html#section-9-3", "text": "9.3 Statistical Tests Every comparison so far used a tolerance, and every sampled one used a statistical window. A tolerance that is too loose accepts bugs, and one that is too tight rejects correct programs. How do we choose these numbers rather than guess them? We treat rounding and approximation first, then sampling. 9.3.1 Numerical tolerances How large are rounding errors in an exact calculation? A computer stores numbers with about 16 significant digits, so each operation can introduce an error near \\(10^{-16}\\). A small gate sequence accumulates a few of these. An absolute tolerance such as \\(10^{-12}\\) therefore leaves a wide margin above rounding while remaining far below any real mistake. A circuit that is approximate by design is different. Its error is part of the method, so the tolerance must come from an analytic bound on that error. We will meet such a bound when we approximate a time evolution by a product of simpler steps. Why not use a relative tolerance alone, accepting \\(a\\) when \\(|a-b|\\le r|b|\\)? When the expected value \\(b\\) is 0, the condition becomes \\(|a|\\le0\\). An amplitude that should vanish but comes out as \\(10^{-17}\\) then fails. So near zero we need an absolute tolerance, and a test should state which tolerance it uses. It should also state which notion of equality it uses, exact or up to global phase, and which distance it measures, entry by entry or over the whole matrix. 9.3.2 Sampling uncertainty and regression tests A sampled test can fail even when the program is correct. For the coin we chose a window of 6.4 standard deviations, which a correct program leaves with probability about \\(2\\times10^{-10}\\). A suite of many such tests multiplies that chance, so windows must be set with the whole suite in mind. Demanding exact counts, as we saw, rejects most correct runs. Fixed seeds help in another way. With a seed, a sampled test gives the same result on every run, so a change signals a change in the code or environment. But a seed can also hide a test that passes only for that seed. So sampled tests sit beside deterministic checks of states and operators, which have no randomness at all. Finally, how do we know a test can fail? We give it a wrong program. The companion includes the Z bug deliberately and asserts that the coherent test rejects it. \"\"\"Runnable companion for Quantum Programming, Chapter 9.\"\"\"\nimport numpy as np\nfrom qiskit import QuantumCircuit\nfrom qiskit.quantum_info import Operator, Statevector\n\nidentity = QuantumCircuit(1)\nwrong = QuantumCircuit(1)\nwrong.z(0)\n# Basis output probabilities match, but relative phase differs.\nfor label in (\"0\", \"1\"):\n    state = Statevector.from_label(label)\n    assert np.allclose(state.evolve(identity).probabilities(), state.evolve(wrong).probabilities())\nassert not Statevector.from_label(\"+\").evolve(identity).equiv(Statevector.from_label(\"+\").evolve(wrong))\nassert not Operator(identity).equiv(Operator(wrong))\nphase = QuantumCircuit(1)\nphase.global_phase = 0.3\nassert Operator(identity).equiv(Operator(phase))\nassert not Operator(identity.to_gate().control()).equiv(Operator(phase.to_gate().control()))\nprint(\"Relative-phase bug detected; controlled global phase also checked\") Companion script: chapter_09.py . Problem 9.3. Is a larger tolerance always safer? Worked solution It reduces false failures but can accept real bugs. Choose it from the numerical and approximation budgets, and include known incorrect examples that should fail. Our tests compare a program with its logical specification. A device, though, will not run our circuit as written. A compiler rewrites it into the device’s own gates and wiring. How does a logical program become executable on a specific device, and how do we check that the rewritten circuit still means the same thing?"}, {"title": "Compilation and Transpilation — Exercise 1", "chapter": "Compilation and Transpilation", "url": "10-compilation-and-transpilation.html#exercise-1", "text": "The companion compiles H on qubit 0 and CX from 0 to 2 for a line of three qubits. How many CNOTs does the compiled circuit contain, and where do the extra ones come from? The compiled circuit has \\(4\\) CNOTs: three implement a SWAP that brings the logical qubits together, and one is the requested CNOT. The logical circuit had one. Connectivity, not the algorithm, accounts for three quarters of the entangling gates here."}, {"title": "Compilation and Transpilation — Exercise 2", "chapter": "Compilation and Transpilation", "url": "10-compilation-and-transpilation.html#exercise-2", "text": "A circuit applies H twice to qubit 0 and then CX from 0 to 1. Compare the compiled gate counts at optimization levels 0 and 1. What does the comparison show, and what does an optimization level not guarantee? Level 0 keeps both Hadamards (decomposed into several rz and sx gates) plus the CNOT; level 1 cancels them and leaves just the CNOT. Higher levels apply more passes, which usually reduces counts, but no level guarantees the smallest possible circuit, or even a smaller one than a lower level, for every input. Compare results and report the level used."}, {"title": "Compilation and Transpilation — Exercise 3", "chapter": "Compilation and Transpilation", "url": "10-compilation-and-transpilation.html#exercise-3", "text": "A device supports CNOT only from qubit 0 to qubit 1. Show that Hadamards on both qubits before and after a CX(0, 1) implement CX(1, 0). What does a wrong-direction CNOT cost? \\((H\\otimes H)\\,\\mathrm{CX}_{0\\to1}\\,(H\\otimes H)=\\mathrm{CX}_{1\\to0}\\), which Operator equivalence confirms. A reversed CNOT therefore costs four extra Hadamards (or their native equivalents). Direction constraints, like connectivity, are part of the target and appear in compiled counts and depth."}, {"title": "Compilation and Transpilation — Exercise 4", "chapter": "Compilation and Transpilation", "url": "10-compilation-and-transpilation.html#exercise-4", "text": "For the companion’s compiled circuit, (a) compare Operator(compiled) directly with Operator(circuit); (b) compare Operator.from_circuit(compiled) with Operator(circuit); (c) inspect the final layout. Explain the results. (a) The direct comparison fails. (b) The layout-aware comparison succeeds. (c) The final index layout is \\([0,2,1]\\): after routing, logical qubits 1 and 2 have exchanged physical positions, because the inserted SWAP was not undone. A matrix built on physical indices therefore differs from the logical operator by that permutation. Equivalence checks of compiled circuits must account for layout, or they report false errors (or, worse, hide real ones)."}, {"title": "Compilation and Transpilation — Exercise 5", "chapter": "Compilation and Transpilation", "url": "10-compilation-and-transpilation.html#exercise-5", "text": "Recompile the companion with initial_layout=[0, 2, 1], placing logical qubit 2 on physical qubit 1. Predict the CNOT count, and explain why layout is part of a resource estimate. With logical qubits 0 and 2 placed on adjacent physical qubits 0 and 1, the CNOT is native and no routing is needed: \\(1\\) CNOT instead of \\(4\\). The same logical circuit can cost four times more depending on placement, so a reported count must state the target, the layout (or the layout method), and the seed."}, {"title": "Compilation and Transpilation — Exercise 6", "chapter": "Compilation and Transpilation", "url": "10-compilation-and-transpilation.html#exercise-6", "text": "Compile an arbitrary single-qubit unitary (a seeded random_unitary wrapped in UnitaryGate) to the basis rz, sx, x. How many gates result? Why can every single-qubit unitary be written with at most two SX gates and three RZ gates? The compiled circuit has \\(3\\) RZ and \\(2\\) SX gates. Every single-qubit unitary has an Euler decomposition \\(R_z(\\alpha)R_y(\\beta)R_z(\\gamma)\\) up to global phase, and a Y rotation can be written as RZ rotations sandwiching SX gates, giving the pattern RZ, SX, RZ, SX, RZ. Arbitrary single-qubit gates are cheap on this basis; two-qubit gates and routing dominate compiled costs."}, {"title": "Compilation and Transpilation — Exercise 7", "chapter": "Compilation and Transpilation", "url": "10-compilation-and-transpilation.html#exercise-7", "text": "A four-qubit circuit applies a CNOT between every pair of qubits (six CNOTs). Compile it for all-to-all connectivity and for a line. Compare the CNOT counts, and estimate the failure contribution of the extra gates if each CNOT fails with probability \\(5\\times10^{-3}\\). All-to-all: \\(6\\) CNOTs. Line: about \\(11\\) in this run, nearly double, because non-neighbouring pairs need routing. The extra five CNOTs add roughly \\(5\\times5\\times10^{-3}=2.5\\%\\) failure probability. Interaction patterns that are dense in qubit pairs pay heavily on sparse hardware unless the algorithm is restructured, for example with the swap networks of Quantum Hardware Chapter 17."}, {"title": "Compilation and Transpilation — Exercise 8", "chapter": "Compilation and Transpilation", "url": "10-compilation-and-transpilation.html#exercise-8", "text": "A student compiles a circuit for one device and then runs the compiled circuit on a different device “because it is already in native gates.” Name three reasons the compiled circuit may be invalid or poor on the second device. The second device may support a different native gate set (for example a different entangling gate), have different connectivity or allowed CNOT directions, so the routed circuit uses pairs it cannot couple, and have different error rates, so a layout chosen to avoid bad qubits on the first device may land on bad qubits on the second. A compiled circuit is specific to its target; keep the logical circuit and recompile for each backend."}, {"title": "Compilation and Transpilation — Exercise 9", "chapter": "Compilation and Transpilation", "url": "10-compilation-and-transpilation.html#exercise-9", "text": "Full Operator equivalence checks are impossible for large compiled circuits. Propose a scalable check and implement a small version: compare the compiled and logical circuits on several random product input states, accounting for the layout. What can such a check miss? For each trial, prepare a random product state on the logical qubits, run it through the logical circuit, and through the compiled circuit with inputs and outputs permuted by the layout; then compare. Statevector simulation scales further than dense matrices, and product inputs are easy to prepare. The small implementation below checks the companion’s circuit this way. Random tests can miss errors confined to a small subspace, so for critical components they should complement structured proofs rather than replace them."}, {"title": "Compilation and Transpilation — Exercise 10", "chapter": "Compilation and Transpilation", "url": "10-compilation-and-transpilation.html#exercise-10", "text": "Explain what seed_transpiler controls, and design a fair comparison between two compilation strategies that both use randomized passes. What should be reported alongside the best circuit found? seed_transpiler fixes the pseudorandom choices inside randomized passes, such as trial layouts and routing decisions, making a compilation reproducible with the same software version. A fair comparison runs both strategies over the same set of seeds (or the same number of trials) on the same target, and reports the distribution of results (mean, spread, best), the number of seeds tried, the compilation time, and the software versions. Reporting only the best of many seeds for one strategy and a single seed for the other overstates the difference."}, {"title": "Backend Targets", "chapter": "Compilation and Transpilation", "url": "10-compilation-and-transpilation.html#section-10-1", "text": "10.1 Backend Targets Our circuit asks for a CNOT between qubits 0 and 2, but the device has only a line of qubits in which 0 touches 1 and 1 touches 2. Before the compiler can do anything, it needs a precise description of the device. What must that description contain? We consider the operations first, then the connections between qubits. 10.1.1 Native operations Which gates can the device actually perform? In Qiskit the device is called a backend , and its description is called its target . The target lists the native operations together with the qubits on which each one is available. It often adds how long each operation takes and how accurately it was last calibrated. Is a list of gate names enough? No, because an operation may exist on some qubits or qubit pairs and not on others. That is exactly the constraint our CNOT runs into. To see the problem without contacting a device, the companion builds a toy target by hand. Its native gates are rz , sx , x and cx , where rz is a Z rotation and sx is \\(\\sqrt X\\), a quarter turn about X. Its connections form a line of three qubits. 10.1.2 Connectivity and device constraints Before asking how to run our CNOT, we must say which qubit is meant. The qubits of our program are logical qubits : variables of the algorithm. The device’s qubits are physical qubits : places where the computation happens. A compiler chooses which physical qubit holds each logical one, and that correspondence can change as the circuit runs. Now suppose logical qubits 0, 1 and 2 sit on physical qubits 0, 1 and 2 of the line. The CNOT from 0 to 2 cannot be applied directly. One way out is to swap the states of physical qubits 1 and 2. Logical qubit 2 then sits next to logical qubit 0, and the CNOT can act on physical qubits 0 and 1. A swap is three CNOTs, so this one logical CNOT becomes four. Some devices also allow a two-qubit gate in only one direction, and reversing it costs extra one-qubit gates. These costs depend on the circuit and on where we place it. Problem 10.1. Why may a native gate count exceed a logical count? Worked solution A logical gate may require decomposition or routing. The two counts use different operation vocabularies."}, {"title": "Transpilation", "chapter": "Compilation and Transpilation", "url": "10-compilation-and-transpilation.html#section-10-2", "text": "10.2 Transpilation We now know what the device offers and what our circuit asks for. Rewriting one into the other is called transpilation . It has to solve two problems in our example: H is not a native gate, and the CNOT acts on qubits that are not neighbours. We take the gate problem first, then the placement problem. 10.2.1 Decomposition and synthesis How does the compiler run H on a device whose only one-qubit gates are rz , sx and x ? It rewrites H in those gates. The compiled circuit contains \\(R_z(\\pi/2)\\,\\sqrt X\\,R_z(\\pi/2)\\), which equals H up to a global phase of \\(\\pi/4\\), and Qiskit records that phase with the circuit. Breaking an operation into a known sequence of smaller ones is called decomposition . Some operations have no exact short decomposition in the available gates. For these, the compiler constructs a sequence that approximates the operation within a stated error. That is called synthesis . Transpilation combines decomposition and synthesis with placement, routing, and simplification. Its optimization level , from 0 to 3, selects how much effort these steps use. A higher level tries harder, but it does not guarantee a smaller or better circuit for every input. 10.2.2 Layout, routing, and optimization Now the CNOT. The compiler first fixes an initial layout , which places each logical qubit on a physical one. The companion fixes it to the identity, logical \\(k\\) on physical \\(k\\). Then routing inserts swaps wherever a two-qubit gate acts on non-neighbours. The compiled circuit for the line is exactly the plan we worked out by hand: global phase: π/4\n         ┌─────────┐┌────┐┌─────────┐\nq_0 -> 0 ┤ Rz(π/2) ├┤ √X ├┤ Rz(π/2) ├──■──\n         └─────────┘├───┬┘└─────────┘┌─┴─┐\nq_1 -> 1 ─────■─────┤ X ├──────■─────┤ X ├\n            ┌─┴─┐   └─┬─┘    ┌─┴─┐   └───┘\nq_2 -> 2 ───┤ X ├─────■──────┤ X ├────────\n            └───┘            └───┘ Three CNOTs swap physical qubits 1 and 2, and the fourth acts on physical 0 and 1. The swap is never undone, so the circuit ends with logical qubit 1 on physical qubit 2 and logical qubit 2 on physical qubit 1. This leftover rearrangement is the final layout , which the companion prints as [0, 2, 1] . The final layout has a consequence for testing. If we compared the compiled circuit’s matrix directly with the original, the swapped wires would make a correct compilation look wrong. Operator.from_circuit reads the layout recorded with the compiled circuit and undoes it, so the comparison is between the same logical qubits. Problem 10.2. What does an optimization level guarantee? Worked solution It selects a compilation strategy; it does not certify a globally minimal circuit or better physical performance on every backend."}, {"title": "Compiler Inspection", "chapter": "Compilation and Transpilation", "url": "10-compilation-and-transpilation.html#section-10-3", "text": "10.3 Compiler Inspection The compiler made choices: a layout, a route, a decomposition. Can we control those choices, reproduce them and report what they cost? We first look at the object that carries out the compilation, then check its output for meaning and count its resources. 10.3.1 Pass managers What actually runs when we compile? Transpilation is a sequence of steps called passes . Some passes analyse the circuit, for example by finding which gates act on non-neighbours. Others transform it, by choosing a layout, inserting swaps, or decomposing gates. An object that holds the passes and runs them in order is a pass manager . Qiskit’s convenience function transpile builds a standard pass manager for us and runs it. The companion builds that pass manager explicitly with generate_preset_pass_manager , giving it the optimization level, the native gates, the connections, the initial layout and a seed. Then pass_manager.run(qc) compiles the circuit. Some passes make random choices, and fixing seed_transpiler makes those choices reproducible within the tested implementation. Running several seeds and keeping the best result is a legitimate search, but its classical cost belongs in the report. The companion wraps this in a function, compile_for(coupling_map) , so that it can compile the same circuit for two connection patterns. CouplingMap.from_line(3) is our line. CouplingMap.from_full(3) connects every pair of qubits. 10.3.2 Logical equivalence and physical resource counts Does the compiled circuit still mean the original? For each connection pattern, the companion checks that Operator.from_circuit(compiled) is equivalent to the logical operator. Where does the extra cost come from? On the full pattern, the CNOT needs no routing and the count stays at one. On the line, it becomes four, as we predicted. The single-qubit gates are the same in both, so the difference is entirely the cost of connectivity. \"\"\"Runnable companion for Quantum Programming, Chapter 10.\"\"\"\nfrom qiskit import QuantumCircuit\nfrom qiskit.transpiler import CouplingMap, generate_preset_pass_manager\nfrom qiskit.quantum_info import Operator\n\nqc = QuantumCircuit(3)\nqc.h(0)\nqc.cx(0, 2)\n\ndef compile_for(coupling_map):\n    # The same preset pipeline that transpile() builds internally.\n    pass_manager = generate_preset_pass_manager(\n        optimization_level=1, basis_gates=[\"rz\", \"sx\", \"x\", \"cx\"],\n        coupling_map=coupling_map, initial_layout=[0, 1, 2], seed_transpiler=19)\n    return pass_manager.run(qc)\n\nline = compile_for(CouplingMap.from_line(3))\nfull = compile_for(CouplingMap.from_full(3))\nfor compiled in (line, full):\n    assert Operator.from_circuit(compiled).equiv(Operator(qc))\nassert full.count_ops()[\"cx\"] == 1 and line.count_ops()[\"cx\"] > 1\nprint(\"Logical\", qc.count_ops())\nprint(\"Line\", line.count_ops(), \"full\", full.count_ops())\nprint(\"Line final layout:\", line.layout.final_index_layout()) Companion script: chapter_10.py . For larger circuits, the full matrix comparison is impossible, by the \\(4^n\\) count we derived for operators. Then we rely on tests of each component, structured tests on chosen inputs, and checks of observables, each stated with its limits. A resource report should also include any added ancillas, the routing, the precision of any synthesis, and the target it assumes. Problem 10.3. Why record the layout with an experiment? Worked solution It tells which physical wires realize the logical ones and is needed to interpret observables and sometimes output mappings correctly. Our compiled circuit is ready for a device, but so far the device was a description we made up. Running on real hardware needs an account, a choice of backend, and a budget. How do we run a tested program there and interpret what comes back? Sources and further study: IBM, transpiler API ."}, {"title": "Quantum Hardware Execution — Exercise 1", "chapter": "Quantum Hardware Execution", "url": "11-quantum-hardware-execution.html#exercise-1", "text": "A three-qubit GHZ circuit is compiled for a two-qubit synthetic backend, GenericBackendV2(2). What happens, and what should the program check before compiling for a real backend? The pass manager raises a TranspilerError (“Number of qubits greater than device”). Before compiling, a program should check width, supported instructions (including any dynamic-circuit features), and connectivity, and fail with a clear message rather than attempt a submission that cannot run."}, {"title": "Quantum Hardware Execution — Exercise 2", "chapter": "Quantum Hardware Execution", "url": "11-quantum-hardware-execution.html#exercise-2", "text": "List the information that should be saved with every hardware job so that its results can be interpreted and compared later. Save the job identifier immediately; the backend name; submission and execution times; package versions; the compiled (ISA) circuit and its layout; shots and primitive options; any mitigation settings; the returned metadata; and the raw results. Device calibrations drift, so without this record a later run, or the same circuit on another device, cannot be compared meaningfully."}, {"title": "Quantum Hardware Execution — Exercise 3", "chapter": "Quantum Hardware Execution", "url": "11-quantum-hardware-execution.html#exercise-3", "text": "A study plans \\(50\\) circuits with \\(4096\\) shots each, and each shot takes about \\(200\\) μs on the device, including reset. Estimate the device time. Why is this only a lower bound on the cost? About \\(50\\times4096\\times2\\times10^{-4}\\ \\mathrm s\\approx41\\) s of shot time. Actual usage also includes per-job and per-circuit overhead, classical processing, possible repetitions, and calibration circuits for mitigation; queue time adds wall-clock delay. Budgets should be estimated before submission rather than discovered afterwards."}, {"title": "Quantum Hardware Execution — Exercise 4", "chapter": "Quantum Hardware Execution", "url": "11-quantum-hardware-execution.html#exercise-4", "text": "A circuit flips logical qubit 0 and is compiled with initial_layout [2, 0, 1], so logical qubit 0 sits on physical qubit 2. (a) Evaluate the logical observable IIZ (Z on logical qubit 0) on the compiled circuit without remapping. (b) Remap it with apply_layout and evaluate again. (c) Which value is correct? (a) Unmapped, IIZ measures physical qubit 0, which holds logical qubit 1 in \\(\\ket0\\): the result is \\(+1\\). (b) apply_layout turns it into ZII, Z on physical qubit 2, and the result is \\(-1\\). (c) \\(-1\\) is correct: logical qubit 0 was flipped. An Estimator workload on a compiled circuit must use observables mapped through the layout, or it silently measures the wrong qubits."}, {"title": "Quantum Hardware Execution — Exercise 5", "chapter": "Quantum Hardware Execution", "url": "11-quantum-hardware-execution.html#exercise-5", "text": "Save a compiled circuit for provenance using Qiskit’s QPY format, reload it, and check that the reloaded circuit equals the original. Why store the compiled circuit rather than only the source code that produced it? QPY serialization round-trips the compiled circuit exactly, including parameters and layout information, and the reloaded circuit compares equal. Storing the compiled circuit records what actually ran: recompiling the same source later can give a different layout or gate sequence, because of different software versions, different backend calibrations, or seeds. Results are about executed circuits, not intended ones."}, {"title": "Quantum Hardware Execution — Exercise 6", "chapter": "Quantum Hardware Execution", "url": "11-quantum-hardware-execution.html#exercise-6", "text": "Compile the Bell circuit for GenericBackendV2(2, seed=3) and inspect the operation counts. Which native gates appear, and how many two-qubit gates does the ISA circuit contain? The backend’s operations include cx, rz, sx, x, reset, delay, and measure. The compiled Bell circuit contains one CNOT, two RZ, one SX, a barrier, and two measurements: the Hadamard was rewritten in RZ and SX. Inspecting the ISA circuit before submission confirms that only supported instructions remain and gives the counts that a resource estimate needs."}, {"title": "Quantum Hardware Execution — Exercise 7", "chapter": "Quantum Hardware Execution", "url": "11-quantum-hardware-execution.html#exercise-7", "text": "Simulate the Bell circuit on a noisy model of the synthetic backend (AerSimulator.from_backend). Estimate the fraction of shots giving 00 or 11 with its standard error. What does this number describe, and what does it not? With \\(4000\\) shots the success fraction is about \\(0.99\\) with a standard error near \\(0.0015\\). It describes this synthetic noise model under these settings: the error rates of GenericBackendV2 are generated, not measured on any device. It does not predict a real backend’s performance, and population success alone would not distinguish a Bell state from a classical mixture; that needs a second measurement basis."}, {"title": "Quantum Hardware Execution — Exercise 8", "chapter": "Quantum Hardware Execution", "url": "11-quantum-hardware-execution.html#exercise-8", "text": "A student reports that “the device gave \\(99\\%\\) Bell fidelity,” based on a run with GenericBackendV2. What is wrong with the claim, and what would a hardware claim need? GenericBackendV2 is a synthetic backend with generated properties; no device was involved, so nothing was measured about real hardware. The quantity measured, the fraction of 00 and 11, is a population success rate, not a fidelity. A hardware claim needs the device name and date, the compiled circuit and layout, shots and uncertainty, a fidelity estimate that includes coherence (for example from \\(XX\\) and \\(YY\\) measurements as well as \\(ZZ\\)), and a statement of any mitigation applied."}, {"title": "Quantum Hardware Execution — Exercise 9", "chapter": "Quantum Hardware Execution", "url": "11-quantum-hardware-execution.html#exercise-9", "text": "Teaching code must never launch hardware work by accident. Write a small guard that raises an error unless submission is explicitly enabled and an exact backend name is given, and test both branches without any account access. The guard returns “local” when submission is not requested, raises a ValueError when submission is requested without a backend name, and returns the backend name otherwise; only then would the caller construct a service and submit. Testing the guard needs no credentials. Keeping the decision in one tested function makes the submission boundary explicit, as the chapter’s companion does with --submit and --backend."}, {"title": "Quantum Hardware Execution — Exercise 10", "chapter": "Quantum Hardware Execution", "url": "11-quantum-hardware-execution.html#exercise-10", "text": "A parameter sweep of \\(100\\) values is to be run on hardware. Compare (a) \\(100\\) separate jobs, each with one bound circuit; (b) one job with a single PUB containing the parameterized circuit and a \\(100\\)-row value array. Which costs are reduced by (b), and which are not? What must still be recorded per parameter value? Option (b) compiles once, submits once, and incurs per-job overhead and queue time once, and its results arrive as one array in parameter order. It does not reduce the shots per value or the device time spent on them. Each value’s result must still be linked to its parameter and to the job’s metadata, and if calibration drifts during a long job, the timing within the job matters too."}, {"title": "Execution Setup", "chapter": "Quantum Hardware Execution", "url": "11-quantum-hardware-execution.html#section-11-1", "text": "11.1 Execution Setup We take the Bell circuit, which we have tested locally. A local simulation and a hardware run of it make different claims: the first predicts what an ideal model does, while the second reports what a particular device did at a particular time. Before any submission we must decide which device to use, how to prove we may use it, and what form of circuit it accepts. We take the device and the account first, then the circuit. 11.1.1 Backend selection and credentials Which backend can run our circuit? It needs at least as many qubits as the circuit, and it must support the circuit’s instructions and any classical control the circuit uses. Using it also requires an account, identified by a secret token. Where should that token live? Never in a source file, since source files are shared and stored in version histories. Qiskit Runtime saves the account separately on our computer, and QiskitRuntimeService() reads it from there. A harder question is how to make spending execution time impossible by accident. A script that submitted a job whenever it ran would do so every time someone ran the course checks. So the companion compiles locally by default, and it submits only when the user types two options on the command line: python chapter_11.py --submit --backend NAME . Python’s standard module argparse reads such options. Each add_argument line declares one. action=\"store_true\" makes --submit a switch, true when present and false otherwise. parse_args() reads what the user typed, and parser.error stops the program with a message if --submit comes without a backend name. One more guard matters. Another program may import this file to reuse its build function. Python sets the variable __name__ to \"__main__\" only when a file is run directly, so the block under if __name__ == \"__main__\": never runs on import. Importing the file therefore never submits, and neither does importing the Runtime package. Problem 11.1. Does importing the Runtime package submit a circuit? Worked solution No. A run call on a remote execution object is the submission action. Keep that action separated from module import and local testing. 11.1.2 Executable circuits and mapped observables What form must the circuit take before the backend accepts it? IBM’s devices accept only circuits already written in the backend’s native instructions, on its physical qubits, respecting its connections. Such a circuit is called an ISA circuit , after the backend’s instruction set architecture, its list of native instructions. Producing one is what our pass manager did for the toy line. Here generate_preset_pass_manager(backend=backend, …) reads the native gates and connections from the backend’s own target. For local runs, the companion uses GenericBackendV2(2, seed=3) , an invented two-qubit backend with realistic native gates, rz , sx , x and cx . Compiling for it turns H into Z rotations and a \\(\\sqrt X\\), as before, and the measured Bell circuit needs one CNOT. Compilation also moves qubits, and that affects observables. Suppose we use an Estimator to ask for \\(\\langle ZZ\\rangle\\) on logical qubits 0 and 1, and the layout places them on physical qubits 5 and 6 of a large device. The observable must then act on 5 and 6. observable.apply_layout(compiled.layout) rewrites it accordingly. A Sampler needs no such step, because the measurements are part of the compiled circuit and still write to the bits we chose. Problem 11.2. Why map an Estimator observable after layout? Worked solution The logical qubits may have moved. Measuring Z on a different physical qubit can answer a different question even when the compiled state is correct."}, {"title": "Runtime Workloads", "chapter": "Quantum Hardware Execution", "url": "11-quantum-hardware-execution.html#section-11-2", "text": "11.2 Runtime Workloads With an ISA circuit and a backend, the next step uses real execution time. What happens when we submit, how do we find our job again, and how do we keep the cost under control? We follow a single submission first, then consider how groups of jobs are organized and paid for. 11.2.1 Submitting and retrieving jobs What does a submission look like in code? SamplerV2 from Runtime is the remote counterpart of the local sampler we already know. Its input mode=backend says where its jobs run. Calling run sends the PUBs and returns a job at once, while the work may still wait in a queue. job.job_id() is a string that identifies the job. We print or save it immediately, so that a later program can retrieve the result even if this one stops. job.result() waits for the result, and we read the counts exactly as before. This branch uses a separately configured account. It was checked against the Runtime documentation but not run on hardware for this course. 11.2.2 Execution modes and budgets Suppose a program sends many related jobs, as an optimization loop does. Must each one wait in the queue separately? The mode decides. In job mode, which our companion uses, each job is queued on its own. A session reserves a window on one device, so the jobs of an iterative program run one after another without queuing again. A batch submits a group of independent jobs together, so the service can schedule them efficiently. Which modes are available, and how they are charged, depends on the service and the account. What will a submission cost? We estimate it before submitting, from the number of circuits, the shots, and the expected time on the device. No fixed free allowance should be assumed. A one-line statement can launch a large amount of work, so the explicit submission boundary stays visible in any code we share."}, {"title": "Result Analysis", "chapter": "Quantum Hardware Execution", "url": "11-quantum-hardware-execution.html#section-11-3", "text": "11.3 Result Analysis When counts come back from hardware, what can we claim from them? Two things limit the claim. We can compare a run only with what we recorded about it, and we can compare it with our prediction only after separating sampling fluctuation from device error. We take the record first, then the comparison. 11.3.1 Metadata and provenance Suppose we rerun the job a month later and the counts differ. Is that a bug, or the device? Without a record we cannot tell. Devices are recalibrated often, and their errors drift, so the same source circuit can give different results at different times. So we save the history of the result, its provenance : the backend name, the time of execution, the package versions, the compiled circuit and its layout, the shots, the options, and the metadata the service returns. The compiled circuit matters most, because it is what actually ran. A table of counts without these facts cannot be compared reliably with another run. 11.3.2 Simulation, hardware, and uncertainty How should hardware counts be compared with our prediction? The ideal Bell state gives 00 and 11 with probability one half each, and never 01 or 10 . Sampling alone moves the frequency of 00 by a standard error of \\(\\sqrt{0.25/1024}=1/64\\approx0.016\\) at 1024 shots. But sampling can never produce 01 or 10 from the ideal state. So if those strings appear, the cause is the device, not the finite number of shots. Telling apart the kinds of device error is a question we take up with noise. The companion’s local path compiles for the invented backend and prints the compiled gate counts: two Z rotations, one \\(\\sqrt X\\), one CNOT, the two measurements and a barrier. The remote path compiles for the chosen backend, submits 1024 shots, prints the job identifier and then the counts. Only the local path runs in the automated checks. \"\"\"Runnable companion for Quantum Programming, Chapter 11.\"\"\"\nimport argparse\nfrom qiskit import QuantumCircuit\nfrom qiskit.transpiler import generate_preset_pass_manager\nfrom qiskit.providers.fake_provider import GenericBackendV2\nfrom qiskit_ibm_runtime import QiskitRuntimeService, SamplerV2\n\ndef build(backend):\n    circuit = QuantumCircuit(2)\n    circuit.h(0); circuit.cx(0, 1); circuit.measure_all()\n    return generate_preset_pass_manager(backend=backend, optimization_level=1,\n                                        seed_transpiler=17).run(circuit)\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser()\n    parser.add_argument(\"--submit\", action=\"store_true\", help=\"Explicitly submit a paid/allocated hardware job\")\n    parser.add_argument(\"--backend\", help=\"Exact accessible hardware backend name\")\n    args = parser.parse_args()\n    if not args.submit:\n        compiled = build(GenericBackendV2(2, seed=3))\n        print(\"Local compilation only; no account accessed and no job submitted.\")\n        print(compiled.count_ops())\n    else:\n        if not args.backend:\n            parser.error(\"--backend is required with --submit\")\n        service = QiskitRuntimeService()  # Uses a separately configured account.\n        backend = service.backend(args.backend)\n        compiled = build(backend)\n        job = SamplerV2(mode=backend).run([compiled], shots=1024)\n        print(\"Submitted job:\", job.job_id())\n        print(job.result()[0].data.meas.get_counts()) Companion script: chapter_11.py . Problem 11.3. What is missing from a claim that hardware “gave 95% success”? Worked solution The task, success definition, shots and uncertainty, device and time, circuit and layout, execution options, and comparison baseline are all needed. All our circuits so far measured only at the end. Some hardware can measure a qubit partway through a circuit and act on the result before the circuit finishes. How can a measurement change the remaining computation? Sources and further study: IBM, Runtime SamplerV2 ."}, {"title": "Dynamic Circuits — Exercise 1", "chapter": "Dynamic Circuits", "url": "12-dynamic-circuits.html#exercise-1", "text": "In the companion’s teleportation, Alice obtains \\(c_0=1\\) and \\(c_1=0\\). Which state does Bob hold before correction, and which correction does the program apply? Bob holds \\(Z\\ket\\psi\\) (branch operator \\(X^0Z^1\\)). The \\(X\\) condition on \\(c_1\\) does not fire, the \\(Z\\) condition on \\(c_0\\) does, and \\(Z\\cdot Z\\ket\\psi=\\ket\\psi\\). The verification measurement on Bob then gives \\(0\\)."}, {"title": "Dynamic Circuits — Exercise 2", "chapter": "Dynamic Circuits", "url": "12-dynamic-circuits.html#exercise-2", "text": "Prepare a Bell pair and reset qubit 0. Using DensityMatrix.evolve, find the joint state afterwards. What happened to qubit 1’s reduced state and to the correlation? The joint state becomes \\(\\ket0\\bra0\\otimes I/2\\): qubit 0 is \\(\\ket0\\), qubit 1 is maximally mixed, and the correlation is gone. Qubit 1’s reduced state is unchanged, as no-signalling requires, but the reset destroyed the entanglement. Reset is a physical, irreversible operation, not a reassignment of a variable."}, {"title": "Dynamic Circuits — Exercise 3", "chapter": "Dynamic Circuits", "url": "12-dynamic-circuits.html#exercise-3", "text": "The companion’s counts are written c2 c1 c0. Decode the key 010: which corrections were applied on those shots, and did the verification succeed? c2 \\(=0\\), c1 \\(=1\\), c0 \\(=0\\). Alice’s records called for an X correction and no Z correction, and the verification bit c2 \\(=0\\) shows that Bob’s corrected state matched the input. Decoding must use the register order; reading the key left to right as c0, c1, c2 would misidentify both the branch and the result."}, {"title": "Dynamic Circuits — Exercise 4", "chapter": "Dynamic Circuits", "url": "12-dynamic-circuits.html#exercise-4", "text": "Swap the correction rules in the teleportation program, so that X is conditioned on c0 and Z on c1. Run it in Aer and inspect the verification bit for each branch. Which branches fail, and why does one branch still pass? Branches \\(c_1c_0=00\\) and \\(11\\) still verify (c2 \\(=0\\)): branch \\(00\\) needs no correction, and in branch \\(11\\) the swapped rules apply both corrections in the opposite order, which differs only by a global sign. Branches \\(01\\) and \\(10\\) receive the wrong Pauli and fail most of the time. A test that only looked at the aggregate success fraction could be fooled; checking every branch separately exposes the bug immediately."}, {"title": "Dynamic Circuits — Exercise 5", "chapter": "Dynamic Circuits", "url": "12-dynamic-circuits.html#exercise-5", "text": "Rewrite teleportation with deferred measurement: replace the classically conditioned X and Z by cx(1, 2) and cz(0, 2) applied before any measurement. Verify with Statevector that, after the inverse test preparation on Bob’s qubit, Bob always reads \\(0\\). What practical difference remains between the two versions? With cx(1, 2) and cz(0, 2) in place of the conditional corrections, Bob’s qubit ends in \\(\\ket\\psi\\) for every branch, and after the inverse preparation it reads \\(0\\) with probability \\(1\\); the circuit no longer needs mid-circuit measurement or feedback. The difference is physical: the deferred version needs quantum two-qubit gates between Alice’s and Bob’s qubits, so it cannot be used at a distance, while the dynamic version needs only classical communication, at the cost of measurement and feedback latency."}, {"title": "Dynamic Circuits — Exercise 6", "chapter": "Dynamic Circuits", "url": "12-dynamic-circuits.html#exercise-6", "text": "Reuse one qubit for two independent random bits: apply H, measure into c0, reset, apply H again, and measure into c1. Run it in Aer and check that the four outcomes are about equally frequent. Is the reset necessary here? Would it be if the second H were replaced by ry(\\(\\pi/3\\))? The four outcomes occur about \\(1000\\) times each in \\(4000\\) shots: two independent fair bits from one qubit. In this particular example the reset happens not to matter, because H maps both \\(\\ket0\\) and \\(\\ket1\\) to states with equal probabilities. With ry(\\(\\pi/3\\)) as the second step it would matter: from \\(\\ket0\\) the second bit is \\(1\\) with probability \\(\\frac14\\), but from \\(\\ket1\\) with probability \\(\\frac34\\), so without reset the second bit would depend on the first. Reset returns the reused qubit to the known state that the next step assumes; mid-circuit measurement and reset let a small device act like a larger one over time."}, {"title": "Dynamic Circuits — Exercise 7", "chapter": "Dynamic Circuits", "url": "12-dynamic-circuits.html#exercise-7", "text": "Implement active reset with feedback: prepare \\(\\ket+\\), measure into c0, and apply X if c0 is \\(1\\); then measure again into c1. Show that c1 is always \\(0\\). How does this differ from the reset instruction, and what can make it fail on hardware? The second measurement always gives \\(0\\) in simulation: the qubit is returned to \\(\\ket0\\) whatever the first outcome. This is how reset is often implemented physically. On hardware it fails when the first measurement reports the wrong value (the correction is then applied or skipped wrongly), when the qubit relaxes or is excited during the feedback delay, or when the measurement leaves it outside the qubit subspace. The reset instruction hides these details, but they still set its error rate."}, {"title": "Dynamic Circuits — Exercise 8", "chapter": "Dynamic Circuits", "url": "12-dynamic-circuits.html#exercise-8", "text": "A student says: “I don’t need feedforward for teleportation. I can run the circuit without corrections and afterwards keep only the shots where Alice measured 00.” Compare the fraction of usable shots in the two approaches, and explain why the postselected version is not teleportation. Postselection keeps only the \\(25\\%\\) of shots in branch \\(00\\), and it decides after the run which shots to keep: in any single run Bob’s qubit was wrong three times out of four, and he could not have used it for anything before the data were sorted. Feedforward corrects every branch during the run, so all shots deliver the state and Bob’s qubit is usable immediately. Postselection estimates what teleportation would give; it does not perform the task."}, {"title": "Dynamic Circuits — Exercise 9", "chapter": "Dynamic Circuits", "url": "12-dynamic-circuits.html#exercise-9", "text": "In dynamic teleportation, Bob’s qubit must wait for Alice’s measurement (\\(1\\) μs) and the classical decision (\\(0.5\\) μs). (a) With \\(T_2=100\\) μs, estimate the coherence loss during the wait. (b) Compare with the deferred version, which instead adds two two-qubit gates with error \\(5\\times10^{-3}\\) each. (c) Which is better here, and what changes the answer? (a) \\(1-e^{-1.5/100}\\approx1.5\\times10^{-2}\\). (b) About \\(10^{-2}\\) from the two extra gates, if the qubits can interact at all. (c) Here the deferred version is slightly better, but only when quantum gates between the parties are possible; with faster feedback or longer \\(T_2\\), the dynamic version wins, and at a distance it is the only option. Latency is a resource to budget, like gate error."}, {"title": "Dynamic Circuits — Exercise 10", "chapter": "Dynamic Circuits", "url": "12-dynamic-circuits.html#exercise-10", "text": "Validate teleportation more strongly by teleporting half of an entangled pair. Let qubit 3 be a reference entangled with the input qubit 0 in a Bell state. After teleportation and corrections, undo the Bell preparation on the reference and Bob’s qubit and measure both. What outcome indicates success, and why is this test stronger than teleporting one known state? After corrections, cx(3, 2) and h(3) map the reference–Bob Bell pair back to \\(\\ket{00}\\), so both final measurements give \\(0\\) on every shot. Preserving entanglement with an untouched reference tests the channel on all input states at once (it is equivalent to checking the full process), whereas one known test state checks only one input. Any error that would corrupt some inputs shows up as nonzero outcomes here."}, {"title": "Mid-Circuit Operations", "chapter": "Dynamic Circuits", "url": "12-dynamic-circuits.html#section-12-1", "text": "12.1 Mid-Circuit Operations A circuit with a measurement in the middle keeps running after the measurement. What becomes of the measured qubit and its result, and how can later gates use that result? We look at the measurement and the reset first, then at conditions that read a measured bit. 12.1.1 Measurement and reset What does a measurement in the middle of a circuit do? It writes a classical bit, and it leaves the measured qubit in the basis state it reported. The other qubits stay available for later gates. A reset then puts a qubit back in \\(\\ket0\\), so that it can be reused. If that qubit was entangled with others, the reset destroys the correlation, as we saw when we found that a reset has no inverse. Both are physical operations on a device. Neither is a Python assignment. Which simulator can run such circuits? StatevectorSampler is meant for circuits measured only at the end. For circuits whose later gates depend on mid-circuit results, the companion uses Aer’s AerSimulator . It follows each shot down the branch selected by that shot’s measurement results. 12.1.2 Classical conditions and feedback Suppose we want a qubit to end in \\(\\ket0\\) whatever it held: measure it, and apply X if the result was 1. Could we write that with an ordinary Python if bit == 1: qc.x(0) ? No. That line runs at construction time, when the circuit has not been executed and no bit exists. The condition has to be part of the circuit, so that the controller evaluates it during the run. Qiskit writes such a condition as a block: with qc.if_test((qc.clbits[0], 1)):\n    qc.x(0) Python’s with statement opens a context, and the indented lines run inside it. Here the context is if_test , and it changes what appending means. The gates appended inside the block are not applied unconditionally. They are recorded into the circuit as the body of a condition. The tuple (qc.clbits[0], 1) names the condition: classical bit 0 equals 1. Using a measurement result to choose later operations is called feedback , or feedforward . The condition must name the exact classical bit that the measurement map writes to."}, {"title": "Conditional Programs", "chapter": "Dynamic Circuits", "url": "12-dynamic-circuits.html#section-12-2", "text": "12.2 Conditional Programs With feedback we can program protocols that branch on measurement results. Teleportation is the standard example: Alice sends a qubit’s state to Bob using a shared Bell pair and two classical bits. Which correction belongs to which bit, and how can a program show that all four branches work? We derive the corrections first, then verify each branch. 12.2.1 Teleportation The companion places the input \\(\\ket\\psi=\\alpha\\ket0+\\beta\\ket1\\) on q0. A Bell pair \\((\\ket{00}+\\ket{11})/\\sqrt2\\) is made on q1 and q2 with H on q1 and a CNOT from q1 to q2. Alice holds q1, and Bob holds q2. Alice then applies a CNOT from q0 to q1 and H on q0, and measures q0 into c0 and q1 into c1. Call those results \\(m\\) and \\(n\\). The figure uses the same names. What state does Bob hold in each branch? Writing the qubits in the order q0, q1, q2, the CNOT gives \\[\\tfrac1{\\sqrt2}\\bigl[\\alpha\\ket0(\\ket{00}+\\ket{11})+\\beta\\ket1(\\ket{10}+\\ket{01})\\bigr].\\] H on q0 replaces \\(\\ket0\\) by \\((\\ket0+\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\) and \\(\\ket1\\) by \\((\\ket0-\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\). Collecting the terms by the values \\(m,n\\) of q0 and q1, Bob’s qubit is left in \\[m n=00:\\ \\alpha\\ket0+\\beta\\ket1,\\quad 01:\\ \\alpha\\ket1+\\beta\\ket0,\\quad 10:\\ \\alpha\\ket0-\\beta\\ket1,\\quad 11:\\ \\alpha\\ket1-\\beta\\ket0,\\] each with probability \\(1/4\\). These are \\(X^nZ^m\\ket\\psi\\). So Bob applies X when \\(n=1\\) and then Z when \\(m=1\\). This gives \\(Z^mX^n\\,X^nZ^m\\ket\\psi=\\ket\\psi\\) exactly in every branch. In the program, the two corrections are two if_test blocks, on qc.clbits[1] for X and on qc.clbits[0] for Z. Exchanging the two records would apply the wrong correction in branches 01 and 10. Problem 12.1. Why apply Z controlled by c0 rather than c1? Worked solution The H measurement on the original input determines the phase correction. The Bell-half measurement determines the bit-flip correction. Swapping these records fails on general input states. Teleportation with classical feedforward. Alice’s top measurement gives m and the middle gives n; Bob applies X^n then Z^m using the communicated records. No quantum control from the measured wires is implied. 12.2.2 Verifying every branch How can a program check that Bob holds \\(\\ket\\psi\\)? We cannot read amplitudes from a measurement. But if \\(\\ket\\psi\\) was made by a known preparation \\(V\\ket0\\), then applying \\(V^\\dagger\\) to Bob’s qubit returns it to \\(\\ket0\\) exactly when the state is right. A measurement then gives 0 with certainty. The companion prepares \\(\\ket\\psi\\) with \\(R_y(0.7)\\) and then \\(R_z(0.4)\\), so the state has unequal amplitudes and a nontrivial relative phase. After the corrections it applies \\(R_z(-0.4)\\) and then \\(R_y(-0.7)\\) to Bob’s qubit, and measures it into c2. Every shot, in every branch, should give c2 = 0. Why insist on the phase? A missing Z correction changes only the sign of \\(\\beta\\). On a basis state such as \\(\\ket0\\) that change is invisible, and on any state with real amplitudes it is a different but plausible state. A test state with a relative phase exposes it. One test state still does not determine the whole process. Stronger validation repeats the test with other preparations, or with an input entangled with a reference qubit. Problem 12.2. Does Bob receive the state before Alice communicates her outcomes? Worked solution Without the records his state is averaged over the four Pauli branches. The corrections require those classical bits, so the protocol does not signal instantly."}, {"title": "Execution Constraints", "chapter": "Dynamic Circuits", "url": "12-dynamic-circuits.html#section-12-3", "text": "12.3 Execution Constraints Our teleportation program runs on a simulator. A device has to do something harder: measure, send the bit to a classical controller, decide, and apply a gate, all while the other qubits wait. What does that require of a device, and what does it change about how we judge correctness? We take capabilities first, then timing and branch-by-branch checking. 12.3.1 Simulator and backend capabilities If Aer accepts our if_test blocks, will a device accept them too? Not necessarily. A simulator implements the abstract circuit, while a device’s controller supports only certain conditions, certain resets and certain timing. Before submitting, we check what the chosen backend supports. A controller may evaluate only simple classical expressions, so a high-level condition may have to be rewritten. The companion runs locally and says nothing about hardware availability. 12.3.2 Latency, branches, and correctness What does feedback cost? It can save qubits or depth. A measured and reset qubit can be reused, for example, instead of allocating a new one. But each measurement and each classical decision takes time, called latency , and the waiting qubits are exposed to noise during it. How do we read the companion’s counts? Each key lists the classical bits as c2 c1 c0, with c0 on the right, as in our bit-order convention. So key[0] is c2, the verification bit. With seed 23 the run gave about 250 counts in each of the four branches c1 c0 = 00, 01, 10, 11, because Alice’s outcomes are random. Every key began with 0. The final assertion requires key[0] == \"0\" for every key that appeared, using all , which is true only if the test holds for every item. It is a check on every observed branch. A check on the overall fraction of zeros could pass even if one rare branch were always wrong. The circuit is first passed through transpile(qc, sim) , which rewrites it in the simulator’s supported instructions. Aer then returns counts through result().get_counts() , an older result interface than the primitives use. \"\"\"Runnable companion for Quantum Programming, Chapter 12.\"\"\"\nfrom qiskit_aer import AerSimulator\nfrom qiskit import QuantumCircuit, transpile\n\n# q0=input, q1=Alice's half, q2=Bob's half; c0,c1 are correction records.\nqc = QuantumCircuit(3, 3)\nqc.ry(0.7, 0); qc.rz(0.4, 0)\nqc.h(1); qc.cx(1, 2)\nqc.cx(0, 1); qc.h(0)\nqc.measure(0, 0); qc.measure(1, 1)\nwith qc.if_test((qc.clbits[1], 1)):\n    qc.x(2)\nwith qc.if_test((qc.clbits[0], 1)):\n    qc.z(2)\n# Inverse of the known test preparation verifies the recovered state.\nqc.rz(-0.4, 2); qc.ry(-0.7, 2)\nqc.measure(2, 2)\nsim = AerSimulator(seed_simulator=23, max_parallel_threads=1)\ncounts = sim.run(transpile(qc, sim), shots=1024).result().get_counts()\nassert all(key[0] == \"0\" for key in counts)\nprint(\"c2 c1 c0:\", counts) Companion script: chapter_12.py . Problem 12.3. Why test a nontrivial phase as well as real amplitudes? Worked solution A missing Z correction may be invisible on a basis state. A phase-sensitive input exposes that error. A program whose logic is correct can still give wrong counts on a device, because the device is noisy. When a result disagrees with our prediction, how can we tell a noisy experiment from an incorrect program? Sources and further study: IBM, classical feedforward ."}, {"title": "Noise and Reliability — Exercise 1", "chapter": "Noise and Reliability", "url": "13-noise-and-reliability.html#exercise-1", "text": "With the depolarizing convention \\(\\mathcal E(\\rho)=(1-p)\\rho+pI/2\\) attached to X, predict \\(P(1)\\) after X on \\(\\ket0\\) for \\(p=0.3\\), and check it with a noisy Aer simulation. \\(P(1)=0.7+0.15=0.85\\), so the bit is wrong with probability \\(0.15=p/2\\). The simulation with \\(20\\,000\\) shots agrees to within sampling error. Reporting “\\(30\\%\\) depolarizing” without its convention invites a factor-of-two misunderstanding of the error rate."}, {"title": "Noise and Reliability — Exercise 2", "chapter": "Noise and Reliability", "url": "13-noise-and-reliability.html#exercise-2", "text": "A readout error reports 1 with probability \\(0.03\\) for a true \\(0\\) and reports 0 with probability \\(0.05\\) for a true \\(1\\). Predict the reported \\(P(1)\\) for an idle qubit and for X\\(\\ket0\\), and check with Aer’s ReadoutError. Idle qubit: reported \\(P(1)=0.03\\). After X: reported \\(P(1)=0.95\\). Readout errors change only the classical label, and their two directions can differ, so they must be calibrated with both preparations."}, {"title": "Noise and Reliability — Exercise 3", "chapter": "Noise and Reliability", "url": "13-noise-and-reliability.html#exercise-3", "text": "Inverse correction of a symmetric readout error multiplies the variance of the estimated \\(\\langle Z\\rangle\\) by \\(1/(1-2e)^2\\). Evaluate this factor for \\(e=0.05\\) and \\(e=0.3\\). How many more shots are needed to keep the same precision? \\(e=0.05\\): factor \\(1/0.81\\approx1.23\\), so about \\(23\\%\\) more shots. \\(e=0.3\\): factor \\(1/0.16=6.25\\), more than six times the shots. Mitigation trades bias for variance, and the cost grows rapidly as the error approaches \\(\\frac12\\)."}, {"title": "Noise and Reliability — Exercise 4", "chapter": "Noise and Reliability", "url": "13-noise-and-reliability.html#exercise-4", "text": "A noise model contains both a depolarizing error (\\(p=0.1\\)) on X and the asymmetric readout error above. (a) Predict \\(P(1)\\) for an idle qubit and after X. (b) Explain how running both experiments separates gate error from readout error. (c) Check the predictions with Aer. (a) Idle: only readout acts, so \\(P(1)=0.03\\). After X: the true \\(P(1)\\) is \\(0.95\\), and readout reports \\(0.03\\times0.05+0.95\\times0.95\\approx0.904\\). (b) The idle experiment measures readout error alone (no X applied); the X experiment combines both. With readout calibrated from idle and from a separately trusted preparation, the extra loss in the X experiment is attributed to the gate. (c) The simulation gives about \\(0.031\\) and \\(0.902\\)."}, {"title": "Noise and Reliability — Exercise 5", "chapter": "Noise and Reliability", "url": "13-noise-and-reliability.html#exercise-5", "text": "Mitigate the readout error for the state ry(\\(\\pi/3\\))\\(\\ket0\\), whose ideal \\(P(1)\\) is \\(0.25\\). (a) Calibrate the assignment matrix from idle and X runs. (b) Measure the noisy distribution of the test state. (c) Solve the linear system for the corrected distribution and compare with \\(0.25\\). The calibration gives \\(A\\approx\\begin{pmatrix}0.97&0.05\\\\0.03&0.95\\end{pmatrix}\\). The noisy test distribution reports \\(P(1)\\approx0.26\\). Solving \\(Ap=p_{\\rm meas}\\) returns \\(P(1)\\approx0.251\\), close to the ideal \\(0.25\\). The correction assumes that readout errors do not depend on what happened before measurement and that the calibration still describes the device; it removes readout bias but does nothing about gate errors."}, {"title": "Noise and Reliability — Exercise 6", "chapter": "Noise and Reliability", "url": "13-noise-and-reliability.html#exercise-6", "text": "A noise model attaches a strong depolarizing error to the instruction named h. A circuit applies H twice and measures. (a) Run it with the simulator’s default transpilation and with transpilation to the basis rz, sx, x. (b) Explain the different results. (c) What should be checked whenever a noise model is used? (a) With h preserved, the two noisy Hadamards give \\(P(1)\\approx0.37\\); after transpiling to rz, sx, x, the result is \\(P(1)=0\\): no noise at all. (b) The error is attached to the name h, and in the second circuit every H was rewritten as RZ and SX gates, so the model never fires. (c) Inspect the compiled circuit that is actually simulated and confirm that every intended noisy instruction appears with the name used in the model; otherwise a “noisy” simulation can silently be ideal."}, {"title": "Noise and Reliability — Exercise 7", "chapter": "Noise and Reliability", "url": "13-noise-and-reliability.html#exercise-7", "text": "Zero-noise extrapolation: each X carries depolarizing error \\(p=0.05\\), so \\(\\langle Z\\rangle\\) after one X is \\(-(0.95)\\). Amplify the noise by inserting pairs of X gates (logically the identity) to get noise scales \\(\\lambda=1,3,5\\). (a) Predict \\(\\langle Z\\rangle(\\lambda)\\). (b) Estimate the zero-noise value from \\(\\lambda=1,3\\) with the Richardson formula \\((3E_1-E_3)/2\\). (c) Check with Aer and state the assumptions. (a) \\(E(\\lambda)=-(0.95)^\\lambda\\): \\(-0.950\\), \\(-0.857\\), \\(-0.774\\). (b) \\((3(-0.950)-(-0.857))/2\\approx-0.996\\), close to the ideal \\(-1\\). (c) Sampling with \\(2\\times10^5\\) shots reproduces these values. The method assumes the inserted gates scale the noise by exactly \\(\\lambda\\) and that \\(E(\\lambda)\\) is close to linear over the range used; the extrapolated value also has larger statistical error than the raw measurements."}, {"title": "Noise and Reliability — Exercise 8", "chapter": "Noise and Reliability", "url": "13-noise-and-reliability.html#exercise-8", "text": "A student calibrates a symmetric readout flip rate of \\(e=0.10\\) and divides every measured \\(\\langle Z\\rangle\\) by \\(1-2e\\). By the time the experiment runs, the true rate has drifted to \\(0.12\\). (a) What fraction of the true value does the “mitigated” result report? (b) Why does collecting more shots not reveal the problem? (a) The measured mean is \\(0.76\\langle Z\\rangle\\), and dividing by \\(0.80\\) gives \\(0.95\\langle Z\\rangle\\): a \\(5\\%\\) bias remains after “mitigation.” (b) More shots shrink the statistical error around the biased value, making a wrong answer look more precise. Mitigation is only as good as its model and calibration; recalibrating close in time, interleaving calibration circuits, and reporting calibration uncertainty are part of the method."}, {"title": "Noise and Reliability — Exercise 9", "chapter": "Noise and Reliability", "url": "13-noise-and-reliability.html#exercise-9", "text": "Full readout mitigation on \\(n\\) qubits calibrates a \\(2^n\\times2^n\\) assignment matrix. (a) How many calibration circuits and matrix entries does this need for \\(n=5\\) and \\(n=20\\)? (b) If readout errors are independent per qubit, how does a tensor-product model reduce the cost? (c) What error does that assumption risk? (a) \\(n=5\\): \\(32\\) calibration circuits and \\(1024\\) entries. \\(n=20\\): about \\(10^6\\) circuits and \\(10^{12}\\) entries, infeasible. (b) With independent errors the matrix is a tensor product of \\(n\\) one-qubit matrices: two calibration circuits per qubit (or two in total, run in parallel) and \\(4n\\) numbers. (c) Correlated readout errors, such as crosstalk between neighbouring readout resonators, are ignored and leave residual bias. Scalable mitigation trades completeness for assumptions that must themselves be tested."}, {"title": "Noise and Reliability — Exercise 10", "chapter": "Noise and Reliability", "url": "13-noise-and-reliability.html#exercise-10", "text": "An idle qubit suffers quasi-static dephasing that a pair of X gates (an echo) would refocus, reducing the idle error from \\(\\epsilon_{\\rm idle}\\) to nearly zero, but each X adds error \\(5\\times10^{-4}\\). (a) For which \\(\\epsilon_{\\rm idle}\\) does adding the echo help? (b) What if the dephasing noise changes quickly during the idle window? (c) What should a program report when it adds suppression gates? (a) The echo helps when \\(\\epsilon_{\\rm idle}\\) exceeds about \\(10^{-3}\\), the error added by its two gates. (b) If the noise varies during the window, the echo refocuses only part of it, and the break-even idle error is larger; for very fast noise the gates only add error. (c) A baseline without suppression, the added gate count and duration, and measured results for both, so that the benefit is demonstrated rather than assumed."}, {"title": "Noise Models", "chapter": "Noise and Reliability", "url": "13-noise-and-reliability.html#section-13-1", "text": "13.1 Noise Models An X gate should turn \\(\\ket0\\) into \\(\\ket1\\), yet a noisy device sometimes reports 0. Where did the error happen: in the gate, in the readout, or only in our finite sample? Before trying to correct anything, we need a model that separates these causes and predicts their effect. We define the two kinds of error first, then build a simulation that applies one of them. 13.1.1 Gate and readout errors What can go wrong between the X gate and the reported bit? Either the gate leaves the qubit in a different state, or the state is right and the readout reports the wrong label. The first is a gate error , which changes the quantum state before measurement. The second is a readout error , which changes only the classical record. A simple model of a gate error is depolarizing noise. With probability \\(1-\\lambda\\) the gate acts correctly, and with probability \\(\\lambda\\) the qubit is replaced by the completely random state \\(I/2\\). In density-matrix form, \\(\\mathcal E(\\rho)=(1-\\lambda)\\rho+\\lambda I/2\\). The companion uses \\(\\lambda=0.1\\) after X. What probability of 1 should we then see? The intended state is \\(\\ketbra11\\), so the noisy state is \\(0.9\\ketbra11+0.1\\,I/2\\). Its probability of 1 is \\(0.9\\cdot1+0.1\\cdot\\tfrac12=0.95\\). So the parameter 0.1 is not the probability of a wrong bit. Half of the random replacements still give 1, and the wrong-bit probability is 0.05. A model’s parameter means only what its convention says. 13.1.2 Constructing noisy simulations How do we tell a simulator to apply this error? Aer’s NoiseModel attaches errors to instructions by name. add_all_qubit_quantum_error(depolarizing_error(0.1, 1), [\"x\"]) says: after every x instruction on any qubit, apply one-qubit depolarizing noise of strength 0.1. That attachment by name has a trap. Compilation can remove an instruction or replace it by others. If the compiled circuit no longer contains an x , an error attached to x never acts, and the simulation looks cleaner than intended. So we inspect the compiled circuit. The companion compiles with optimization_level=0 , which makes no simplifications, and uses an X that the simulator supports directly, so the error stays attached where we put it. A chosen noise model is a hypothesis about a device’s errors. It is not a calibrated description of any particular device. Problem 13.1. For depolarization strength 0.2 after X, predict P(1). Worked solution The state is 0.8|1〉〈1|+0.2I/2, so P(1)=0.9."}, {"title": "Reliability Techniques", "chapter": "Noise and Reliability", "url": "13-noise-and-reliability.html#section-13-2", "text": "13.2 Reliability Techniques Knowing how noise acts, can we do anything about it? There are two broad answers. We can try to make fewer errors happen during the run, or we can accept the errors and correct the estimated quantity afterwards. Each works only under assumptions about the noise. We take reduction during the run first, then correction afterwards. 13.2.1 Error suppression How can a program make fewer errors happen? The plainest ways reduce exposure. A better layout avoids swaps, and a shorter circuit gives noise less time and fewer gates to act on. Reducing errors during execution in such ways is called error suppression . A less obvious method targets qubits that wait. Suppose an idle qubit slowly picks up an unwanted Z rotation \\(R_z(a)\\) during each half of its waiting time. If we apply X in the middle and again at the end, the total is \\(X\\,R_z(a)\\,X\\,R_z(a)\\). Since \\(XZX=-Z\\), conjugating by X reverses the rotation, \\(XR_z(a)X=R_z(-a)\\), so the total is \\(R_z(-a)R_z(a)=I\\). Inserting such pulses into idle periods is called dynamical decoupling . It works only if the unwanted rotation stays the same over the waiting time. That condition shows the general rule. A suppression technique adds operations and time, and if its assumption fails, the added gates can make the result worse. So we compare against a baseline run without the technique, and count the operations and duration it added. 13.2.2 Error mitigation and its assumptions Suppose instead we accept the errors and correct the estimate afterwards. Estimating an ideal quantity from noisy data in this way is called error mitigation . It works by calibration, by inverting a model of the noise, or by extrapolating from runs at increased noise. How much can it recover, and at what price? Readout errors give a case we can compute exactly. Suppose each reported bit is flipped with probability \\(e\\), the same for 0 and 1. A shot that should give \\(s=\\pm1\\) then reports \\(s\\) with probability \\(1-e\\) and \\(-s\\) with probability \\(e\\). Its expected reported value is \\((1-e)s-es=(1-2e)s\\), so \\[\\langle Z\\rangle_{\\text{observed}}=(1-2e)\\,\\langle Z\\rangle_{\\text{true}}.\\] If we know \\(e\\), dividing the observed mean by \\(1-2e\\) corrects it. The price is variance. Dividing an estimate by \\(1-2e\\) multiplies its variance by \\(1/(1-2e)^2\\). As \\(e\\) approaches one half, the factor grows without bound, because the reported bits then carry almost no information. In practice \\(e\\) is itself estimated by calibration, so its uncertainty, and its drift between calibration and run, add to the error. The correction is right only if the model is right. Problem 13.2. At readout flip rate e=0.1, how does inverse correction scale variance? Worked solution It multiplies variance by 1/0.8²=1.5625, even if e is known exactly."}, {"title": "Interpreting Discrepancies", "chapter": "Noise and Reliability", "url": "13-noise-and-reliability.html#section-13-3", "text": "13.3 Interpreting Discrepancies We can now name four possible causes for a result that disagrees with its prediction: a wrong program, an approximate circuit, random shots, and noise that our model does not describe. How do we decide which one is responsible, and what may we claim once we have? We set out the order of diagnosis first, then what a report must include. 13.3.1 Noise, approximation, and sampling Suppose the noisy X run gives \\(P(1)=0.90\\) instead of 0.95. Where do we look first? We work through the causes from the most controlled experiment to the least. An exact simulation tests the program’s logic, with no sampling and no noise. If the circuit is approximate by design, its known error bound tells us how far the exact result may legitimately move. A sampled ideal simulation adds only shot noise, whose size we can derive. Only then does a noisy simulation test the noise model, and a device test the device. Each step changes one thing, so a discrepancy that first appears at some step points to what that step added. More shots shrink the random fluctuation, but they cannot repair a biased model. Comparing an exact simulator directly with noisy hardware skips every step, and its disagreement diagnoses nothing. 13.3.2 Bias, uncertainty, and reporting limits What does the companion actually establish? It runs 8192 simulated shots and compares the frequency of 1 with the predicted 0.95. The standard error is \\(\\sqrt{0.95\\cdot0.05/8192}\\approx0.0024\\), so the tolerance of 0.03 is about 12 standard errors and deliberately generous. With seed 31 the run gave 0.9485. The check confirms that the simulator applies the model we wrote down, with the convention we derived. It does not show that depolarizing noise describes any device. Nor is its tolerance a confidence interval suitable for other experiments. So with the output we report the model, its parameter convention, the instructions it is attached to after compilation, the seed and the number of shots. A comparison with hardware would add a device, a time and a calibration, and it would need all the records of a hardware run. \"\"\"Runnable companion for Quantum Programming, Chapter 13.\"\"\"\nfrom qiskit_aer import AerSimulator\nfrom qiskit import QuantumCircuit, transpile\nfrom qiskit_aer.noise import NoiseModel, depolarizing_error\n\nqc = QuantumCircuit(1)\nqc.x(0); qc.measure_all()\nnoise = NoiseModel()\nnoise.add_all_qubit_quantum_error(depolarizing_error(0.1, 1), [\"x\"])\nsim = AerSimulator(noise_model=noise, seed_simulator=31, max_parallel_threads=1)\ncounts = sim.run(transpile(qc, sim, optimization_level=0), shots=8192).result().get_counts()\np1 = counts.get(\"1\", 0) / 8192\n# E(rho) = .9 rho + .1 I/2, so expected P(1) = .95.\nassert abs(p1 - 0.95) < 0.03\nprint(\"Simulated P(1):\", p1, \"analytic prediction: 0.95\") Companion script: chapter_13.py . Problem 13.3. Can error mitigation certify a fault-tolerant computation? Worked solution No. Mitigation depends on a noise model and statistical processing. It does not supply an error-correcting logical computation or a general guarantee against arbitrary errors. So far a person read each result and decided what to do next. Some programs let a classical optimizer make that decision, using estimates from a quantum circuit at every step. How do quantum measurements guide a classical optimization loop, and what does the noise in each estimate do to it? Sources and further study: IBM, noise model construction ."}, {"title": "Hybrid Quantum Programs — Exercise 1", "chapter": "Hybrid Quantum Programs", "url": "14-hybrid-quantum-programs.html#exercise-1", "text": "For the trial state with \\(E(\\theta)=-1-\\sin\\theta\\), compute the energy and the parameter-shift gradient \\(\\frac12[E(\\theta+\\pi/2)-E(\\theta-\\pi/2)]\\) at \\(\\theta=\\pi/6\\), using the Estimator. Compare with \\(-\\cos\\theta\\). \\(E(\\pi/6)=-1.5\\) and the parameter-shift gradient is \\(-0.866=-\\cos(\\pi/6)\\), exact for this single-rotation circuit. The rule needs two energy evaluations per parameter, which on hardware means two separately estimated energies, each with shot noise."}, {"title": "Hybrid Quantum Programs — Exercise 2", "chapter": "Hybrid Quantum Programs", "url": "14-hybrid-quantum-programs.html#exercise-2", "text": "The companion’s grid has \\(101\\) points on \\([0,\\pi]\\). Estimate the worst-case energy error at the grid minimum, using \\(E\\approx-2+\\frac12(\\theta-\\pi/2)^2\\) near the optimum. Is the grid fine enough here? The spacing is about \\(0.031\\), so the nearest grid point is at most \\(0.016\\) from \\(\\pi/2\\), giving an energy error of at most \\(\\frac12(0.016)^2\\approx1.2\\times10^{-4}\\). Here \\(\\pi/2\\) is actually a grid point (index \\(50\\)), so the error is zero. Energy is quadratic near a minimum, so modest grids give accurate energies, though less accurate parameters."}, {"title": "Hybrid Quantum Programs — Exercise 3", "chapter": "Hybrid Quantum Programs", "url": "14-hybrid-quantum-programs.html#exercise-3", "text": "A gradient-based optimization uses \\(10\\) parameters, the parameter-shift rule, and \\(50\\) iterations, each energy estimated with \\(10^4\\) shots. How many energy evaluations and shots does it need, ignoring line searches and restarts? \\(2\\times10\\times50=1000\\) energy evaluations, or \\(10^7\\) shots, plus any evaluations of the energy itself for stopping decisions. The classical loop looks short, but the quantum cost scales with parameters, iterations, and precision, and must be budgeted before running on hardware."}, {"title": "Hybrid Quantum Programs — Exercise 4", "chapter": "Hybrid Quantum Programs", "url": "14-hybrid-quantum-programs.html#exercise-4", "text": "Implement gradient descent with the parameter-shift rule, starting at \\(\\theta=0.2\\), for \\(20\\) steps. Compare learning rates \\(\\eta=0.01\\), \\(1.0\\), and \\(2.5\\). Which reaches the minimum, and why do the others fail? With \\(\\eta=1\\), the iteration reaches \\(\\theta=\\pi/2\\) and \\(E=-2\\). With \\(\\eta=0.01\\) it barely moves (to about \\(0.39\\)) in \\(20\\) steps. With \\(\\eta=2.5\\) it overshoots: near the minimum the error is multiplied by about \\(1-\\eta=-1.5\\) each step, so it oscillates and grows instead of converging. Step size must match the landscape’s curvature; the analytic curve lets the program be tested before noise complicates the picture."}, {"title": "Hybrid Quantum Programs — Exercise 5", "chapter": "Hybrid Quantum Programs", "url": "14-hybrid-quantum-programs.html#exercise-5", "text": "Estimate the energy at \\(\\theta=\\pi/3\\) from samples instead of exactly: measure ZZ directly and XX after Hadamards, with \\(4096\\) shots each. (a) What are the exact energy and the single-shot variances? (b) What standard error should the estimate have? (c) Check the sampled estimate. (a) \\(E=-1-\\sin\\frac\\pi3\\approx-1.866\\). The ZZ term has zero variance on this family, and the XX term has variance \\(1-0.75=0.25\\). (b) The standard error is about \\(\\sqrt{0.25/4096}\\approx0.008\\), all from the XX term. (c) The sampled estimate lands within a few hundredths of \\(-1.866\\). A hardware optimizer sees energies with this kind of noise, which affects gradients and stopping rules."}, {"title": "Hybrid Quantum Programs — Exercise 6", "chapter": "Hybrid Quantum Programs", "url": "14-hybrid-quantum-programs.html#exercise-6", "text": "Change the Hamiltonian to \\(H=ZZ-XX\\) while keeping the same ansatz. (a) Evaluate the ansatz energy over a full grid \\(\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi]\\) and find its minimum. (b) Compute the true ground energy by diagonalizing \\(H\\). (c) Explain the gap. (a) The ansatz energy is \\(1-\\sin\\theta\\), with minimum \\(0\\). (b) The true ground energy is \\(-2\\), reached by \\((\\ket{01}+\\ket{10})/\\sqrt2\\). (c) The ansatz only produces states with equal bits (\\(ZZ=+1\\)), so it cannot reach the ground state, which has unequal bits. No optimizer can fix an ansatz that excludes the answer; checking against exact diagonalization on small instances exposes the expressiveness gap."}, {"title": "Hybrid Quantum Programs — Exercise 7", "chapter": "Hybrid Quantum Programs", "url": "14-hybrid-quantum-programs.html#exercise-7", "text": "Implement depth-1 QAOA for MaxCut on one edge: H on both qubits, the cost evolution as rzz(\\(-\\gamma\\)), and the mixer as rx(\\(2\\beta\\)) on each qubit. Use the observable \\(C=\\frac12(II-ZZ)\\). Evaluate \\(\\langle C\\rangle\\) at several \\((\\gamma,\\beta)\\) and compare with \\(F=\\frac12+\\frac12\\sin4\\beta\\sin\\gamma\\). Watch the parameter order. Because the circuit’s parameters are ordered beta, gamma, each value row must be written as \\([\\beta,\\gamma]\\). With that order, the Estimator reproduces \\(F\\) exactly: \\(1\\) at \\((\\gamma,\\beta)=(\\pi/2,\\pi/8)\\), \\(0.854\\) at \\((\\pi/2,\\pi/16)\\) and at \\((\\pi/4,\\pi/8)\\), and \\(0\\) at \\((\\pi/2,3\\pi/8)\\). Supplying \\([\\gamma,\\beta]\\) instead gives \\(0.5\\) everywhere for these points, a silent error that the analytic comparison catches."}, {"title": "Hybrid Quantum Programs — Exercise 8", "chapter": "Hybrid Quantum Programs", "url": "14-hybrid-quantum-programs.html#exercise-8", "text": "A noisy hardware run reports \\(E=-2.03\\pm0.02\\) for \\(H=-ZZ-XX\\), whose true ground energy is \\(-2\\). A student concludes that the optimizer found “a better solution than the ground state.” Explain what the result actually indicates. No state has energy below \\(-2\\), so the reading is an estimation artifact: \\(-2.03\\) is \\(1.5\\) standard errors below the bound, consistent with statistical fluctuation, or with bias from noise or mitigation. Selecting the lowest of many noisy evaluations also biases the reported minimum downward. The right response is to re-estimate the energy at the chosen parameters with fresh shots and report it with its uncertainty, not to accept the lowest value seen."}, {"title": "Hybrid Quantum Programs — Exercise 9", "chapter": "Hybrid Quantum Programs", "url": "14-hybrid-quantum-programs.html#exercise-9", "text": "Compare gradient estimators under shot noise. Each energy estimate has standard error \\(\\sigma=0.01\\). (a) The parameter-shift rule uses shifts of \\(\\pm\\pi/2\\). Find the standard error of its gradient. (b) A central finite difference uses \\(\\pm h\\) with \\(h=0.01\\). Find its standard error. (c) Why is a small \\(h\\) attractive in exact arithmetic but harmful with shots? (a) \\(\\sigma\\sqrt2/2\\approx0.007\\). (b) \\(\\sigma\\sqrt2/(2h)\\approx0.71\\), a hundred times larger than the gradient’s typical size. (c) Small \\(h\\) reduces the truncation error of a finite difference, but it divides the noise by \\(h\\). Parameter shift is exact for Pauli-rotation parameters and uses large shifts, so it does not amplify noise. Gradient methods must be chosen for noisy evaluations, not exact ones."}, {"title": "Hybrid Quantum Programs — Exercise 10", "chapter": "Hybrid Quantum Programs", "url": "14-hybrid-quantum-programs.html#exercise-10", "text": "An optimization is run from \\(20\\) random initial points and the best result is reported. Each run uses \\(500\\) energy evaluations of \\(10^4\\) shots. (a) What is the total quantum cost? (b) If \\(15\\) runs converge to a local minimum and \\(5\\) to the global one, what should the report include? (c) Why does reporting only the best run misrepresent the method? (a) \\(20\\times500\\times10^4=10^8\\) shots. (b) The success rate (\\(5/20\\)), the distribution of final energies, the evaluation budget per run, the seeds, and the total cost. (c) The best run alone hides the \\(75\\%\\) failure rate and the twentyfold cost of finding it; a different method that succeeds from every start may be better even if its best result is identical."}, {"title": "Parameterized Workflows", "chapter": "Hybrid Quantum Programs", "url": "14-hybrid-quantum-programs.html#section-14-1", "text": "14.1 Parameterized Workflows A variational program repeatedly prepares a state that depends on parameters and asks for its energy. If we started on a problem we could not solve, a wrong circuit and a weak optimizer would look the same. So we start with an energy we can compute by hand at every angle, and then decide how to request many evaluations of it. 14.1.1 Parameter binding and objectives We apply \\(R_y(\\theta)\\) to qubit 0 and then a CNOT from qubit 0 to qubit 1. This prepares \\(\\cos(\\theta/2)\\ket{00}+\\sin(\\theta/2)\\ket{11}\\). For the observable \\(H=-ZZ-XX\\), what energy \\(E(\\theta)=\\langle H\\rangle\\) should the program report? Write \\(c=\\cos(\\theta/2)\\) and \\(s=\\sin(\\theta/2)\\). Both \\(\\ket{00}\\) and \\(\\ket{11}\\) have equal bits, so \\(\\langle ZZ\\rangle=c^2+s^2=1\\). The operator \\(XX\\) exchanges \\(\\ket{00}\\) and \\(\\ket{11}\\), so \\(\\langle XX\\rangle=2cs=\\sin\\theta\\). Hence \\[E(\\theta)=-1-\\sin\\theta,\\] with its minimum \\(-2\\) at \\(\\theta=\\pi/2\\), where the state is the Bell pair. This curve is our oracle for testing the program: the computed energies must match it at every angle before we trust the loop on anything larger. The figure shows the circuit and the two measurements the energy needs, \\(ZZ\\) directly and \\(XX\\) after H on both wires. A one-parameter entangled trial state: cos(θ/2)|00⟩+sin(θ/2)|11⟩. Measure ZZ directly or rotate both wires with H before measuring XX. Problem 14.1. Why is the energy at θ=0 equal to −1? Worked solution The state is 00, so ZZ has expectation one and XX has expectation zero. 14.1.2 Repeated estimation An optimizer may ask for the energy at hundreds of angles. Should the program build a new circuit for each? No: as with parameter sweeps, we keep one symbolic circuit and pass the angles to the Estimator as an array of parameter rows. The companion does this for a grid of 101 angles. np.linspace(0, np.pi, 101) makes 101 equally spaced angles from 0 to \\(\\pi\\). angles[:, None] turns that flat list into 101 rows of one value each. That is the general shape of a parameter array, one row per experiment and one column per circuit parameter, so the code states its workload explicitly rather than relying on Qiskit to interpret a flat list. Each evaluation has a cost, even when the host loop is a single line. With the exact local Estimator there is no shot noise, which is right for this first debugging stage. On hardware, every energy would be an estimate, and the program would need to decide how many shots each term gets and how confident each value is."}, {"title": "Optimization Loops", "chapter": "Hybrid Quantum Programs", "url": "14-hybrid-quantum-programs.html#section-14-2", "text": "14.2 Optimization Loops A grid evaluates everywhere. An optimizer instead moves from one guess to a better one, so it needs a direction to move in, and a rule for when to stop. Both must be computed from estimates. We derive the direction first, then turn to stopping and to making runs comparable. 14.2.1 Gradients and classical updates Which way should \\(\\theta\\) move? The derivative \\(E'(\\theta)\\) says, but the circuit gives us values of \\(E\\), not derivatives. Can we get the derivative exactly from a few values? For our curve, try evaluating at \\(\\theta\\pm\\pi/2\\): \\[\\frac{E(\\theta+\\pi/2)-E(\\theta-\\pi/2)}2=\\frac{-\\sin(\\theta+\\pi/2)+\\sin(\\theta-\\pi/2)}2=-\\cos\\theta=E'(\\theta).\\] This is no accident. The rotation \\(R_y(\\theta)\\) has entries \\(\\cos(\\theta/2)\\) and \\(\\pm\\sin(\\theta/2)\\), and the energy is quadratic in them. Products such as \\(\\cos^2(\\theta/2)\\) and \\(\\cos(\\theta/2)\\sin(\\theta/2)\\) are combinations of \\(1\\), \\(\\cos\\theta\\) and \\(\\sin\\theta\\), so \\(E(\\theta)=A+B\\cos\\theta+C\\sin\\theta\\) for some constants. The identity above holds for \\(\\cos\\theta\\) and \\(\\sin\\theta\\) separately, so it holds for every such \\(E\\). This is the parameter-shift rule : for a rotation generated by a single Pauli operator, the exact derivative is half the difference of two energies. A gradient step then sets \\(\\theta\\leftarrow\\theta-\\eta E'(\\theta)\\), where the step size \\(\\eta\\) is called the learning rate . For one parameter, though, the grid is simpler and gives a reproducible baseline. Larger parameter spaces need more careful optimizers. No optimizer, however sophisticated, repairs a wrong circuit or a wrong objective. 14.2.2 Stopping criteria and reproducibility When should the loop stop? “When the energy stops improving” sounds right, but with estimates the energy always fluctuates. An improvement smaller than its standard error is not evidence of progress. So a stopping rule combines a minimum improvement, judged against the uncertainty, with a fixed budget of evaluations. Noise also distorts the direction. Estimated gradients can be biased, and in flat regions they are small compared with their errors, so the loop wanders. To compare two methods fairly, we fix their starting points and seeds. We also record failed runs and how results depend on the starting point. Choosing the best of many seeds afterwards understates both the classical and the quantum cost. Problem 14.2. Can a measured energy below −2 be accepted as a better solution? Worked solution Not for this exact Hamiltonian. It indicates statistical fluctuation, bias, or a programming error; the true minimum is −2."}, {"title": "Applications", "chapter": "Hybrid Quantum Programs", "url": "14-hybrid-quantum-programs.html#section-14-3", "text": "14.3 Applications Our loop is a small instance of methods from the algorithms course. What do those methods promise, and what can a run like ours show about them? We treat the variational eigensolver first, since our example is one, then QAOA and the question of performance. 14.3.1 Variational eigensolvers A variational eigensolver has three parts: a family of trial states, an estimator of the energy, and a classical search over parameters. Why should the lowest energy found say anything about the ground energy \\(\\lambda_{\\min}\\), the smallest eigenvalue of \\(H\\)? Expand a trial state in eigenvectors of \\(H\\), \\(\\ket\\psi=\\sum_kc_k\\ket{e_k}\\). Then \\[\\langle H\\rangle=\\sum_k|c_k|^2\\lambda_k\\ \\ge\\ \\lambda_{\\min}\\sum_k|c_k|^2=\\lambda_{\\min}.\\] So every exact trial energy is an upper bound on the ground energy. This is the variational bound . It does not promise that the family contains the ground state, or that the search finds its best member. In our example the ground state of \\(-ZZ-XX\\) is the Bell pair, with energy \\(-2\\), and our family contains it at \\(\\theta=\\pi/2\\). An estimated energy can still fall below \\(\\lambda_{\\min}\\) through noise or bias. That breaks no law of quantum mechanics, but it does mean the estimate is wrong. 14.3.2 QAOA and performance comparisons The quantum approximate optimization algorithm (QAOA), from the algorithms course, keeps the same loop and changes the trial family. Its states alternate evolutions under a cost observable, which encodes an optimization problem, and under a mixing observable. What would show that QAOA, or any variational method, is useful? A comparison with a good classical heuristic on the same instances, counting the evaluations spent on training as well as the final answer. Our companion shows something much smaller: that the loop’s interface is correct. It compares every one of the 101 grid energies with \\(-1-\\sin\\theta\\), and then finds the minimum. np.argmin returns the position of the smallest entry, and int makes it an ordinary integer. The grid spacing is \\(\\pi/100\\), so entry 50 is exactly \\(\\theta=\\pi/2\\), and the minimum is \\(-2\\). A solved two-qubit problem demonstrates correctness, not advantage. \"\"\"Runnable companion for Quantum Programming, Chapter 14.\"\"\"\nimport numpy as np\nfrom qiskit import QuantumCircuit\nfrom qiskit.circuit import Parameter\nfrom qiskit.primitives import StatevectorEstimator\nfrom qiskit.quantum_info import SparsePauliOp\n\ntheta = Parameter(\"theta\")\nqc = QuantumCircuit(2)\nqc.ry(theta, 0); qc.cx(0, 1)\nH = SparsePauliOp.from_list([(\"ZZ\", -1), (\"XX\", -1)])\nangles = np.linspace(0, np.pi, 101)\nenergies = StatevectorEstimator().run([(qc, H, angles[:, None])]).result()[0].data.evs\nassert np.allclose(energies, -1 - np.sin(angles))\nbest = int(np.argmin(energies))\nassert np.isclose(energies[best], -2)\nprint(\"Grid minimum:\", angles[best], energies[best]) Companion script: chapter_14.py . Problem 14.3. Why keep a grid baseline? Worked solution It provides a transparent reference on the small problem and exposes errors in sophisticated optimization machinery. Its cost grows rapidly with parameter dimension, so it is not a general scalable strategy. Our observables were sums of two Pauli strings. Advanced algorithms instead transform operators: they simulate time evolution, embed non-unitary matrices in unitaries, and apply polynomials to them. How do we implement such transformations with explicit resource costs?"}, {"title": "Advanced Algorithm Construction — Exercise 1", "chapter": "Advanced Algorithm Construction", "url": "15-advanced-algorithm-construction.html#exercise-1", "text": "To simulate \\(e^{-i(X+Z)t}\\) for \\(t=2\\) with \\(r=10\\) first-order steps, what angle should each rz and rx receive? Why is it not \\(t/r\\)? Each step needs \\(e^{-iZt/r}\\), and \\(R_z(\\theta)=e^{-i\\theta Z/2}\\), so \\(\\theta=2t/r=0.4\\); likewise for rx. Using \\(t/r\\) would simulate half the intended time. The factor of two comes from the convention of Qiskit’s rotation gates and must be checked against the formula, as the chapter does."}, {"title": "Advanced Algorithm Construction — Exercise 2", "chapter": "Advanced Algorithm Construction", "url": "15-advanced-algorithm-construction.html#exercise-2", "text": "For the LCU encoding of \\(A=(I+X)/2\\) (ancilla q1, system q0), compute the probability that the ancilla reads \\(0\\) for system inputs \\(\\ket0\\), \\(\\ket+\\), and \\(\\ket-\\). Explain the three values. \\(\\ket0\\): \\(\\|(\\ket0+\\ket1)/2\\|^2=\\frac12\\). \\(\\ket+\\): \\(A\\ket+=\\ket+\\), probability \\(1\\). \\(\\ket-\\): \\(A\\ket-=0\\), probability \\(0\\): the two terms cancel completely. The success probability depends on the input, which is why a block encoding’s cost must be stated for the inputs it will see."}, {"title": "Advanced Algorithm Construction — Exercise 3", "chapter": "Advanced Algorithm Construction", "url": "15-advanced-algorithm-construction.html#exercise-3", "text": "Rebuild the LCU circuit with the ancilla on qubit 0 and the system on qubit 1. Which rows and columns of the operator now form the selected block? With the ancilla as qubit 0 (the low bit), ancilla \\(=0\\) corresponds to indices \\(0\\) and \\(2\\), so the block is the submatrix with rows and columns \\([0,2]\\), not the top-left \\(2\\times2\\) corner. Extracting the wrong slice would “verify” a different matrix. The ancilla’s position must be part of the encoding’s documented interface."}, {"title": "Advanced Algorithm Construction — Exercise 4", "chapter": "Advanced Algorithm Construction", "url": "15-advanced-algorithm-construction.html#exercise-4", "text": "Compare the first-order product formula with the symmetric second-order formula, rx(\\(1/r\\)), rz(\\(2/r\\)), rx(\\(1/r\\)) per step, for \\(H=X+Z\\), \\(t=1\\), and \\(r=10,20,40\\). Compute both errors against the exact unitary and describe how each scales with \\(r\\). First order: about \\(0.070\\), \\(0.035\\), \\(0.0175\\), halving with each doubling of \\(r\\) (error \\(\\propto1/r\\)). Second order: about \\(2.7\\times10^{-3}\\), \\(6.8\\times10^{-4}\\), \\(1.7\\times10^{-4}\\), falling fourfold (error \\(\\propto1/r^2\\)). The symmetric formula costs only slightly more per step, since adjacent half-steps merge, yet is far more accurate."}, {"title": "Advanced Algorithm Construction — Exercise 5", "chapter": "Advanced Algorithm Construction", "url": "15-advanced-algorithm-construction.html#exercise-5", "text": "On hardware, each rotation has error about \\(10^{-3}\\), so a first-order simulation with \\(r\\) steps (two rotations each) has total error roughly \\(0.7/r+2\\times10^{-3}r\\). (a) Find the \\(r\\) that minimizes it and the minimum. (b) Compare with \\(r=100\\). (c) How does the second-order formula change the picture? (a) \\(r^*=\\sqrt{0.7/0.002}\\approx19\\), giving \\(0.7/19+0.038\\approx0.075\\). (b) At \\(r=100\\): \\(0.007+0.2=0.207\\), almost three times worse. (c) With a second-order error near \\(0.27/r^2\\), the optimum moves to far fewer steps (about \\(r\\approx6\\) to \\(7\\), total near \\(0.03\\)), because each step is more accurate. On noisy hardware the best formula and step count balance algorithmic and gate errors."}, {"title": "Advanced Algorithm Construction — Exercise 6", "chapter": "Advanced Algorithm Construction", "url": "15-advanced-algorithm-construction.html#exercise-6", "text": "Build an LCU encoding of \\((Z+X)/2=H/\\sqrt2\\): H on the ancilla (qubit 1), apply Z to the system when the ancilla is \\(0\\) and X when it is \\(1\\), then H on the ancilla. (a) Verify the top-left block. (b) Show that the success probability is \\(\\frac12\\) for every input. (c) Why does this make the encoding a candidate for oblivious amplification? (a) The top-left \\(2\\times2\\) block equals \\(H/\\sqrt2\\). (b) Since \\(H\\) is unitary, \\(\\|H\\ket\\psi\\|^2/2=\\frac12\\) for every input. (c) An encoded matrix proportional to a unitary, with input-independent success probability, is exactly the situation in which oblivious amplitude amplification can boost success to certainty without knowing the input, as Quantum Algorithms Chapter 7 develops."}, {"title": "Advanced Algorithm Construction — Exercise 7", "chapter": "Advanced Algorithm Construction", "url": "15-advanced-algorithm-construction.html#exercise-7", "text": "For the scalar signal \\(W(x)=xI+i\\sqrt{1-x^2}X\\), verify numerically that (a) the top-left entry of \\(W^3\\) is \\(T_3(x)=4x^3-3x\\), and (b) inserting the phase \\(\\mathrm{diag}(e^{i\\phi},e^{-i\\phi})\\) between two calls gives \\(\\cos\\phi(2x^2-1)+i\\sin\\phi\\). Use \\(x=0.3\\), \\(x=-0.7\\), and \\(\\phi=0.4\\). Both identities hold at the sample points: the three-call response is \\(4x^3-3x\\), and the phased two-call response is \\(\\cos\\phi(2x^2-1)+i\\sin\\phi\\). Point checks catch convention errors (sign of \\(i\\), order of phases, which entry is selected) before a phase list from a synthesis tool is trusted. They do not prove the bound on a whole interval, which must come from the construction."}, {"title": "Advanced Algorithm Construction — Exercise 8", "chapter": "Advanced Algorithm Construction", "url": "15-advanced-algorithm-construction.html#exercise-8", "text": "A student applies the LCU circuit for \\(A=(I+X)/2\\) twice in a row, on the same ancilla and without measuring in between, expecting the selected block to be \\(A^2\\). Compute the actual block and explain the discrepancy. The circuit \\(U\\) is its own inverse, so \\(U^2=I\\) and the selected block is the identity, not \\(A^2=(I+X)/2\\). Between the two applications, the amplitude that left the ancilla-\\(0\\) branch returns and interferes. Multiplying encoded blocks requires fresh ancillas (or measurement and postselection between uses), and polynomial constructions insert reflections between calls; repetition alone does not compose blocks."}, {"title": "Advanced Algorithm Construction — Exercise 9", "chapter": "Advanced Algorithm Construction", "url": "15-advanced-algorithm-construction.html#exercise-9", "text": "A block encoding of \\(A=\\mathrm{diag}(1,0.1)\\) with \\(\\alpha=1\\) is applied to the input \\(\\ket1\\). (a) Find the success probability. (b) Estimate the expected number of repetitions with repeat-until-success, and the number of encoding calls with amplitude amplification. (c) What does this imply about reporting the cost of an encoded operation? (a) \\(0.1^2=0.01\\). (b) Repeat-until-success needs about \\(100\\) attempts on average; amplitude amplification about \\(\\frac\\pi4\\cdot10\\approx8\\) rounds, each using the encoding and its inverse, so roughly \\(16\\) calls. (c) The cost depends on the input through \\(\\|A\\psi\\|\\), and on \\(\\alpha\\). A single “one call” figure for the encoding hides a factor that can reach \\(\\alpha^2/\\|A\\psi\\|^2\\), so reports must state the success probability for the relevant inputs and how it is handled."}, {"title": "Advanced Algorithm Construction — Exercise 10", "chapter": "Advanced Algorithm Construction", "url": "15-advanced-algorithm-construction.html#exercise-10", "text": "A polynomial transformation is promised to act on signals \\(x\\in[-1,1]\\), which holds when the subnormalization satisfies \\(\\alpha\\ge\\|A\\|\\). Suppose \\(\\alpha\\) is underestimated, so that some eigenvalue gives \\(x=1.1\\). (a) Can \\(W(x)=xI+i\\sqrt{1-x^2}X\\) be formed as a unitary? (b) Evaluate \\(T_3(1.1)\\). (c) What does this say about validating \\(\\alpha\\)? (a) No: \\(\\sqrt{1-1.21}\\) is imaginary, the matrix is not unitary, and no circuit implements it; a block of a unitary cannot have norm above \\(1\\). (b) \\(T_3(1.1)=5.324-3.3\\approx2.02\\), outside \\([-1,1]\\), so even the target response would be impossible. (c) The promise \\(\\alpha\\ge\\|A\\|\\) must be verified (from a bound on the norm, or from the construction of the encoding), not assumed. Polynomials that are bounded on \\([-1,1]\\) grow rapidly outside it, so a small underestimate can make an algorithm silently wrong."}, {"title": "Hamiltonian Simulation", "chapter": "Advanced Algorithm Construction", "url": "15-advanced-algorithm-construction.html#section-15-1", "text": "15.1 Hamiltonian Simulation Suppose we must implement \\(e^{-iHt}\\) for \\(H=X+Z\\) and \\(t=1\\). We have rotations about X and about Z, but none about the axis of \\(X+Z\\). Can we build the evolution from the pieces we have, and how accurately? We write the circuit first, then bound its error. 15.1.1 Product-formula circuits The natural attempt is to apply the two terms one after the other: \\(e^{-iX}e^{-iZ}\\). That would be exact if \\(X\\) and \\(Z\\) commuted, but they do not. Splitting the time into \\(r\\) short steps helps, because over a short step the terms almost commute. So we approximate \\[e^{-i(X+Z)}\\approx\\left(e^{-iX/r}\\,e^{-iZ/r}\\right)^r.\\] An approximation of this kind is a product formula . Which gates give each factor? Qiskit defines \\(R_z(\\theta)=e^{-i\\theta Z/2}\\), with a factor one half in the exponent. So \\(e^{-iZ/r}\\) is \\(R_z(2/r)\\), and likewise \\(e^{-iX/r}\\) is \\(R_x(2/r)\\). Each step appends rz and then rx . Since the gate applied last stands on the left, a step has the matrix \\(R_x(2/r)R_z(2/r)\\), which matches the formula. The companion compares the result with the exact \\(e^{-i(X+Z)}\\), computed by SciPy’s matrix exponential expm . That classical calculation is cheap only for tiny matrices, which is why it serves only as a reference for this small check. Problem 15.1. Why is the rotation angle 2/r rather than 1/r? Worked solution Qiskit’s Pauli rotation convention has a factor one half in the exponential. Using 1/r would simulate half the intended term strength. 15.1.2 Time and approximation error How large must \\(r\\) be? To answer, we need a bound on the error, and we use a quoted result. For Hermitian \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), the first-order product formula satisfies \\[\\left\\|e^{-i(A+B)t}-\\left(e^{-iAt/r}e^{-iBt/r}\\right)^r\\right\\|\\le\\frac{t^2}{2r}\\,\\bigl\\|[A,B]\\bigr\\|,\\] where \\([A,B]=AB-BA\\) is the commutator (see Childs and coauthors, “Theory of Trotter error”). The norm here is the operator norm , the largest factor by which a matrix can stretch a unit vector. A bound in this norm therefore holds for every input state. For our terms, \\([X,Z]=XZ-ZX=-2iY\\), and \\(\\|Y\\|=1\\), so \\(\\|[X,Z]\\|=2\\). With \\(t=1\\) the bound is \\(1/r\\). In NumPy, np.linalg.norm(M, 2) computes the operator norm, and the companion asserts that the error is at most \\(1/r\\) for \\(r=10,20,40\\). The measured errors were about \\(0.070\\), \\(0.035\\) and \\(0.017\\), so they halve as \\(r\\) doubles, as a \\(1/r\\) bound suggests. This error belongs to the method, and we report it separately from sampling and hardware noise. Increasing \\(r\\) shrinks it but adds gates, and each gate adds noise on a device. So on hardware the best \\(r\\) can be a finite number, not the largest we can afford."}, {"title": "Operator Embeddings", "chapter": "Advanced Algorithm Construction", "url": "15-advanced-algorithm-construction.html#section-15-2", "text": "15.2 Operator Embeddings Many algorithms need to apply a matrix that is not unitary, such as \\(A=(I+X)/2\\). A circuit can only apply unitaries. How can a unitary circuit act like \\(A\\)? The answer uses an ancilla and a selected measurement outcome, and it has a price, a success probability. We first set up the embedding and its normalization, then build one from a combination of unitaries. 15.2.1 Ancillas and normalization Suppose we find a unitary \\(U\\) on an ancilla and the system whose block for ancilla \\(\\ket0\\) in and \\(\\ket0\\) out is \\(A/\\alpha\\): \\[U=\\begin{pmatrix}A/\\alpha&\\ast\\\\\\ast&\\ast\\end{pmatrix}.\\] Then \\(U\\ket0\\ket\\psi=\\ket0\\,(A\\ket\\psi/\\alpha)+\\ket1\\,(\\cdots)\\). If we measure the ancilla and get 0, the system holds \\(A\\ket\\psi\\), normalized. This is a block encoding of \\(A\\), and \\(\\alpha\\ge\\|A\\|\\) is its scale factor, needed because a block of a unitary cannot stretch any vector. What does it cost? The outcome 0 occurs with probability \\(\\|A\\ket\\psi\\|^2/\\alpha^2\\), which depends on the input. So the interface of a block encoding includes its scale \\(\\alpha\\) and its number of ancillas. We also check two different things. A check of matrix entries verifies the encoding. A probability check on a particular state tells us how often the selection will succeed. 15.2.2 Linear combinations of unitaries How do we build a block encoding of \\((I+X)/2\\)? It is an average of two unitaries, \\(I\\) and \\(X\\). Could we pick one of them at random? That produces a mixture of \\(\\ket\\psi\\) and \\(X\\ket\\psi\\), not the vector \\((\\ket\\psi+X\\ket\\psi)/2\\). We need the two to interfere. So we let an ancilla choose coherently. H on the ancilla gives \\((\\ket0\\ket\\psi+\\ket1\\ket\\psi)/\\sqrt2\\). A CNOT from the ancilla applies X in the \\(\\ket1\\) branch, giving \\((\\ket0\\ket\\psi+\\ket1X\\ket\\psi)/\\sqrt2\\). A second H on the ancilla gives \\[\\ket0\\,\\frac{\\ket\\psi+X\\ket\\psi}2+\\ket1\\,\\frac{\\ket\\psi-X\\ket\\psi}2.\\] The ancilla-0 block is \\((I+X)/2\\), with \\(\\alpha=1\\). This construction is a linear combination of unitaries , or LCU. Where is that block in the matrix? In the companion the ancilla is q1, the high bit, so indices 0 and 1 are the ones with ancilla 0. The block is therefore the top-left \\(2\\times2\\) slice, [:2, :2] . With the ancilla on q0 instead, the correct slice would be different. Problem 15.2. What is the success probability of the LCU example on system zero? Worked solution The selected vector is (|0〉+|1〉)/2, with squared norm one half. The normalized conditional state is plus. An encoding of (I+X)/2. Select ancilla outcome zero to obtain the desired sum on the system; success depends on the input state."}, {"title": "Polynomial Transformations", "chapter": "Advanced Algorithm Construction", "url": "15-advanced-algorithm-construction.html#section-15-3", "text": "15.3 Polynomial Transformations With a block encoding of \\(A\\), can we apply \\(A^2\\), or another polynomial of \\(A\\), by calling the encoding several times? The obvious attempt fails, and the method that works, quantum signal processing, comes with conventions that a program must fix. We look at the phase sequences first, then at how to verify them and what they cost. 15.3.1 Phase sequences The simplest case has a one-qubit signal unitary whose selected block is a number \\(x\\) between \\(-1\\) and \\(1\\): \\[W(x)=\\begin{pmatrix}x&i\\sqrt{1-x^2}\\\\i\\sqrt{1-x^2}&x\\end{pmatrix}=xI+i\\sqrt{1-x^2}\\,X.\\] Does calling \\(W\\) twice give the block \\(x^2\\)? The top-left entry of \\(W^2\\) is \\(x\\cdot x+(i\\sqrt{1-x^2})^2=2x^2-1\\). Amplitude that left the selected block in the first call returned to it in the second, adding \\(-(1-x^2)\\). So repeating a block encoding does not, in general, square its block. Can we control what comes back? Yes, by placing phase rotations \\(e^{i\\phi_kZ}\\) between the calls. This is quantum signal processing , from the algorithms course. We quote its main result: with \\(d\\) calls, a suitable list of phases makes the selected entry any polynomial \\(P(x)\\) of degree at most \\(d\\), with the same parity as \\(d\\), and satisfies \\(|P(x)|\\le1\\) on \\([-1,1]\\), together with a further condition on a companion polynomial. Polynomials meeting those conditions are called admissible . With all phases zero and two calls, we get \\(2x^2-1\\), the Chebyshev polynomial \\(T_2\\). Different sources write the signal and the phases with different signs, orders and selected blocks. A phase list computed under one convention gives a different polynomial under another. So before using a phase list from a paper or a package, we fix the sign convention, the order of operations, the selected block and the target polynomial. 15.3.2 Verification and compilation costs How do we check a phase sequence? First, evaluate the polynomial it produces at sample points and at the edges of the promised interval. Then test the matrix action on small encoded examples. The companion does the first kind of check for \\(W\\): at 21 points from \\(-1\\) to \\(1\\) it confirms that \\(W\\) is unitary and that the top-left entry of \\(W^2\\) is \\(2x^2-1\\). It uses max(0, 1-x*x) so that rounding cannot make the square root’s argument slightly negative at \\(x=\\pm1\\). Checking points is not a proof. A promise that the polynomial approximates some function to within \\(\\epsilon\\) everywhere on the interval must come from the construction itself. What does the transformation cost? The degree counts calls to the encoding, but each call may be a large circuit. The scale \\(\\alpha\\) lowers the success probability, which may require amplification. The phases must be synthesized, and everything must be compiled. A resource report charges each of these. The companion verifies the two building blocks the larger constructions rest on, the product formula and the LCU encoding. \"\"\"Runnable companion for Quantum Programming, Chapter 15.\"\"\"\nimport numpy as np\nfrom qiskit import QuantumCircuit\nfrom qiskit.quantum_info import Operator\nfrom scipy.linalg import expm\n\nX = np.array([[0, 1], [1, 0]])\nZ = np.diag([1, -1])\nerrors = []\nfor steps in (10, 20, 40):\n    qc = QuantumCircuit(1)\n    for _ in range(steps):\n        qc.rz(2/steps, 0)\n        qc.rx(2/steps, 0)\n    error = np.linalg.norm(Operator(qc).data - expm(-1j*(X+Z)), 2)\n    assert error <= 1/steps + 1e-12\n    errors.append(error)\n# An LCU encoding of (I+X)/2: ancilla q1, system q0.\nlcu = QuantumCircuit(2)\nlcu.h(1); lcu.cx(1, 0); lcu.h(1)\nassert np.allclose(Operator(lcu).data[:2, :2], (np.eye(2)+X)/2)\nprint(\"Product-formula errors:\", errors)\n\n# Verify the low-degree signal response independently of a synthesis package.\nfor x in np.linspace(-1, 1, 21):\n    W = x*np.eye(2) + 1j*np.sqrt(max(0, 1-x*x))*X\n    assert np.allclose(W.conj().T @ W, np.eye(2))\n    assert np.isclose((W @ W)[0, 0], 2*x*x-1) Companion script: chapter_15.py . Problem 15.3. Why cannot a degree count alone specify total cost? Worked solution Each signal call may contain a large encoding circuit, and success amplification or gate synthesis adds work. The access implementation matters. Each chapter has ended with a program that passed its checks once, on one machine. What would it take for someone else to obtain the same result independently, from our code and our records alone? Sources and further study: Childs, Su, Tran, Wiebe and Zhu, Theory of Trotter error ."}, {"title": "Reproducible Quantum Projects — Exercise 1", "chapter": "Reproducible Quantum Projects", "url": "16-reproducible-quantum-projects.html#exercise-1", "text": "For the capstone’s Bernstein–Vazirani construction, which input qubits receive CNOTs to the target for the secret 0110? What should the input register read? Reading 0110 from the right, bits \\(1\\) and \\(2\\) are \\(1\\), so qubits 1 and 2 control CNOTs onto the target. The input register then reads 0110 with certainty (in Qiskit’s order, qubit 0 rightmost), and the full measured string has the target bit on the left."}, {"title": "Reproducible Quantum Projects — Exercise 2", "chapter": "Reproducible Quantum Projects", "url": "16-reproducible-quantum-projects.html#exercise-2", "text": "For an \\(n\\)-bit secret with \\(k\\) ones, count the gates of the capstone circuit (before measurement). Check the counts and the depth for the all-ones secret with \\(n=10\\). The circuit has \\(1\\) X, \\(2n+1\\) Hadamards, and \\(k\\) CNOTs. For \\(n=10\\), \\(k=10\\): \\(21\\) H, \\(10\\) CNOTs, depth \\(13\\), since the CNOTs all target the same qubit and run one after another. The query count is one, but the oracle’s gate count and depth grow with the secret, which a resource report must include."}, {"title": "Reproducible Quantum Projects — Exercise 3", "chapter": "Reproducible Quantum Projects", "url": "16-reproducible-quantum-projects.html#exercise-3", "text": "A project records the Qiskit version but not the versions of Aer, NumPy, or the Python interpreter. Which results could fail to reproduce, and what file in this course records the complete environment? Noisy-simulation results depend on the Aer version; numerical tolerances and random streams can change with NumPy and Python; even ideal sampling with a fixed seed can differ between versions of the primitives. requirements.txt pins the direct dependencies, and requirements-lock.txt records the full resolved environment, while TESTING.md notes the platform. A record should cite all of them."}, {"title": "Reproducible Quantum Projects — Exercise 4", "chapter": "Reproducible Quantum Projects", "url": "16-reproducible-quantum-projects.html#exercise-4", "text": "Turn the capstone into a tested function. (a) Write bernstein_vazirani(secret) and a check that samples the circuit and verifies the input register for every shot. (b) Apply it to 0000, 1111, and three random six-bit secrets from a seeded generator. (c) Why include the all-zero and all-one secrets? The function passes for all five secrets. The all-zero secret has no CNOTs at all, so it checks that the Hadamard layers alone return \\(\\ket{0^n}\\) and that an empty oracle does not break the construction; the all-one secret exercises every CNOT and the longest sequential chain on the target. Random secrets cover typical cases. Edge cases and random cases together make a stronger test than the single secret in the companion."}, {"title": "Reproducible Quantum Projects — Exercise 5", "chapter": "Reproducible Quantum Projects", "url": "16-reproducible-quantum-projects.html#exercise-5", "text": "Verify the oracle’s phase contract directly: after the oracle acts on the uniform input with the target in \\(\\ket-\\), the input amplitudes should be \\((-1)^{s\\cdot x}/2^{n/2}\\). Implement the check for the secret 1011 by returning the target to \\(\\ket0\\) and comparing the statevector with the predicted sign pattern. Undoing the target preparation leaves the state \\(\\sum_x(-1)^{s\\cdot x}\\ket x/4\\otimes\\ket0\\). Comparing the first \\(16\\) amplitudes (target \\(0\\)) with \\((-1)^{\\mathrm{popcount}(s\\,\\&\\,x)}/4\\) confirms the phase oracle exactly. This tests the oracle’s promised behavior independently of the final Hadamards, so a failure would point to the oracle rather than to the readout."}, {"title": "Reproducible Quantum Projects — Exercise 6", "chapter": "Reproducible Quantum Projects", "url": "16-reproducible-quantum-projects.html#exercise-6", "text": "Build an experiment record for the capstone that a reader can verify. Include the problem, secret, shots, seed, and package versions for qiskit, qiskit-aer, and numpy, and add a SHA-256 fingerprint of the QPY-serialized coherent circuit. Why is the fingerprint useful? The record below stores the descriptive fields, the versions, and a hash of the serialized circuit. The fingerprint identifies exactly which circuit produced the results: if someone later regenerates the circuit and the hash differs, something in the construction or the software changed. Storing the QPY file itself lets them load and rerun that exact circuit. (QPY files can depend on the Qiskit version, so the version belongs next to the hash.)"}, {"title": "Reproducible Quantum Projects — Exercise 7", "chapter": "Reproducible Quantum Projects", "url": "16-reproducible-quantum-projects.html#exercise-7", "text": "Run the capstone (secret 1011) on a noisy simulator in which every CNOT suffers two-qubit depolarizing error \\(0.02\\). (a) Estimate the fraction of shots whose input register equals the secret. (b) Why is it below \\(1\\), and how would it change for a longer secret with more ones? (c) How should the result be labelled? (a) About \\(0.96\\) with \\(8000\\) shots. (b) Each of the three CNOTs fails with probability about \\(0.02\\), and many failures corrupt the readout, so success is roughly \\(1-3\\times0.02\\times(\\text{fraction harmful})\\). A secret with \\(k\\) ones has \\(k\\) noisy CNOTs, so success falls roughly linearly in \\(k\\) for small errors. (c) As a noisy simulation with a stated, hypothetical noise model, distinct from both ideal simulation and hardware, with the model parameters, seed, and shots recorded."}, {"title": "Reproducible Quantum Projects — Exercise 8", "chapter": "Reproducible Quantum Projects", "url": "16-reproducible-quantum-projects.html#exercise-8", "text": "A student argues: “The capstone finds a \\(50\\)-bit secret with one query, while a classical algorithm needs \\(50\\), so this project demonstrates a quantum speedup.” List what the project actually demonstrates and what a speedup claim would require. The project demonstrates a correct, reproducible implementation of the algorithm in the query model, where the oracle is a supplied interface. Here the oracle was constructed from the known secret, with one CNOT per one-bit, so reading the circuit reveals the secret and building it costs as many gates as the classical queries it replaces. A speedup claim would need a setting where the oracle is genuinely given as a black box, matched classical and quantum access, end-to-end costs including oracle construction and noise, and measured performance as \\(n\\) grows, not a single success."}, {"title": "Reproducible Quantum Projects — Exercise 9", "chapter": "Reproducible Quantum Projects", "url": "16-reproducible-quantum-projects.html#exercise-9", "text": "Extend the capstone study to \\(n=50\\). (a) Why is a statevector simulation impossible, and why can Aer’s stabilizer method still run the circuit? (b) Run it for the secret “10” repeated \\(25\\) times and check the result. (c) What does this say about choosing a simulation method in a scaling study? (a) \\(51\\) qubits need \\(2^{51}\\approx2\\times10^{15}\\) amplitudes, about \\(36\\) petabytes. The circuit uses only Clifford gates (X, H, CNOT), which the stabilizer method simulates with polynomial resources. (b) Every shot returns the secret in the input register. (c) The simulator must match the circuit class: stabilizer methods scale for Clifford circuits, tensor networks for low-entanglement ones, statevectors for small general circuits. A scaling study should state which was used, since simulation cost is a property of the method, not evidence about quantum hardware."}, {"title": "Reproducible Quantum Projects — Exercise 10", "chapter": "Reproducible Quantum Projects", "url": "16-reproducible-quantum-projects.html#exercise-10", "text": "Design the directory layout and test plan for turning the capstone into a reusable project. Name the modules, what each test checks, and how hardware submission is kept from happening by accident. A reasonable layout: a package module with the circuit factory (bernstein_vazirani) and the oracle; an experiment driver script that builds, compiles, runs, and writes a JSON record and QPY file; a tests directory with exact tests (phase contract, statevector output for small \\(n\\), edge-case secrets), seeded sampling tests, a noisy-simulation smoke test, and a serialization round-trip test; and requirements and lock files with a README describing the environment. The driver submits to hardware only with an explicit option and backend name, and importing any module never submits. Continuous testing reruns the suite whenever dependencies change."}, {"title": "Project Structure", "chapter": "Reproducible Quantum Projects", "url": "16-reproducible-quantum-projects.html#section-16-1", "text": "16.1 Project Structure A project is reproducible when another reader can reconstruct its task, its circuit, its assumptions and its evidence. A plot without the program that made it, and the record of how it ran, cannot be reconstructed. So what must the code itself look like? We decide how to divide it into files first, then how to pin and track the software it depends on. 16.1.1 Modules, notebooks, and tests Suppose all of the capstone lived in one notebook: building the oracle, running it, and checking it. Could a reader reuse the oracle in a test without rerunning the experiment? Not easily. So we split the code by purpose. Circuit factories, such as one that builds the oracle for a given \\(s\\), go in a module , a Python file that other files import. An experiment driver, a script, runs the experiment from a fresh process. Tests import the same factories and check them. A notebook may still explain the experiment, but the script is what we trust. What should the tests contain? The checks that caught real errors earlier in the course: a small case solvable by hand, an asymmetric input that exposes bit order, and a superposition input that exposes phases. And, as with the hardware program, importing a module must never submit a job. 16.1.2 Dependencies and version control Our coin program showed that the same text can behave differently with different packages underneath it. So we pin the tested versions and record the Python version. The requirements file captures the packages the project uses directly. A full lock file and a note on the platform make an exact reconstruction possible. Updating any package means rerunning the examples, not assuming that nothing changed. The code itself changes too. How does a result point to the code that produced it? A version-control system, such as Git, records every change to the source with an identifier, so a result can name the exact version it came from. Credentials stay out of it, as they stay out of every source file."}, {"title": "Experiment Records", "chapter": "Reproducible Quantum Projects", "url": "16-reproducible-quantum-projects.html#section-16-2", "text": "16.2 Experiment Records Well-organized code still runs differently with different settings. A reader rerunning the capstone needs to know exactly what ran, and with what result. What should we save? We consider the circuits and settings first, then the results and what they cost. 16.2.1 Saved circuits and configurations Is a drawing of the circuit enough to rerun it? No. A drawing can omit the global phase, the values bound to parameters, and other details of the object. So we save the circuit itself, in a file format that a program can load. Qiskit’s format for this is QPY , a binary file that preserves the circuit’s full structure, for loading by compatible Qiskit versions. The companion checks a save and load in memory, without writing a file. io.BytesIO() creates an in-memory file, qpy.dump writes the circuit to it, and buffer.seek(0) moves back to its start. qpy.load then reads back a list of circuits, of which we take the first. The test requires that the restored circuit has an equivalent operator. When compilation is involved, we save the logical circuit, the compiled circuit, its layout, the parameter values and the seeds, because each of them changed results in earlier chapters. 16.2.2 Results, metadata, and execution costs What would a reader need in order to rerun the capstone and check our claim? Each item answers a failure we have already met. The problem and its input size say what was attempted. The access assumption says what the program may do with the oracle, since the claim is about queries. The success criterion says what counts as a correct answer. The shots and the seed determine the sampled counts. The number of qubits and the logical and compiled gate counts give the cost, since compilation can multiply it. The package versions explain differences between machines. The kind of execution matters too: exact local simulation, noisy simulation and hardware make different claims, so each result is labelled with its kind. Finally, the measured outputs belong in the record, with their confidence intervals and error budgets stated beside the conclusions rather than left in raw logs. The companion assembles such a record as a Python dictionary and prints it with json.dumps in JSON, a plain-text format that other programs can read."}, {"title": "Capstone Project", "chapter": "Reproducible Quantum Projects", "url": "16-reproducible-quantum-projects.html#section-16-3", "text": "16.3 Capstone Project We now apply all of this to the capstone. The Bernstein–Vazirani problem gives us an oracle for \\(f(x)=s\\cdot x \\bmod 2\\) and asks for \\(s\\). Classically each query reveals one bit of \\(s\\), while the quantum algorithm needs one query. What exactly does our program implement, and what does a successful run establish? We write the specification and the implementation first, then evaluate them and state their limits. 16.3.1 Specification and implementation How does one query reveal all of \\(s\\)? With the target in \\(\\ket-\\), the oracle is a phase oracle, multiplying \\(\\ket x\\) by \\((-1)^{s\\cdot x}\\). Starting from the uniform superposition, \\[H^{\\otimes n}\\,\\frac1{\\sqrt{2^n}}\\sum_x(-1)^{s\\cdot x}\\ket x=\\ket s,\\] because \\(H^{\\otimes n}\\ket s\\) is exactly that superposition and \\(H^{\\otimes n}\\) is its own inverse. So measuring the input register gives \\(s\\) with certainty. How is the oracle built? Since \\(s\\cdot x=\\bigoplus_{j:\\,s_j=1}x_j\\), it needs one CNOT from input qubit \\(j\\) to the target for each bit \\(s_j=1\\), exactly as we built an XOR term in our first oracle. The secret \"1011\" is written high bit first, like Qiskit’s strings. So the code walks through reversed(secret) , and enumerate pairs each character with its qubit index, starting from qubit 0. Passing range(n+1) to h applies H to all those qubits at once. Is the program discovering a secret? The program knows \\(s\\), because it built the oracle from it. The algorithm’s claim concerns queries: given only access to the oracle, one query suffices. Knowing \\(s\\) is what lets us verify the construction. 16.3.2 Evaluation, limitations, and documentation What should the output look like? The companion uses \\(s=1011\\) and also measures the target. The target, qubit \\(n\\), is the leftmost character of each string. It stays in \\(\\ket-\\), so it reads 0 or 1 at random. With seed 41 the counts were 540 for 11011 and 484 for 01011 . The expression bits[1:] drops the first character, and the check requires that the remaining four bits equal the secret in every observed string. \"\"\"Runnable companion for Quantum Programming, Chapter 16.\"\"\"\nimport io, json, sys\nfrom importlib.metadata import version\nfrom qiskit import QuantumCircuit, qpy\nfrom qiskit.primitives import StatevectorSampler\nfrom qiskit.quantum_info import Operator\n\nsecret = \"1011\"  # Written high bit first.\nn = len(secret)\nqc = QuantumCircuit(n+1)\nqc.x(n); qc.h(range(n+1))\nfor qubit, bit in enumerate(reversed(secret)):\n    if bit == \"1\":\n        qc.cx(qubit, n)\nqc.h(range(n))\n# Save/load the coherent circuit before appending measurements.\nbuffer = io.BytesIO()\nqpy.dump(qc, buffer); buffer.seek(0)\nrestored = qpy.load(buffer)[0]\nassert Operator(qc).equiv(Operator(restored))\nqc.measure_all()\ncounts = StatevectorSampler(seed=41).run([qc], shots=1024).result()[0].data.meas.get_counts()\nassert all(bits[1:] == secret for bits in counts)\nrecord = {\"problem\": \"Bernstein-Vazirani\", \"secret\": secret,\n    \"python\": sys.version.split()[0], \"qiskit\": version(\"qiskit\"),\n    \"seed\": 41, \"shots\": 1024, \"qubits\": qc.num_qubits,\n    \"gate_counts\": dict(qc.count_ops()), \"counts\": counts,\n    \"execution\": \"local ideal StatevectorSampler\", \"oracle_queries_per_shot\": 1}\nprint(json.dumps(record, indent=2)) Companion script: chapter_16.py . Problem 16.1. Why does the target bit vary in the capstone output? Worked solution It remains in minus, which gives a fair computational-basis result. The useful secret is in the other bits, so the decoder excludes the target bit. Problem 16.2. What changes if the secret is all zeros? Worked solution The oracle has no CNOTs and the input Hadamards cancel, so the output secret is all zeros. This is an important edge case. What does the run establish? That this implementation is correct for one secret of length 4, in ideal local simulation. To make it a study, we extend it across lengths, and test the edge cases \\(s=0\\cdots0\\) and \\(s=1\\cdots1\\). We report how the oracle’s gate count grows with \\(n\\), and compare with the classical baseline of \\(n\\) queries under the same oracle contract. The result is a reproducible implementation study. It is not a claim of speedup on hardware. Problem 16.3. What evidence would be needed for a runtime advantage claim? Worked solution A scalable task and access model, matched classical baseline, complete preparation and oracle costs, execution and readout costs, comparable success and precision, and measurements or justified resource analysis over growing sizes. Phase-oracle readout. U_f is the reversible bit oracle on the input register and the minus target, not an elementary gate. The final input measurement reads the encoded pattern. The companion programs of this course form a locally tested starting point for such projects. Each one states its prediction, checks it, and keeps any hardware submission explicit. Sources and further study: IBM, QPY serialization ."}];
